[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Re8 {Black's best move, objectively, but not the most popular. Same as ...Be7, it's played about 12% of the time. We'll activate our queen and bring it out to f5.} 7.Qf5 7...d6 {d6, attacking the queen, is the most popular move. We'll play an intermezzo with Nxf6+.} 8.Nxf6+ 8...Qxf6 {If the queen chooses to take, then they'll simply trade down into a worse endgame after we exchange on f6. Structure is very important, and doubled pawns aren't ideal in many situations.} 9.Qxf6 9...gxf6 {Black recaptures. Let's kick the bishop away from b4. This will accelerate our expansion and development because we're going to get tempi until we reach Bb2.} 10.a3 10...Bc5 {The bishop goes to c5. We'll attack the c5-bishop with b4, and prepare Bb2.} 11.b4 11...Bb6 {The bishop goes back... And we'll fianchetto our bishop. It aims across the diagonal towards the king. Hence, our position is quite strong because Black is seriously discouraged from moving the e5- and f6-pawns, which would only activate our bishop further.} 12.Bb2 12...a5 {Black often challenges our b-pawn. We'll just push the pawn forward. We don't want to trade and split our queenside pawns. Neither do we want to open up their rook.} 13.b5 13...Ne7 {The knight has to retreat. We'll gain space in the center by playing d4!} 14.d4 14...c5 {If Black tries to break with ...c5 now... We'll trade on e5.} 15.dxe5 15...dxe5 {Black recaptures...} 16.g4 {And we have ideas of Nd2-e4 and Rg1 and g5. White is better because of the extra space and the bishop on b2, which is deceptively active. Black can't capture on g4, because we'd win the bishop after Rg1.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Re8 {Black's best move, objectively, but not the most popular. Same as ...Be7, it's played about 12% of the time. We'll activate our queen and bring it out to f5.} 7.Qf5 7...d6 {d6, attacking the queen, is the most popular move. We'll play an intermezzo with Nxf6+.} 8.Nxf6+ 8...gxf6 {This move is possible. It's actually the best recapture if Black plays their cards right. We'll get the queen to safety and also keep it on the kingside by playing Qh5.} 9.Qh5 9...d5 {d5 attacks the center and also intends to bring the bishop back to a defensive role. We'll chase it back with a3.} 10.a3 10...Bf8 {It goes back... We'll exchange pawns in the center.} 11.cxd5 11...Qxd5 {And once Black recaptures...} 12.d3 {We'll cover more squares in the position and we'll get ready to castle kingside. Moves like Be2 and 0-0 are next, and White is better because of the pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Bc5 {This bishop retreat is the most popular, but it's not particularly any good. In the database, it's played 25% of the time. We'll play Ng5, with significant pressure on h7. Black must block the diagonal.} 7.Ng5 7...g6 {Cutting out the attack on h7. We'll exchange on f6, with a knight fork to follow.} 8.Nxf6+ 8...Qxf6 {The queen takes. And we move the knight back, with an attack on queen and bishop.} 9.Ne4 9...Qe7 {The queen defends c5. We'll try to get in b4, expanding on the queenside.} 10.a3 10...a5 {So Black stops that idea. We'll settle for b3, preparing to fianchetto.} 11.b3 11...Ba7 {Black chooses to hold on to the bishop. And we'll fianchetto. Notice that the move g6 weakened the diagonal our bishop now sits on.} 12.Bb2 12...d6 {d6 is logical, to activate the light-squared bishop.} 13.h4 {And we can continue with plans of a kingside attack and castling queenside! Like we'll find in other variations, ...f5 isn't good because our knight may reroute to a better square like d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...d6 {...d6 is a developing move that seems natural. Its popularity is close to ...Nxd5 with 17%, according to the database. We'll prepare queenside expansion with a3.} 7.a3 7...Bc5 {Black retreats... And now we go b4 for free. We don't always want to push our pawns out of the opening, but in this case the center is closed and we can fianchetto without any danger.} 8.b4 8...Bb6 {The bishop falls back. We'll use an idea we've already seen, and play Ng5, targeting h7.} 9.Ng5 9...g6 {Black blocks the diagonal. We'll exchange on f6, and argue that we've weakened the dark squares around the black king.} 10.Nxf6+ 10...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures. Our knight jumps to the middle, attacking the queen and blocking the e-pawn. This prevents any discoveries with a future ...e4.} 11.Ne4 11...Qe7 {And as the queen goes back...} 12.Bb2 {Our bishop sits on the weakened diagonal. We can also go for an attack here with h4 and h5 and 0-0-0. Notice how the knight here is quite tricky. Natural moves like ...f5 to kick it out just send it where it wants to go.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 13"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...d6 {...d6, defending e5, is played 25% of the time in the database. The idea here remains the same: plant the knight in the center and challenge Black's setup!} 6.Nd5 6...Ba5 {...Ba5 is another option for Black. It's only played about 10% of the time, so you might not see it a lot, but it definitely comes in handy to know how to play against it! Be2 is a sound response, preparing to castle.} 7.Be2 7...O-O {Black castles. And now Ng5 is a neat idea! We put pressure on h7 and threaten Nxf6+ followed by checkmate on h7.} 8.Ng5 8...g6 {Black has to block the diagonal. We'll trade on f6.} 9.Nxf6+ 9...Qxf6 {Black recaptures.} 10.h4 {And our ideas include stuff like Ne4, and a3 to get the b4 pawn push. At any moment, if it is useful, Ne4 is a tempo gain that we should hold in reserve. As you can see, the lack of space is something we'll use to our advantage: the knight on g5. cannot be easily evicted with moves like ...h6 since every pawn move made in front of a king is a weakness and the knight wants to go to e4 anyway.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Nxd5 {This trade isn't a blunder at this point since the king is castled. Although it might seem counterintuitive to bring White's pawn to d5, this move is the second most popular, being played 18% of the time! Naturally, we will recapture.} 7.cxd5 7...Ne7 {The knight goes to e7. Now the e5-pawn is unprotected. We'll take it.} 8.Nxe5 8...Nxd5 {And after this capture, we can develop our bishop with a tempo, and set up ideas on f7.} 9.Bc4 9...c6 {Hence, a move like c6 is normal, after which an idea can be: Capturing on d5 to double the pawns!} 10.Bxd5 10...cxd5 {Black recaptures.} 11.Nf3 {And lets bring the knight back, to cover the d4 square. Black won't get rid of their doubled d-pawns easily! Expect to go 0-0 soon, but keep an eye out for Qb3, which would be an attack on the bishop and on the d5-pawn. So if Black tries 11...Qf6,for instance, 12.Qb3 is strong.} *

