1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Nxd5 {This trade isn't a blunder at this point since the king is castled. Although it might seem counterintuitive to bring White's pawn to d5, this move is the second most popular, being played 18% of the time! Naturally, we will recapture.} 7.cxd5 7...Ne7 {The knight goes to e7. Now the e5-pawn is unprotected. We'll take it.} 8.Nxe5 8...Nxd5 {And after this capture, we can develop our bishop with a tempo, and set up ideas on f7.} 9.Bc4 9...Nf6 {For example, if this retreat is innocently played... Capturing on f7 enables us to win material.} 10.Nxf7 10...Rxf7 {The rook recaptures. And we'll take it with check.} 11.Bxf7+ 11...Kxf7 {And once the king takes...} 12.Qc4+ {We've got a fork, winning the bishop and emerging the exchange up.} *

