[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Re8 {Black's best move, objectively, but not the most popular. Same as ...Be7, it's played about 12% of the time. We'll activate our queen and bring it out to f5.} 7.Qf5 7...d6 {d6, attacking the queen, is the most popular move. We'll play an intermezzo with Nxf6+.} 8.Nxf6+ 8...gxf6 {This move is possible. It's actually the best recapture if Black plays their cards right. We'll get the queen to safety and also keep it on the kingside by playing Qh5.} 9.Qh5 9...e4 {Black has to be aggressive! But we can respond with some aggression as well! We offer a knight by playing Rg1, aiming for open lines against the black king.} 10.Rg1 10...d5 {Again, d5 with the idea of Bf8 to defend. We'll encourage the bishop to retreat.} 11.a3 11...Bf8 {It goes back.. And with d4 we claim a central square for our pawn. Black has to capture en passant if they want to challenge us, but that would bring our bishop to d3, hitting h7.} 12.d4 12...Be6 {The bishop defends d5 and f7. And now we've got Nh4, transferring attackers to the kingside.} 13.Nh4 13...Ne7 {The knight goes to e7. This is normal looking: bringing more defenders.} 14.f4 {The problem is we can bring more attackers and create serious problems for Black. Our plan is simply g4 and while we do have some strong g5 ideas, g4 mostly supports the computer suggested Nf5 for Nh6+ followed by f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 8"]
