1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Re8 {Black's best move, objectively, but not the most popular. Same as ...Be7, it's played about 12% of the time. We'll activate our queen and bring it out to f5.} 7.Qf5 7...d6 {d6, attacking the queen, is the most popular move. We'll play an intermezzo with Nxf6+.} 8.Nxf6+ 8...gxf6 {This move is possible. It's actually the best recapture if Black plays their cards right. We'll get the queen to safety and also keep it on the kingside by playing Qh5.} 9.Qh5 9...e4 {Black has to be aggressive! But we can respond with some aggression as well! We offer a knight by playing Rg1, aiming for open lines against the black king.} 10.Rg1 10...exf3 {This is exactly what we want! We'll capture, and suddenly the rook comes to life on the g-file!} 11.gxf3+ 11...Kh8 {The king hides. We'll prepare b4 to put our bishop on the same diagonal as the black king.} 12.a3 12...Bc5 {The bishop goes to c5... We'll threaten checkmate with Qh6.} 13.Qh6 13...Rg8 {The rook guards the mate threat... We'll trade, removing a defender of the king.} 14.Rxg8+ 14...Kxg8 {The king recaptures... We'll threaten a crushing check on h7.} 15.Bd3 15...f5 {The diagonal is blocked... But we'll play b4 to bring our dark-squared bishop into the attack...} 16.b4 16...Ne5 {Black may think they can centralize the knight and go for this intermezzo... But we've got a cool double bishop sacrifice with Bb2!} 17.Bb2 17...Nxd3+ {If it is taken...} 18.Ke2 {We just move up and clear the way for the rook! Black is lost!} *

