1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Re8 {Black's best move, objectively, but not the most popular. Same as ...Be7, it's played about 12% of the time. We'll activate our queen and bring it out to f5.} 7.Qf5 7...Nxd5 {If Black trades, they can get in trouble quickly. We'll recapture... 8...Ne7 would drop a pawn.} 8.cxd5 8...d6 {Just as an example of what can go wrong... The c6 knight is still under attack, and our queen has been pushed to a better square.} 9.Qh5 9...Ne7 {So once the knight moves...} 10.Ng5 {We're attacking two pawns and the black king is in trouble.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...O-O {Castling kingside by Black is the most popular move at this point, so we'll go through it first. This traditional and principled move is played about 45% of the time. I suggest going for the knight jump with Nd5: if Black trades, the c6 -knight will be dislodged.} 6.Nd5 6...Re8 {Black's best move, objectively, but not the most popular. Same as ...Be7, it's played about 12% of the time. We'll activate our queen and bring it out to f5.} 7.Qf5 7...Be7 {The bishop simply goes back. This line is a gambit by Black, because we can take the pawn on e5.} 8.Nxe5 8...Nb4 {And now Black's idea is to take advantage of our lack of development. Let's just trade on b4.} 9.Nxb4 9...Bxb4 {Black recaptures. ...d6, winning a piece, is a threat, so we retreat the knight.} 10.Nf3 10...d5 {Black opens up an attack on the queen!} 11.Qd3 {The queen may look awkward on this square, but we're defending against d4 somewhat. We're going to castle next and boast that we're a pawn up. Be2 and 0-0 and it's up to Black to try to get an initiative.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...d6 {...d6, defending e5, is played 25% of the time in the database. The idea here remains the same: plant the knight in the center and challenge Black's setup!} 6.Nd5 6...Bc5 {...Bc5 is one of the options Black has. It's definitely a solid approach, keeping the bishop safe. Black players choose this move about 20% of the time. To this we respond a3, with a possible idea of b4 if Black allows it.} 7.a3 7...a5 {That's why Black covers the b4 square.} 8.b3 {And now we'll develop the dark-squared bishop, with ideas of b4 or d4 pawn pushes in the future. Definitely expect to see moves like 8...Be6 (in which case 9. Nc3 is good), or 8...0-0. after which 9. Bb2 is great, with a quick d4 to come.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Reverse Sicilian (1. c4 e5) Part 1 / Study 1: The Main Lines (5...0-0 and 5...d6) / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/3a03d0ee-f6d3-41ec-be46-eeb7a5df6bb9/studies/b6c43711-79a1-420a-af3d-df4217faeb22/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {One way they may counter our setup is by bringing their pawn out to e5. This controls the center directly, and borrows from the positional concepts of the closed Sicilian. Just that in this case, of course, the plans are reversed. We'll bring our knight out, controlling the center further.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop our other knight.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {And now, following sound opening principles, both sides have developed their knights. We'll play e3, preparing the d4 pawn push.} 4.e3 4...Bb4 {And now ...Bb4 is a popular way for Black to continue, aiming to undermine our central control. Black players play this move 28% of the time. We'll continue with Qc2, supporting the c3-knight.} 5.Qc2 5...d6 {...d6, defending e5, is played 25% of the time in the database. The idea here remains the same: plant the knight in the center and challenge Black's setup!} 6.Nd5 6...Nxd5 {This move looks natural, and so your opponents could fall for it. Believe it or not, it's the most popular move in this position. Black plays it 41% of the time! And it's a losing blunder. We recapture, attacking the knight on c6.} 7.cxd5 7...Ne7 {When the knight moves back...} 8.Qa4+ {We've got a double attack, winning the bishop!} *

