1.c4 1...e5 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 4.e3 4...Bb4 5.Qc2 5...Bxc3 6.bxc3 6...d6 7.e4 7...Bg4 8.Be2 8...O-O 9.O-O *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...d5 {The most popular alternative to 4...Bb4 is 4...d5! It's nearly 25% of games. But we can have an advantage by taking. The idea is that we have an extra tempo. Usually, in the Sicilian Reversed, it's useful for Black to play with Closed Sicilian motifs. Going for an "open" Sicilian can backfire.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures. And now we'll develop and pin the c6 knight, threatening to double the pawns and ready to castle!} 6.Bb5 6...Qd6 {Black could try holding on to the center with Qd6. They'll try this peculiar move only 2% of the time on average, but it's definitely an interesting line. Black is going for long castling. In which case, we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Bd7 {Black often chooses to break the pin and prepare queenside castling. But we can get the thematic central pawn break here!} 8.d4 8...exd4 {Black trades... We'll recapture, keeping a pawn in the center.} 9.exd4 9...O-O-O {If Black castles...} 10.Ng5 {It's quite unpleasant to deal with this knight move. Just look at White's pieces, much more active than Black's!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...d5 {The most popular alternative to 4...Bb4 is 4...d5! It's nearly 25% of games. But we can have an advantage by taking. The idea is that we have an extra tempo. Usually, in the Sicilian Reversed, it's useful for Black to play with Closed Sicilian motifs. Going for an "open" Sicilian can backfire.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures. And now we'll develop and pin the c6 knight, threatening to double the pawns and ready to castle!} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This trade is by far the most popular move. Black players love to relieve some central tension and capture on c3, allowing themselves to develop easily. This capture is played a whopping 64% of the time! We'll capture towards the center.} 7.bxc3 7...e4 {e4 gains a tempo on the knight. The beauty of Ne5 is that it covers the d7 square, so if Bd7 we can just take.} 8.Ne5 8...Bd7 {Nevertheless, Bd7 is still a necessary move. We can just take.} 9.Nxd7 9...Qxd7 {The queen recaptures. And now we undermine e4.} 10.f3 10...exf3 {So after something like exf3... We'll recapture...} 11.Qxf3 11...O-O-O {Black will castle...} 12.O-O {And we'll have pressure along the f and b-files!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...Bc5 {4...Bc5 develops the bishop and comes third in terms of popularity, being chosen 17% of the time. But we can just gain a tempo on the bishop with d4!} 5.d4 5...exd4 {This capture is by far the most natural and most popular. But we can just recapture and we are left with a strong central pawn duo.} 6.exd4 6...Bb6 {This retreat, played 16% of the time, is a natural move. I suppose Black players under 2000 like the more aggressive route. We're going to cover the b4 square with a3. So a simple threat will be c5 and b4, winning a piece.} 7.a3 7...d6 {That's why Black should cover the c5 square. Now we'll play Qe2+.} 8.Qe2+ 8...Qe7 {Black blocks... We'll guard d4.} 9.Be3 9...O-O {They castle...} 10.O-O-O {And we castle queenside, supporting the d4-pawn. We have a plan of attacking on the kingside with h3 and g4!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...d5 {The most popular alternative to 4...Bb4 is 4...d5! It's nearly 25% of games. But we can have an advantage by taking. The idea is that we have an extra tempo. Usually, in the Sicilian Reversed, it's useful for Black to play with Closed Sicilian motifs. Going for an "open" Sicilian can backfire.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures. And now we'll develop and pin the c6 knight, threatening to double the pawns and ready to castle!} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This trade is by far the most popular move. Black players love to relieve some central tension and capture on c3, allowing themselves to develop easily. This capture is played a whopping 64% of the time! We'll capture towards the center.} 7.bxc3 7...Bd6 {Bd6, developing, is the most popular move. Let's break in the center with d4!} 8.d4 8...e4 {If Black attempts this push... We can retreat and attack e4.} 9.Nd2 9...Qg5 {Black attacks the bishop and the pawn. We'll retreat, defending g2.} 10.Bf1 10...f5 {Black defends e4.} 11.Qb3 {And our queen comes out, preventing castling. Ba3, to trade off Black's powerful dark-squared bishop, is also possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...d5 {The most popular alternative to 4...Bb4 is 4...d5! It's nearly 25% of games. But we can have an advantage by taking. The idea is that we have an extra tempo. Usually, in the Sicilian Reversed, it's useful for Black to play with Closed Sicilian motifs. Going for an "open" Sicilian can backfire.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures. And now we'll develop and pin the c6 knight, threatening to double the pawns and ready to castle!} 6.Bb5 6...f6 {...f6 defends the e5-pawn. It's the second most popular move, but it's only played 10% of the time! That goes to show how popular ...Nxc3 is. The d4 pawn break, once again, is good in these variations.} 7.d4 7...Nxc3 {The knight is traded... And we recapture.} 8.bxc3 8...exd4 {Black trades further... Again, we recapture.} 9.cxd4 9...Bb4+ {Bb4+ is a move some players may try. The idea is to gain a tempo towards castling. We'll block the check.} 10.Bd2 10...Bxd2+ {Black trades. Our queen will recapture.} 11.Qxd2 11...O-O {And Black castles. We'll break up their pawn structure by capturing on c6.} 12.Bxc6 12...bxc6 {Black recaptures... We'll castle, intending Rfc1.} 13.O-O 13...Bf5 {Black develops their bishop.} 14.Rfc1 {We add an attacker to the c6-pawn. White has a better pawn structure and more space on the queenside!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...Bc5 {4...Bc5 develops the bishop and comes third in terms of popularity, being chosen 17% of the time. But we can just gain a tempo on the bishop with d4!} 5.d4 5...exd4 {This capture is by far the most natural and most popular. But we can just recapture and we are left with a strong central pawn duo.} 6.exd4 6...Bb4 {Black moves the bishop and creates a pin. Black will probably play this move more than half of the time, so be ready for it! In the database, it's 57%. We'll go for rapid development with Bd3!} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black doesn't want to be stuck in the center, so they castle. We'll castle as well, and after eight moves, only White has pawns in the center!} 8.O-O 8...d6 {This move, opening up the bishop, is popular. We'll play the knight jump Nd5 and win material by force!} 9.Nd5 9...Nxd5 {If Black captures... We'll recapture...} 10.cxd5 10...Ne7 {The knight goes back... And Qa4 traps the bishop!} 11.Qa4 11...Nxd5 {Nxd5 defends the bishop for one move... But it's still trapped! We attack it again with a3.} 12.a3 12...Bd7 {If Black tries to gain a tempo on the queen to free up the a5 square...} 13.Qb3 {Qb3 gets our queen to safety and attacks both knight and bishop. Black cannot save both.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...d5 {The most popular alternative to 4...Bb4 is 4...d5! It's nearly 25% of games. But we can have an advantage by taking. The idea is that we have an extra tempo. Usually, in the Sicilian Reversed, it's useful for Black to play with Closed Sicilian motifs. Going for an "open" Sicilian can backfire.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures. And now we'll develop and pin the c6 knight, threatening to double the pawns and ready to castle!} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This trade is by far the most popular move. Black players love to relieve some central tension and capture on c3, allowing themselves to develop easily. This capture is played a whopping 64% of the time! We'll capture towards the center.} 7.bxc3 7...Bd6 {Bd6, developing, is the most popular move. Let's break in the center with d4!} 8.d4 8...exd4 {Black usually trades here. We'll recapture towards the center.} 9.cxd4 9...O-O {And Black gets to safety. We can castle as well.} 10.O-O 10...Bg4 {The bishop attacks us, with a pin. So we'll try to break it.} 11.h3 11...Bh5 {Once it goes to h5... We'll defend our knight with Be2.} 12.Be2 12...Qf6 {Black brings their queen out... But we're simply too solid and we have open lines. We activate our rook on the b-file.} 13.Rb1 13...b6 {Once the pawn blocks the attack...} 14.Bb2 {We can just develop the bishop and prepare central pawn pushes to gain space.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...d5 {The most popular alternative to 4...Bb4 is 4...d5! It's nearly 25% of games. But we can have an advantage by taking. The idea is that we have an extra tempo. Usually, in the Sicilian Reversed, it's useful for Black to play with Closed Sicilian motifs. Going for an "open" Sicilian can backfire.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures. And now we'll develop and pin the c6 knight, threatening to double the pawns and ready to castle!} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This trade is by far the most popular move. Black players love to relieve some central tension and capture on c3, allowing themselves to develop easily. This capture is played a whopping 64% of the time! We'll capture towards the center.} 7.bxc3 7...Bd7 {Bd7 defends the knight and indirectly defends e5. If Bxc6, Bxc6 and there will be pressure on g2. We should respond to Bd7 with a central pawn break!} 8.d4 8...exd4 {Taking is the most popular reaction. Let's take towards the center. 9...Bd6 transposes.} 9.cxd4 9...Bb4+ {This check is what's played most frequently. Let's just block.} 10.Bd2 10...Bxd2+ {Black trades... We recapture.} 11.Qxd2 11...O-O {They castle.} 12.Bc4 {And now it's time to castle, bring the rook over to the b and c-files and enjoy our space on the queenside!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...d5 {The most popular alternative to 4...Bb4 is 4...d5! It's nearly 25% of games. But we can have an advantage by taking. The idea is that we have an extra tempo. Usually, in the Sicilian Reversed, it's useful for Black to play with Closed Sicilian motifs. Going for an "open" Sicilian can backfire.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures. And now we'll develop and pin the c6 knight, threatening to double the pawns and ready to castle!} 6.Bb5 6...Nxc3 {This trade is by far the most popular move. Black players love to relieve some central tension and capture on c3, allowing themselves to develop easily. This capture is played a whopping 64% of the time! We'll capture towards the center.} 7.bxc3 7...Bd7 {Bd7 defends the knight and indirectly defends e5. If Bxc6, Bxc6 and there will be pressure on g2. We should respond to Bd7 with a central pawn break!} 8.d4 8...e4 {Black could try attacking you. But in this case the knight will be able to go up to e5.} 9.Bxc6 9...Bxc6 {Black takes, preserving the structure. The knight comes to e5.} 10.Ne5 10...Bd6 {If the bishop is developed... We can double the pawns...} 11.Nxc6 11...bxc6 {Black recaptures...} 12.Qg4 {And we're winning one of these pawns!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 1: Black's Other 4th Moves (4...d5 or 4...Bc5) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/65d71217-3ec1-472a-9bd2-8c57637d216a/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...d5 {The most popular alternative to 4...Bb4 is 4...d5! It's nearly 25% of games. But we can have an advantage by taking. The idea is that we have an extra tempo. Usually, in the Sicilian Reversed, it's useful for Black to play with Closed Sicilian motifs. Going for an "open" Sicilian can backfire.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures. And now we'll develop and pin the c6 knight, threatening to double the pawns and ready to castle!} 6.Bb5 6...Bg4 {Pinning our knight is something Black sometimes plays. Sometimes meaning 6% of the time according to the database. Qa4 is a counterpin!} 7.Qa4 7...Nxc3 {The pressure is too great. Black may try to muddy the waters... But we can take the c6-knight with check!} 8.Bxc6+ 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures. And we'll take the bishop!} 9.Qxg4 9...Nd5 {Black retreats... And now we win a central pawn.} 10.Nxe5 10...Qd6 {To illustrate how bad Black's coordination is...} 11.Qc4 {They're not even attacking our knight. White is doing much better in this position, with more activity. Qxc6+ follows if ...Qxe5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 2: Black's Extra 4th Moves (4...e4, 4...Be7, and 4...g6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/f1d3d71d-c20e-4d40-b436-c0b48b708d82/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...e4 {Black will immediately attack the knight with ...e4 about 7% of the time. This is not among the most popular moves, but it's definitely worth considering! We'll bring our knight to g5. The e4-pawn won't survive.} 5.Ng5 5...Qe7 {Black tries to hold on to the pawn. But we can increase the pressure with Qc2.} 6.Qc2 6...Nb4 {Black gains a tempo... But our simple retreat keeps an eye on e4.} 7.Qb1 7...d5 {Black tries to go for maximum activity. With a3, we can just kick the knight out, removing a defender of d5.} 8.a3 8...Nd3+ {That's why Black prefers this check. We'll capture.} 9.Bxd3 9...exd3 {The pawn recaptures.} 10.Qxd3 {And we win a pawn! Now it's up to us to develop and keep any potential Black compensation at bay. We put some pressure on the d5-pawn. Moves like b3 to fianchetto and O-O are the plan!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 2: Black's Extra 4th Moves (4...e4, 4...Be7, and 4...g6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/f1d3d71d-c20e-4d40-b436-c0b48b708d82/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...Be7 {Be7 is sixth in terms of popularity, played only 5% of the time. You might come across it because it is solid. However, with squares like b4 or c5 available for the bishop, the e7-square isn't very attractive. The d4 pawn break should be automatic, if Black does nothing to prevent it.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades... And we'll recapture, keeping our pawn duo as always.} 6.exd4 6...d5 {Going for the active ...d5 is the second most popular move in this position, played 30% of the time. It's definitely an active approach you might see a lot. We'll capture on d5, intending to lure the knight away from the kingside.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {Black recaptures... We'll bring our bishop out, pointing at h7.} 8.Bd3 8...O-O {Both sides go for castling... And we're playing with the IQP, so we should make some IQP style plans. Stuff like Re1 and Ne5 should be considered.} 9.O-O 9...Bg4 {Black can choose to pin us. We'll simply challenge the pin.} 10.h3 10...Bh5 {Bh5 is the most popular. But it falls into a trap! We have a nice tactic, beginning with Bxh7+!} 11.Bxh7+ 11...Kxh7 {The king takes. We'll play Ng5+ when the h5-bishop comes under attack.} 12.Ng5+ 12...Bxg5 {If the knight is taken... We'll come in, attacking the bishop.} 13.Qxh5+ 13...Bh6 {Blocking is the best try. But now we can take on d5.} 14.Nxd5 14...Nxd4 {Black recovers the pawn. But we keep attacking. We'll capture on h6, further disrupting the pawn cover around the black king.} 15.Bxh6 15...gxh6 {The pawn recaptures.} 16.Rae1 {And we threaten Re7. Infiltrating Black's position via the e7-square is a very strong idea, even if the queens come off the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 2: Black's Extra 4th Moves (4...e4, 4...Be7, and 4...g6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/f1d3d71d-c20e-4d40-b436-c0b48b708d82/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...Be7 {Be7 is sixth in terms of popularity, played only 5% of the time. You might come across it because it is solid. However, with squares like b4 or c5 available for the bishop, the e7-square isn't very attractive. The d4 pawn break should be automatic, if Black does nothing to prevent it.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades... And we'll recapture, keeping our pawn duo as always.} 6.exd4 6...d5 {Going for the active ...d5 is the second most popular move in this position, played 30% of the time. It's definitely an active approach you might see a lot. We'll capture on d5, intending to lure the knight away from the kingside.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {Black recaptures... We'll bring our bishop out, pointing at h7.} 8.Bd3 8...O-O {Both sides go for castling... And we're playing with the IQP, so we should make some IQP style plans. Stuff like Re1 and Ne5 should be considered.} 9.O-O 9...Bg4 {Black can choose to pin us. We'll simply challenge the pin.} 10.h3 10...Bh5 {Bh5 is the most popular. But it falls into a trap! We have a nice tactic, beginning with Bxh7+!} 11.Bxh7+ 11...Kxh7 {The king takes. We'll play Ng5+ when the h5-bishop comes under attack.} 12.Ng5+ 12...Kg6 {The king moving forwards defends the bishop. We'll now check on d3. Black is forced to block because moving the king would be bad news.} 13.Qd3+ 13...f5 {f5 is played... And we can land a fork on e6. The point is to play Nxd5 on the next move.} 14.Ne6 14...Qd7 {So if the queen moves to d7... We capture on d5. This is winning. The knights can't be taken.} 15.Nxd5 15...Qxd5 {If the queen takes...} 16.Nf4+ {There's a fork!} *

