1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...Be7 {Be7 is sixth in terms of popularity, played only 5% of the time. You might come across it because it is solid. However, with squares like b4 or c5 available for the bishop, the e7-square isn't very attractive. The d4 pawn break should be automatic, if Black does nothing to prevent it.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades... And we'll recapture, keeping our pawn duo as always.} 6.exd4 6...d5 {Going for the active ...d5 is the second most popular move in this position, played 30% of the time. It's definitely an active approach you might see a lot. We'll capture on d5, intending to lure the knight away from the kingside.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {Black recaptures... We'll bring our bishop out, pointing at h7.} 8.Bd3 8...O-O {Both sides go for castling... And we're playing with the IQP, so we should make some IQP style plans. Stuff like Re1 and Ne5 should be considered.} 9.O-O 9...Bg4 {Black can choose to pin us. We'll simply challenge the pin.} 10.h3 10...Bxf3 {This capture is sometimes played. We'll take back with the queen, hitting d5.} 11.Qxf3 11...Ndb4 {The knight gets out of the way and attacks the d3 bishop. We'll push forward, attacking the knight!} 12.d5 12...Nxd3 {Black trades... We'll recapture on d3.} 13.Qxd3 13...Ne5 {And once the knight reaches the center...} 14.Qb5 {We can target the b7 pawn. We'll continue with Bf4, Re1/Rd1.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 2: Black's Extra 4th Moves (4...e4, 4...Be7, and 4...g6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/f1d3d71d-c20e-4d40-b436-c0b48b708d82/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...Be7 {Be7 is sixth in terms of popularity, played only 5% of the time. You might come across it because it is solid. However, with squares like b4 or c5 available for the bishop, the e7-square isn't very attractive. The d4 pawn break should be automatic, if Black does nothing to prevent it.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black trades... And we'll recapture, keeping our pawn duo as always.} 6.exd4 6...O-O {Castling is played 50% of the time here, being the most logical move after all. However, Black has allowed us to do something here. We'll go after the knight and gain space in the center!} 7.d5 7...Nb8 {The knight goes back to b8. We'll develop the bishop and point to h7. Notice our large space advantage.} 8.Bd3 8...d6 {Black wants to get their pieces out. So we'll play h3 to disrupt their free development.} 9.h3 9...Nbd7 {Black develops the knight. We'll castle.} 10.O-O 10...Nc5 {Of course, we aren't going to let Black take our bishop.} 11.Bc2 {So we fall back. We enjoy more space, and have easy ways to develop.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Reverse Sicilian Part 2 / Study 2: Black's Extra 4th Moves (4...e4, 4...Be7, and 4...g6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/11ceb6da-dfe5-403e-8389-da0e5f016f9b/studies/f1d3d71d-c20e-4d40-b436-c0b48b708d82/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {Black goes for the Sicilian Reversed! We'll bring our knight out.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. We'll put some pressure on e5.} 3.Nf3 3...Nc6 {Black defends. With e3, we prepare the d4 pawn break.} 4.e3 4...g6 {...g6, to fianchetto the bishop, is played only 2% of the time. It's definitely an unorthodox, and one could probably say unnecessary approach to the position. Let's just achieve our central pawn break.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Even though Black captures once... We are the ones left with the pawn duo.} 6.exd4 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes... Let's make use of our central pawns with d5!} 7.d5 7...Ne7 {The knight goes back... We'll develop with Bd3.} 8.Bd3 8...O-O {Black castles... And so do we.} 9.O-O 9...d6 {When Black gets ready to activate their bishop...} 10.h3 {We cover the g4 square, preventing Bg4. We stand moderately better since we have more space. We can continue the game with moves like Re1, Bg5, and Qd2. Even queenside play is available should we want it.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 1: Popular Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/53927157-63b6-4a7d-9e83-a96232c3240e/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the Reverse Sicilian. The most critical response. We'll build behind the pawn and activate the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {2...Nc6 is the second most common move by Black, played 19% of the time. Black chooses to activate the queenside knight first and gain more control of d4. Often, Black will follow it up with ...Nf6, but here we'll look at alternatives. Let's get both knights out, still pressurizing e5. It is worth noting that 3...Bb4 often leads us to transpositions if we play 4.Qc2.} 3.Nf3 3...g6 {An interesting option for Black is 3...g6, which is played only 4% of the time, but is definitely instructive and worth covering. Black wants to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. This move is a bit slower and gives us time for a quick d4. We'll challenge the e5-pawn immediately and force Black to make a decision.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {The vast majority of the time (65%) Black will capture in the center, so it's extremely important to know what to do. Of course, we'll recapture. We aren't afraid of any trades.} 5.Nxd4 5...Bg7 {If Black chooses to develop and attack our knight one more time, that's definitely more logical than 5...Nxd4. If we defend with e3 now, our dark-squared bishop will be blocked. If we retreat, we've lost a tempo. Therefore, we'll take on c6.} 6.Nxc6 6...bxc6 {Black recaptures towards the center. And we'll go for g3, aiming for Bg2. Nothing is contesting the long diagonal.} 7.g3 7...Ne7 {Black also wants to keep their bishop active, so they jump to e7 instead of f6. We'll fianchetto.} 8.Bg2 8...O-O {Black is ready to castle.} 9.O-O {We'll castle as well. A calm and equal-ish position, but we've got a slightly more comfortable setup. We'll aim for Qc2 and eventually develop the dark-squared bishop, potentially with b3 and Bb2. The b3-setup is desirable to blunt the rook's attack along the b-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 1: Popular Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/53927157-63b6-4a7d-9e83-a96232c3240e/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the Reverse Sicilian. The most critical response. We'll build behind the pawn and activate the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {2...Nc6 is the second most common move by Black, played 19% of the time. Black chooses to activate the queenside knight first and gain more control of d4. Often, Black will follow it up with ...Nf6, but here we'll look at alternatives. Let's get both knights out, still pressurizing e5. It is worth noting that 3...Bb4 often leads us to transpositions if we play 4.Qc2.} 3.Nf3 3...f5 {If Black goes for the ...f5-approach (played 9% of the time), we can also play actively in the center. We'll see a similar position in the next study. Our go-to move is d4 here, counter-challenging the center. The point is that ...f5 attempts to control the center, and with 4. d4 we want to take away this control.} 4.d4 4...e4 {If our knight is attacked, many moves are possible. Ng5, Ng1, Nd2. There's more than 45,000 games here I like Nd2 because we set up a little positional trap. 5...Nxd4 runs into 6.Ndxe4, with a discovered attack on the knight.} 5.Nd2 5...Nf6 {Black should just develop. We connect our pawns. This means our dark-squared bishop will have to be developed on b2 or a3 if available.} 6.e3 6...Bb4 {...Bb4 is a popular idea. We're not afraid of the capture on c3. Be2 to castle is a good plan.} 7.Be2 7...O-O {Black castles.} 8.O-O {And so do we. We'll play Qc2 and try to get our bishop out on b2 or a3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 1: Popular Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/53927157-63b6-4a7d-9e83-a96232c3240e/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the Reverse Sicilian. The most critical response. We'll build behind the pawn and activate the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {2...Nc6 is the second most common move by Black, played 19% of the time. Black chooses to activate the queenside knight first and gain more control of d4. Often, Black will follow it up with ...Nf6, but here we'll look at alternatives. Let's get both knights out, still pressurizing e5. It is worth noting that 3...Bb4 often leads us to transpositions if we play 4.Qc2.} 3.Nf3 3...f5 {If Black goes for the ...f5-approach (played 9% of the time), we can also play actively in the center. We'll see a similar position in the next study. Our go-to move is d4 here, counter-challenging the center. The point is that ...f5 attempts to control the center, and with 4. d4 we want to take away this control.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {We should also examine this capture. We'll of course recapture. Most of the games that go along these lines also end up trading knights.} 5.Nxd4 5...Nxd4 {These recaptures activate our queen, but Black didn't really have good alternatives according to the engine. Black trades to defend f5. We'll recapture, with an active queen.} 6.Qxd4 6...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. Since f5 controls e4, I like g3 for Bg2. We'll fight for e4 this way.} 7.g3 7...Be7 {Black brings their bishop out to castle. We'll fianchetto, fighting for the critical e4 square.} 8.Bg2 8...O-O {Black castles.} 9.O-O {And so do we. We have space to develop our pieces and our queen is active. White is slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 1: Popular Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/53927157-63b6-4a7d-9e83-a96232c3240e/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the Reverse Sicilian. The most critical response. We'll build behind the pawn and activate the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Bc5 {A move that's played 12% of the time is 2...Bc5. It looks like a logical and natural square for the bishop. However, we'll prove the bishop on c5 is misplaced once we get our pawns rolling down the board! As we saw in the previous chapters, our typical strategy is to slowly gain control of the center with the e3-d4 advances. We'll follow our plan and now d4 is threatened with tempo.} 3.e3 3...Nc6 {That's why Black activates the knight to c6 and prevents d4 for the moment. We'll reintroduce the threat of d4 with Nf3, though. Both players developing towards the center and playing around the d4-square.} 4.Nf3 4...d6 {At this point, nothing can stop our d4-advance. One of the solid options is ...d6, strengthening the center. We'll go for d4! We take control of the center.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Most often will Black capture on d4. However, opening of the position plays right into our hands since we are already better developed. Let's recapture and hit the bishop again. Now our dark-squared bishop is open as well.} 6.exd4 6...Bb4 {The most active move is ...Bb4, putting pressure on our knight. We don't mind Black capturing, however. Let's just keep developing and prepare castling.} 7.Bd3 7...Bg4 {Black has to develop quickly as well, so they activate the bishop to g4. It seems like Black is pressuring our position and is threatening to capture on d4. But we can actually give that pawn up! Let's castle. What's our idea?} 8.O-O 8...Nxd4 {Well, if Black takes on d4, we've got an intermezzo that solves all of our problems. We'll get out of the pin with check, also putting pressure on the bishop on b4. Black has to retreat.} 9.Qa4+ 9...Nc6 {The only move to block the check and defend the bishop. As we have a lead in development and have sacrificed a pawn, we want to play aggressively. Nd5 attacks the b4-bishop once more.} 10.Nd5 10...Bxf3 {Yes, we've let Black capture the knight, but we won't recapture on f3. We'll take on b4 instead, putting more pressure on the knight on c6! The bishop on f3 is also hanging.} 11.Nxb4 11...a5 {The best option for Black is to counter attack our knight now, trying to relieve the tension on the c6-knight. If we take on f3, Black will take on b4. Let's jump back to the center, keeping our knight active. Black still has to deal with the bishop on f3, and capturing on d5 is impossible because of the pin on the c6-knight.} 12.Nd5 12...Bg4 {The bishop retreats to g4 instead. After all the fireworks, we still have some moves to throw in. Like activating the rook with check, for example.} 13.Re1+ 13...Be6 {Making Black move the bishop one more time to block the check.} 14.Bd2 {And we'll play the calm Bd2, simply developing. Our pieces are very powerful and we have more than enough compensation for one pawn. Black still has to make a couple of moves to untangle the pieces and castle. White is much better in this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 1: Popular Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/53927157-63b6-4a7d-9e83-a96232c3240e/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the Reverse Sicilian. The most critical response. We'll build behind the pawn and activate the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nc6 {2...Nc6 is the second most common move by Black, played 19% of the time. Black chooses to activate the queenside knight first and gain more control of d4. Often, Black will follow it up with ...Nf6, but here we'll look at alternatives. Let's get both knights out, still pressurizing e5. It is worth noting that 3...Bb4 often leads us to transpositions if we play 4.Qc2.} 3.Nf3 3...d6 {A more passive option is 3...d6, defending the e5-pawn. However, Black will opt for this move 11% of the time, more than 3...f5. We'll still continue with the active d4, expanding and challenging the center.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {The most logical is for Black to capture... After which some trades will follow in the center.} 5.Nxd4 5...Nxd4 {Black can capture again and lure our queen to d4. Our queen arrives in the center of the board.} 6.Qxd4 6...Nf6 {The most natural move here is finally getting the knight to f6...} 7.g3 {To which we'll respond with g3. We don't have anything to worry about in this position, except for quick development and castling! There's no knight on b8 to dislodge our queen from the center, so we'll keep it there as long as possible. We can even go for a double fianchetto here.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 1: Popular Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/53927157-63b6-4a7d-9e83-a96232c3240e/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the Reverse Sicilian. The most critical response. We'll build behind the pawn and activate the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {The most common move in this position is 2...Nf6, activating the knight. It is the most natural move, after all. It's played 36% of the time, which is almost 10 million games! We have a choice here, to go g3-Bg2 or not? In the English White can choose to fianchetto and develop slowly or develop more quickly and fight for the center. We are doing the latter with Nf3.} 3.Nf3 3...e4 {An aggressive move for Black, although played only 8% of the time. 3...d6 4.g3 would be a transposition to our King's Indian Chapter. The e4-pawn might have a very good chance of being over-extended, however. We'll move the knight forward and attack the pawn on e4!} 4.Ng5 4...d5 {The most popular move is actually ...d5, played a whopping 34% of the time. However, it's a mistake! Black is going to be over-extended. We'll take on d5 and undermine the center.} 5.cxd5 5...Bf5 {That's why ...Bf5 defends the pawn rather than Black recapturing on d5 immediately. But this is a blunder, and we've got a trick to punish it! We'll actually capture on e4!} 6.Ngxe4 6...Nxe4 {Black takes back with the knight. We'll trade again...} 7.Nxe4 7...Bxe4 {And it looks like Black is up a piece.} 8.Qa4+ {However, there's a check on a4 which Black hasn't counted on! The bishop is forked and we'll get back the piece, have a material advantage and a winning position!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 2: Rare Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/fd397b48-5838-4ccb-835b-b09017afa861/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares a kingside fianchetto only 1% of the time. However, that's still around 250,000 games! Let's unleash a powerful idea. We'll strike in the center, with d4, before Black starts developing. Black has to be careful now.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Capturing in the center is already on the verge of losing for Black, and it's played 42% of the time! The position is already extremely dangerous because our queen gets to the center, attacking the rook and preventing Black playing ...Bg7! The best move for Black is ...Qf6, but even then will we get some tempi and an advantage.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nf6 {However, ...Nf6 loses the game on the spot! And the best thing is, the moves play themselves. Let's pin the knight immediately with Bg5. Black has two options to defend, and both lose.} 5.Bg5 5...Bg7 {The more logical ...Bg7 is played 95% of the time. However, it doesn't save Black in the slightest. We'll continue putting pressure on the pinned piece by playing Nd5. Black is out of options here; they only have two defenders and we are attacking the knight with three pieces!} 6.Nd5 6...Nc6 {They could try to counter attack our queen, but we are quicker. Let's capture with check first, simply winning a piece. If Black recaptures, we'll simply play Qxf6.} 7.Nxf6+ 7...Kf8 {They move the king to f8 instead. Now our knight and queen are under pressure, so it's important to know the next move as well. We'll sidestep to h4 to keep defending the knight on f6. Black is out of options here.} 8.Qh4 8...h6 {If they try to chase us out...} 9.Nh7+ {We've got Nh7+! We check the king with a discovered attack on Black's queen on d8. We are going to be up a queen. We can't really ask for more.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 2: Rare Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/fd397b48-5838-4ccb-835b-b09017afa861/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares a kingside fianchetto only 1% of the time. However, that's still around 250,000 games! Let's unleash a powerful idea. We'll strike in the center, with d4, before Black starts developing. Black has to be careful now.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Capturing in the center is already on the verge of losing for Black, and it's played 42% of the time! The position is already extremely dangerous because our queen gets to the center, attacking the rook and preventing Black playing ...Bg7! The best move for Black is ...Qf6, but even then will we get some tempi and an advantage.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nf6 {However, ...Nf6 loses the game on the spot! And the best thing is, the moves play themselves. Let's pin the knight immediately with Bg5. Black has two options to defend, and both lose.} 5.Bg5 5...Be7 {Black can unpin with ...Be7, and they do so 3% of the time. However, it's pretty straightforward why that's a bad move. The knight on f6 is still pinned to the rook on h8! That means we can still continue piling pressure on the knight with Nd5. It can't move.} 6.Nd5 6...Nc6 {Black can't do anything but develop and attack our queen. However, we'll capture with check. If Black takes back, we'll simply be up a piece.} 7.Nxf6+ 7...Kf8 {If they slide to f8, which is the alternative...} 8.Bh6# {We have checkmate with the bishop! An 8 move checkmate that only goes to show how dangerous 3...exd4 is for Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 2: Rare Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/fd397b48-5838-4ccb-835b-b09017afa861/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...Bb4 {As people are taught to get the knights out before the bishops, 2...Bb4 is played only 6% of the time. However, it's a reasonable developing move that threatens to disrupt our pawn structure. Although unusual to move the queen on move 3, we'll do it here. 3.Nd5 was a tempting alternative, but 3.Qc2 could be a shortcut for us to get back into Chapter 1.} 3.Qc2 3...Nf6 {Black continues by developing the knight, ready to castle. We'll do the same. As you can see, we have a similar position as some Chapter 1 positions, with minor differences. Here, 4...Nc6 is the most common move, when 5.e3 sends us back to Chapter 1. Let's explore some alternatives for Black.} 4.Nf3 4...d6 {Black could defend the e5-pawn with ...d6, which is the second most popular move here, played 29% of the time. An interesting idea for us is to give a check on a4! This move might seem surprising since we are moving the queen for the second time in five moves. However, it works very well because Black's bishop is vulnerable.} 5.Qa4+ 5...Nc6 {Black has to block with the knight in order to defend the bishop. Having great control of the d5-square, we'll utilize our setup to jump there and put more pressure on the bishop. Black can't capture on d5 because after cxd5 the pinned knight on c6 would be under attack.} 6.Nd5 6...Bc5 {So they retreat to c5. But they aren't out of the woods yet. Let's continue chasing the bishop with b4! In the process of targeting the bishop, we are also gaining space and improving some pieces.} 7.b4 7...Bb6 {Black retreats to b6. Our plan was to trade for the bishop all along. Here, we gain the bishop pair and damage Black's pawn structure.} 8.Nxb6 8...cxb6 {Black has to recapture with the c-pawn. Let's take advantage of our approach to grab even more space. We'll attack the c6-knight and chase it away from the center.} 9.b5 9...Ne7 {The only safe square is e7. Having disrupted Black's pawn structure, captured their bishop and pushed their knight back...} 10.e3 {It's time to develop ourselves. Positionally speaking, White is better here, with prospects of d4. However, an interesting chess game remains to be played!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 2: Rare Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/fd397b48-5838-4ccb-835b-b09017afa861/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...f5 {2...f5 is fifth in popularity and Black players opt for it 10% of the time. It's an aggressive option that resembles the "Grand Prix" attack that White uses against the Sicilian (with e4 and f4). We'll fight against it by going for the immediate d4! The idea is to split Black's e-and f-pawns and try to counter-attack quickly.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Capturing on d4 only gives us what we want, which is dominance in the center. This move is played 27% of the time. We'll recapture with the queen.} 4.Qxd4 4...Nc6 {Yes, Black can develop the knight with tempo, but we don't have to retreat all the way. We can slide to d3 with an attack on the f5-pawn! It's actually not that comfortable for Black to defend this pawn. They could play ...Nge7 or ...g6, but both those moves have negative consequences. The computer suggests ...Nf6 here, allowing us to capture on f5.} 5.Qd3 {We can slide to d3 with an attack on the f5-pawn! It's actually not that comfortable for Black to defend this pawn. They could play ...Nge7 or ...g6, but both those moves have negative consequences. The computer suggests ...Nf6 here, allowing us to capture on f5.} 5...d6 {However, in online games, specially Blitz, you might see ...d6 frequently. The direct downside is that it blocks the dark-squared bishop.} 6.g3 {We'll just calmly continue with g3 and Bg2, having placed our queen on d3. No need to hurry - we've got a comfortable position.} *

