[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/fd397b48-5838-4ccb-835b-b09017afa861/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...d6 {2...d6 is the fourth most popular move, played 10% of the time. It's a solid approach, defending the center but blocking the bishop on f8. We'll continue with Nf3 anyway. It's the best square for the knight to develop to.} 3.Nf3 3...f5 {Definitely an active approach from Black. They let us place a pawn on c4, but they want to occupy the d4 and the e4-squares! We'll prepare the fianchetto of our bishop. It's often the best way for us to develop.} 4.g3 4...Nf6 {Black develops the knight naturally. We'll prepare castling...} 5.Bg2 5...Be7 {And so does Black. This is more of a positional line with both sides activating pieces before going to battle. We'll castle short...} 6.O-O 6...O-O {And so does Black. What plan do we continue with from here? An interesting idea is b4! We advance on the queenside where we've got more space with the c-pawn.} 7.b4 7...Qe8 {An idea is ...Qe8, going to h5. Black also wants to push ...f4 in the future if possible. However, we are still not worried. With d3, we'll establish our pawn chain in the center and remain solid.} 8.d3 8...Qh5 {Black goes for the queen activation...} 9.e3 {And we'll play e3 to control the f4-square. Going into the middlegame, our bishop is extremely strong on g2, and once the knight moves, the queens might get exchanged. Our play should be focused on the queenside where we've got the advantage and can keep pushing. All in all, there's chess to be played!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 2: Rare Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/fd397b48-5838-4ccb-835b-b09017afa861/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...f5 {2...f5 is fifth in popularity and Black players opt for it 10% of the time. It's an aggressive option that resembles the "Grand Prix" attack that White uses against the Sicilian (with e4 and f4). We'll fight against it by going for the immediate d4! The idea is to split Black's e-and f-pawns and try to counter-attack quickly.} 3.d4 3...d6 {According to the database, 3...d6 is only played 5% of the time here. Let's take on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...dxe5 {Black recaptures, naturally, and now the queens face each other. We'll trade, as it leads us to an advantage.} 5.Qxd8+ 5...Kxd8 {Black has to lose castling rights now. And with the king on d8, we can develop with tempo! We are the ones controlling the game.} 6.Bg5+ 6...Be7 {Once Black blocks with the bishop, do you see another move we can throw in?} 7.O-O-O+ {It's long castling with check! This line is basically forced after 3...d6, and we've developed a minor piece and castled with tempo, while Black is still struggling to get their pieces out. We are better here and will often go on to win the game from this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 2: Rare Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/fd397b48-5838-4ccb-835b-b09017afa861/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares a kingside fianchetto only 1% of the time. However, that's still around 250,000 games! Let's unleash a powerful idea. We'll strike in the center, with d4, before Black starts developing. Black has to be careful now.} 3.d4 3...Bg7 {It's logical to continue the fianchetto, but now we will win tempi for our development! 3...Bg7 is played 45% of the time. We'll take on e5.} 4.dxe5 4...Bxe5 {Black is forced to recapture with the bishop. How should we continue now? We can activate the knight with tempo!} 5.Nf3 5...Bg7 {The bishop retreats...} 6.g3 {And we'll play a fianchetto of our own. We are the only player with some control in the center and are better developed. Our bishop will stand strong on g2, and we'll be able to get the dark-squared bishop out as well.} *

