1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...Bb4 {As people are taught to get the knights out before the bishops, 2...Bb4 is played only 6% of the time. However, it's a reasonable developing move that threatens to disrupt our pawn structure. Although unusual to move the queen on move 3, we'll do it here. 3.Nd5 was a tempting alternative, but 3.Qc2 could be a shortcut for us to get back into Chapter 1.} 3.Qc2 3...Nf6 {Black continues by developing the knight, ready to castle. We'll do the same. As you can see, we have a similar position as some Chapter 1 positions, with minor differences. Here, 4...Nc6 is the most common move, when 5.e3 sends us back to Chapter 1. Let's explore some alternatives for Black.} 4.Nf3 4...Bxc3 {4...Bxc3 is the third most popular move here, played 17% of the time. Black trades our knight off. It might seem like we played 3.Qc2 to recapture with the queen, but... We'll take back with the b-pawn, towards the center! This helps us build a strong pawn formation like we encountered in Chapter 1.} 5.bxc3 5...d6 {Black has to deal with the e5-pawn now, so they do with ...d6. Now the light-squared bishop is open.} 6.e4 {And we'll continue with e4, supported by the queen. We are planning a similar assault as in Chapter 1, with d3, g3 and Bg2. Later on, we could play for f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Reverse Sicilian Part 3 / Study 2: Rare Reverse Sicilian Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f5b2988b-b5a3-49e2-9cc7-57fa2ff6bdcd/studies/fd397b48-5838-4ccb-835b-b09017afa861/lines"]

1.c4 1...e5 {As in the previous two chapters, Black goes for the reverse Sicilian with 1...e5. The e-pawn takes the center and discourages 2.d4 from us. We'll continue by activating the queenside knight. On the second move, Black has got a lot of options.} 2.Nc3 2...f5 {2...f5 is fifth in popularity and Black players opt for it 10% of the time. It's an aggressive option that resembles the "Grand Prix" attack that White uses against the Sicilian (with e4 and f4). We'll fight against it by going for the immediate d4! The idea is to split Black's e-and f-pawns and try to counter-attack quickly.} 3.d4 3...e4 {Black could gain more space and decline the trade by pushing. 3...e4 is the most popular move in this position, played over half of the time with 51%. It gives us the f4-square for our dark-squared bishop, however. We've already activated two pieces against Black's none.} 4.Bf4 4...Nf6 {Now they finally get a minor piece out. Having activated the dark-squared bishop, we can now push e3 and start thinking about the kingside activation. On the queenside and in the center, we've reached our desired setup.} 5.e3 5...Be7 {Once Black develops the bishop and prepares to castle, there's an interesting idea for us.} 6.h4 {We'll go for the immediate h4! We want to push the h-pawn before developing the knight to h3, and leave ourselves with potential attacking opportunities in the future. Our bishop can develop to e2. White is slightly better already!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/b53b3744-578d-4bc4-b9c9-5019dc3ac755/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The most popular option here is ...O-O, played 58% of the time. Black has developed the kingside and simply castles. We'll do the same, developing in a calm manner.} 7.Be2 7...Nbd7 {If Black plays this precise move order (which they do more than half of the time), then we will have "wasted" a tempo on Be2 in conjunction with our kingside attack. Of course we can just go for d4 and O-O and play positionally as well, but let's keep the Bayonet option alive by placing our queen actively on c2, keeping an eye on the kingside. At this point, let's cover some options for Black.} 8.Qc2 8...b6 {Once again, Black can opt for ...b6. However, as mentioned, this is a slow setup that definitely gives us the green light to start attacking. Let's go for the usual Rg1 idea. You know what we are aiming for.} 9.Rg1 9...Bb7 {Just to illustrate how the game could go on, let's look at a real game played between G. Oparin and R. Kevlishvili in Columbia in 2019. Black activated the bishop to b7 here. And, as one should, Oparin started attacking! The next goal was g5.} 10.g4 10...e5 {In chess, it's often a good idea to counter your opponent's attack on the side of the board by creating complications in the center. This is what Black tried here with ...e5. However, that didn't stop the g-pawn from attacking the f6-knight.} 11.g5 11...Ne8 {The only square it could really jump to was e8. Did Black's advance in the center mean that White should slow down? Absolutely not! The h-pawn followed right in the g-pawn's tracks. Suddenly, two dangerous pawns found themselves near Black's king.} 12.h4 12...Rc8 {It was like Kevlishvili was ignoring the kingside attack by playing on the queenside. His position wasn't losing yet, but White already had some advantage. And the moves played themselves. With two pawns on the fifth rank, it was probably time for Black to start reacting.} 13.h5 13...Nc7 {Black should maybe have played e4; however, he played the slow ...Nc7 with the idea of ...Ne6. According to the computer, Oparin should have gone Bd3 here and allowed the fork on e4! After 14.Bd3 e4 15.Nxe4 dxe4 16.Bxe4 White is winning with the two bishops and kingside aggression. Oparin wanted to get another piece into the attack, so he jumped to h4 with the idea of Nf5.} 14.Nh4 14...Ne6 {Black got the knight to e6... But as soon as he added a defender into the position, he had to face another attacking piece! The bishop on d6 was under fire.} 15.Nf5 15...Be7 {Black retreated to e7...} 16.O-O-O {And after O-O-O, the game continued. However, White had the clear advantage. Black's pieces were all tangled up, and White had the active bishop on b2 and prospects of pushing g6 to continue the attack. That's the Bayonet Slav.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/b53b3744-578d-4bc4-b9c9-5019dc3ac755/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...e5 {6...e5 is not the best way to play, and its popularity being only 3% confirms that. Now, we'll introduce a repetitive pattern throughout this study. Since Black has weakened the d5-pawn, let's take it.} 7.cxd5 7...cxd5 {The pawn recaptures. And here's a move we most often play in these types of positions. Let's go Nb5, with an attack on the bishop. That bishop is one of Black's strongest pieces in this opening and conveniently defends the center.} 8.Nb5 8...Nc6 {There's really no point in Black retreating now. We'll take.} 9.Nxd6+ 9...Qxd6 {The queen captures back.} 10.a4 {And our idea here is a4, going for Ba3! This is one way to gain control of the light squares, another one being 10.Bb5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/b53b3744-578d-4bc4-b9c9-5019dc3ac755/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The most popular option here is ...O-O, played 58% of the time. Black has developed the kingside and simply castles. We'll do the same, developing in a calm manner.} 7.Be2 7...e5 {Black could also try to go ...e5 here. We will deploy a similar idea to the one we saw on the branch on move 6. Let's take in the center.} 8.cxd5 8...cxd5 {Once Black recaptures... There's Nb5 again. We'll harass the bishop.} 9.Nb5 9...Nc6 {If Black decides to play without taking any risks, that's okay. We'll just capture.} 10.Nxd6 10...Qxd6 {Now, instead of going for the previous a4-idea, I suggest a different approach. Let's challenge the center! Black taking on d4 would only give us extra activity...} 11.d4 11...e4 {So they push ...e4. But the d4-pawn also serves as support to our knight. Which we can now place in the center! Black can't play ...Nxe5 because of the fork on e5.} 12.Ne5 12...Nd7 {In order to remove our most active piece, they'll have to go ...Nd7 to add more pressure to it. And in all fairness, this does force us to trade. I recommend trading on c6 to leave Black's pieces tied up on the back ranks.} 13.Nxc6 13...bxc6 {Capturing with the pawn establishes a nice pawn chain.} 14.Bc3 {And here's a nice idea. We'll go Bc3, with the idea of Qd2 to gain control of the queenside dark squares. If we achieve that, we'll have threats like Bb4 to skewer the queen or Ba5 to improve the bishop. We'll also take advantage of the semi-open c-file by placing a rook on c1. It's important to play against Black's c6-weakness.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/b53b3744-578d-4bc4-b9c9-5019dc3ac755/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Be7 {Black players choose 5...Be7 the most often! This move has been played nearly a third of the time, 33%. Black goes for solid and traditional development and wants to castle quickly. Let's activate our dark-squared bishop, as we had intended to do anyway.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The king castles short. We'll activate the queen. As always, it's eyeing the kingside, ready to join a potential future attack.} 7.Qc2 7...Nbd7 {If Black goes for ...Ndb7 to activate the queenside knight... Now I would be very tempted to go for the aggressive g4 or Rg1 plan. In practice White has scored tremendously, but the engine disapproves! We'll keep things simple and safe with d4. Black has to untangle their pieces somehow.} 8.d4 8...b6 {And most often, they'll try to do that with ...b6. The bishop is coming to b7. Having decided against the aggressive g4, I suggest we go for the queen-bishop battery with Bd3. The f6-knight is the only defender of h7 at the moment!} 9.Bd3 9...Bb7 {Black doesn't have anything to worry about for the moment, though. But neither do we. Let's castle, safe and sound. The positional approach works as well.} 10.O-O 10...h6 {The prophylactic ...h6 is on the board. Black doesn't want to constantly worry about h7 and takes away the g5-square.} 11.Rad1 {The last move of this line is Rad1, centralizing one of the rooks. Having positioned a lot of pieces to control the e4-square, we are planning to centralize the other rook with Re1 and play for the e4-break! White is slightly better with a comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/b53b3744-578d-4bc4-b9c9-5019dc3ac755/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The most popular option here is ...O-O, played 58% of the time. Black has developed the kingside and simply castles. We'll do the same, developing in a calm manner.} 7.Be2 7...Nbd7 {If Black plays this precise move order (which they do more than half of the time), then we will have "wasted" a tempo on Be2 in conjunction with our kingside attack. Of course we can just go for d4 and O-O and play positionally as well, but let's keep the Bayonet option alive by placing our queen actively on c2, keeping an eye on the kingside. At this point, let's cover some options for Black.} 8.Qc2 8...e5 {If Black goes for ...e5 here, you probably already know what to do. We'll take on d5.} 9.cxd5 9...cxd5 {Once the pawn recaptures... There's Nb5, attacking the bishop, as per usual.} 10.Nb5 10...Bb8 {This time it's got the b8-square...} 11.Rc1 {But we can force the issue with Rc1! Our idea is Nc7. Getting Black's dark-squared bishop is of highest priority!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 4"]
