1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The most popular option here is ...O-O, played 58% of the time. Black has developed the kingside and simply castles. We'll do the same, developing in a calm manner.} 7.Be2 7...Nbd7 {If Black plays this precise move order (which they do more than half of the time), then we will have "wasted" a tempo on Be2 in conjunction with our kingside attack. Of course we can just go for d4 and O-O and play positionally as well, but let's keep the Bayonet option alive by placing our queen actively on c2, keeping an eye on the kingside. At this point, let's cover some options for Black.} 8.Qc2 8...e5 {If Black goes for ...e5 here, you probably already know what to do. We'll take on d5.} 9.cxd5 9...Nxd5 {Important to note: it's also possible for Black to recapture in the center with the knight. This shouldn't prevent our plan, though. Black just gave up control of the e4-square, so we'll put the knight there! This unleashes our bishop on b2 as well.} 10.Ne4 10...Bc7 {Once Black retreats...} 11.g4 {Let's go for the standard attacking idea with g4! We've got the active queen and bishop and will support the g-pawn with the rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/b53b3744-578d-4bc4-b9c9-5019dc3ac755/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The most popular option here is ...O-O, played 58% of the time. Black has developed the kingside and simply castles. We'll do the same, developing in a calm manner.} 7.Be2 7...Nbd7 {If Black plays this precise move order (which they do more than half of the time), then we will have "wasted" a tempo on Be2 in conjunction with our kingside attack. Of course we can just go for d4 and O-O and play positionally as well, but let's keep the Bayonet option alive by placing our queen actively on c2, keeping an eye on the kingside. At this point, let's cover some options for Black.} 8.Qc2 8...Re8 {Black's most common move is ...Re8, possibly supporting ...e5. Let's go with h3. I propose this subtle idea as we want to push g4 (similar to Rg1) without completely burning our bridges (we can still castle!) 9...b6 is similar to our 8...b6 line. We would respond with 10.g4!} 9.h3 9...e5 {Finally, Black pushes ...e5. Do you know what to play here? Well, nothing new. We'll just capture in the center with the idea of Nb5 if Black recaptures with the pawn. Compare the 8...e5 line.} 10.cxd5 10...Nxd5 {It comes in handy to cover what to do if Black chooses to play ...Nxd5. We've got the e4-square available for our knight now. Black's bishop must move! If 11...Bc7 what do you think we should play? 12.g4, of course!} 11.Ne4 11...Bf8 {If Black chooses to retreat to f8, there's another defender of the king now.} 12.a3 {We'll go for a3. A useful positional move to take control of b4. We can decide whether to castle next with a quieter positional struggle or go for the aggressive g4-lunge which has been in the spirit of our study!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/b53b3744-578d-4bc4-b9c9-5019dc3ac755/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The most popular option here is ...O-O, played 58% of the time. Black has developed the kingside and simply castles. We'll do the same, developing in a calm manner.} 7.Be2 7...e5 {Black could also try to go ...e5 here. We will deploy a similar idea to the one we saw on the branch on move 6. Let's take in the center.} 8.cxd5 8...cxd5 {Once Black recaptures... There's Nb5 again. We'll harass the bishop.} 9.Nb5 9...e4 {A more aggressive option for Black is to push ...e4. This does open up our b2-bishop, though. And gives us the nice central d4-square for the knight. However, Black's point is to save the dark-squared bishop.} 10.Nfd4 10...Be5 {Which they can with ...Be5, for example. It would be great if we were the only player with a dark-squared bishop. Do you see a direct way to achieve that? With Ne6, we sacrifice the knight by forking the queen and the rook. Black is forced to capture.} 11.Ne6 11...fxe6 {Once they do... We can get their dark-squared bishop.} 12.Bxe5 12...Nc6 {True, Black can develop with tempo here...} 13.Bb2 {But once we retreat, we are indeed the only ones with a dark-squared bishop. And a very active one at that.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 9"]
