1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The most popular option here is ...O-O, played 58% of the time. Black has developed the kingside and simply castles. We'll do the same, developing in a calm manner.} 7.Be2 7...b6 {At multiple branches in this study will we see Black being able to play ...b6. This type of development usually invites our Bayonet idea. We'll activate the queen to a nice light-squared diagonal.} 8.Qc2 8...Bb7 {Once Black completes the fianchetto...} 9.Rg1 {We'll get the rook off the diagonal and prepare g4! This signals the beginning of our attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 1: Black's Popular Setups / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/b53b3744-578d-4bc4-b9c9-5019dc3ac755/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav Defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. Our queen's knight comes out!} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. We'll go straight for development with Bb2. This bishop often becomes very active in the middlegame. Now, let's go over some popular options for Black on move 5.} 5.b3 5...Bd6 {From a theoretical point of view, this is the most precise move. The good news for us is that this is only played 23% of the time, making it second in popularity. Black logically develops the bishop to an active square and prepares castling. We'll continue as planned and activate the dark-squared bishop.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {The most popular option here is ...O-O, played 58% of the time. Black has developed the kingside and simply castles. We'll do the same, developing in a calm manner.} 7.Be2 7...e5 {Black could also try to go ...e5 here. We will deploy a similar idea to the one we saw on the branch on move 6. Let's take in the center.} 8.cxd5 8...cxd5 {Once Black recaptures... There's Nb5 again. We'll harass the bishop.} 9.Nb5 9...e4 {A more aggressive option for Black is to push ...e4. This does open up our b2-bishop, though. And gives us the nice central d4-square for the knight. However, Black's point is to save the dark-squared bishop.} 10.Nfd4 10...Be7 {Black could try to preserve the bishop in a more passive manner by retreating to e7. However, this gives us additional chances to keep activating pieces. With Rc1, we are threatening Nc7 to trap the rook.} 11.Rc1 11...a6 {Black can kick our knight away...} 12.Nc3 {But we've got other ideas. After jumping back to c3, a great idea would be to reroute to the more active c5-square and try to provoke Black to trade that dark-squared bishop after all. On the kingside, we could try to solve our problems by undermining Black's structure with f3 after castling.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Nbd7 {This move is a bit inflexible for Black because the knight can no longer go to c6 in case of a cxd5 trade. Players commit to ...Nbd7 18% of the time, rendering it the third most popular move. Let's get our bishop to b2, as planned.} 6.Bb2 6...Bd6 {Finally, Black activates the bishop to d6. There was no reason to choose the more passive ...Be7 route now. We'll activate the queen. We continue mobilizing and hold back d4 for a more opportune moment. However, we also have the powerful Bayonet strategy which is our real idea!} 7.Qc2 7...O-O {The most popular move at this point is ...O-O. However, White scores well after this move, mostly because of the Bayonet with Rg1 and g4! The g-pawn is coming.} 8.Rg1 8...e5 {Meeting flank attacks with central expansion is usually a good idea. That's what Black does in this instance. Let's take in the center, as we usually do.} 9.cxd5 9...cxd5 {Once Black takes back, we reach a familiar position. Let's target the bishop on d6.} 10.Nb5 10...Bb8 {The most common retreating square is b8. Black is under a lot of pressure as we'll be able to invade via c7 and obtain the dark square bishop, when our bishop on b2 will be completely dominant. Practically this looks really tough for Black, but there is a miracle computer resource to avoid the worst (that your opponents will never find!)} 11.Rc1 11...a6 {Black can try to prevent our plan with ...a6, attacking the knight. In this instance, we'll jump to c7 anyway.} 12.Nc7 12...Ra7 {The bizarre resource that the computer relies on is ...Ra7. Black simply slides the rook out of the way. The idea is ...b5 to attack our knight. We'll jump to g5 with pressure on the h7-pawn. There are ideas of Nce6 to fork the queen and the rook, as well as Nxd5 to deflect Black's only defender away from f6!} 13.Ng5 13...b5 {Black's best bet is ...b5, putting pressure on our knight. And we can implement our Nxd5 idea!} 14.Nxd5 14...Bb7 {Of course, Black won't capture on d5 and get checkmated in one move. Instead, they activate the bishop, actually threatening to capture our knight this time. But we'll be the ones to capture first, on f6.} 15.Nxf6+ 15...Nxf6 {The other knight recaptures, still keeping an eye on the h7-pawn.} 16.Bd3 {After Bd3, the position is unclear but equal according to the computer. Still, even with best play, the onus is on Black to prove compensation for the missing pawn!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Nbd7 {This move is a bit inflexible for Black because the knight can no longer go to c6 in case of a cxd5 trade. Players commit to ...Nbd7 18% of the time, rendering it the third most popular move. Let's get our bishop to b2, as planned.} 6.Bb2 6...Bd6 {Finally, Black activates the bishop to d6. There was no reason to choose the more passive ...Be7 route now. We'll activate the queen. We continue mobilizing and hold back d4 for a more opportune moment. However, we also have the powerful Bayonet strategy which is our real idea!} 7.Qc2 7...Qe7 {Another idea is ...Qe7, going for ...Ba3. Black wants to trade dark-squared bishops. In this case, this does significantly weaken our chances of getting a successful attack with Rg1 and g4. Let's just stick to solid and traditional development. We can get an advantage that way too.} 8.Be2 8...Ba3 {Black goes for the aforementioned idea. Let's capture and lure their queen to a weird square.} 9.Bxa3 9...Qxa3 {After the queen recaptures... There's no reason not to castle kingside.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Black castles as well, and the crucial aspect of the game that remains is...} 11.d4 {Central control! We've finally pushed d4 and have two powerful pawns in the center. Meanwhile, Black's bishop on c8 is completely blocked in and hopeless at the moment. White is slightly better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Nbd7 {This move is a bit inflexible for Black because the knight can no longer go to c6 in case of a cxd5 trade. Players commit to ...Nbd7 18% of the time, rendering it the third most popular move. Let's get our bishop to b2, as planned.} 6.Bb2 6...Bd6 {Finally, Black activates the bishop to d6. There was no reason to choose the more passive ...Be7 route now. We'll activate the queen. We continue mobilizing and hold back d4 for a more opportune moment. However, we also have the powerful Bayonet strategy which is our real idea!} 7.Qc2 7...O-O {The most popular move at this point is ...O-O. However, White scores well after this move, mostly because of the Bayonet with Rg1 and g4! The g-pawn is coming.} 8.Rg1 8...e5 {Meeting flank attacks with central expansion is usually a good idea. That's what Black does in this instance. Let's take in the center, as we usually do.} 9.cxd5 9...cxd5 {Once Black takes back, we reach a familiar position. Let's target the bishop on d6.} 10.Nb5 10...Bb8 {The most common retreating square is b8. Black is under a lot of pressure as we'll be able to invade via c7 and obtain the dark square bishop, when our bishop on b2 will be completely dominant. Practically this looks really tough for Black, but there is a miracle computer resource to avoid the worst (that your opponents will never find!)} 11.Rc1 11...a6 {Black can try to prevent our plan with ...a6, attacking the knight. In this instance, we'll jump to c7 anyway.} 12.Nc7 12...Bxc7 {If Black takes on c7, that's great for us. We get Black's dark-squared bishop, even at the cost of trading queens.} 13.Qxc7 13...Qxc7 {Black captures. We'll take back.} 14.Rxc7 14...Re8 {Once the rook activates to e8...} 15.g4 {Guess what; we can go for g4 even without the queens on the board! This advancement is positionally strong as well. We've got more active pieces with prospects of attacking as well as playing in the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Nbd7 {This move is a bit inflexible for Black because the knight can no longer go to c6 in case of a cxd5 trade. Players commit to ...Nbd7 18% of the time, rendering it the third most popular move. Let's get our bishop to b2, as planned.} 6.Bb2 6...Bd6 {Finally, Black activates the bishop to d6. There was no reason to choose the more passive ...Be7 route now. We'll activate the queen. We continue mobilizing and hold back d4 for a more opportune moment. However, we also have the powerful Bayonet strategy which is our real idea!} 7.Qc2 7...O-O {The most popular move at this point is ...O-O. However, White scores well after this move, mostly because of the Bayonet with Rg1 and g4! The g-pawn is coming.} 8.Rg1 8...Qe7 {The ...Qe7 idea is that Black wants to render our attack useless by trading one of our most important attackers, the dark-squared bishop. This shouldn't stop us now, having already played Rg1. We're "up a tempo" relative to the 7...Qe7 variation.} 9.g4 9...Ba3 {Black goes for the trade. Let's accept.} 10.Bxa3 10...Qxa3 {The queen arrives at a3, far away from the kingside... Where we'll attack with g5!} 11.g5 11...Nh5 {The best move is to jump to the edge of the board with ...Nh5. Let's continue targeting the knight.} 12.Be2 12...g6 {As we were threatening to move our knight and trap Black's, they defend with ...g6. Their idea is to free up the g7-square. All in all, we should always continue with our pawn storm at some point. We push h4!} 13.h4 13...Ng7 {After Black retreats to g7...} 14.d4 {We can finally play d4, with a concrete idea in mind. Playing ...g6 has severely weakened the dark squares around Black's king, which means we can reroute our knight to h6 or f6! This can be done via Nh2 and Ng4. Also, h5 is on the cards.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Nbd7 {This move is a bit inflexible for Black because the knight can no longer go to c6 in case of a cxd5 trade. Players commit to ...Nbd7 18% of the time, rendering it the third most popular move. Let's get our bishop to b2, as planned.} 6.Bb2 6...Bd6 {Finally, Black activates the bishop to d6. There was no reason to choose the more passive ...Be7 route now. We'll activate the queen. We continue mobilizing and hold back d4 for a more opportune moment. However, we also have the powerful Bayonet strategy which is our real idea!} 7.Qc2 7...O-O {The most popular move at this point is ...O-O. However, White scores well after this move, mostly because of the Bayonet with Rg1 and g4! The g-pawn is coming.} 8.Rg1 8...e5 {Meeting flank attacks with central expansion is usually a good idea. That's what Black does in this instance. Let's take in the center, as we usually do.} 9.cxd5 9...Nxd5 {Recapturing in the center doesn't surprise us at all. There's another way to attack the bishop on d6, as we've shown. And it's centralizing the knight on e4.} 10.Ne4 10...Bc7 {Once Black retreats to c7...} 11.g4 {We'll start the attack with g4! Of course, the h-pawn will join it soon, as well as the bishop on d3, together with the queen. As a defensive countermeasure, we can play a3 to prevent ...Nb4. All in all, extremely pleasant for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Nbd7 {This move is a bit inflexible for Black because the knight can no longer go to c6 in case of a cxd5 trade. Players commit to ...Nbd7 18% of the time, rendering it the third most popular move. Let's get our bishop to b2, as planned.} 6.Bb2 6...Bd6 {Finally, Black activates the bishop to d6. There was no reason to choose the more passive ...Be7 route now. We'll activate the queen. We continue mobilizing and hold back d4 for a more opportune moment. However, we also have the powerful Bayonet strategy which is our real idea!} 7.Qc2 7...O-O {The most popular move at this point is ...O-O. However, White scores well after this move, mostly because of the Bayonet with Rg1 and g4! The g-pawn is coming.} 8.Rg1 8...Re8 {The more passive ...Re8 puts us in the driver's seat. Let's initiate a pawn armada.} 9.g4 9...Nf8 {If Black abstains from ...e5, which is the best move and opts for the more passive ...Nf8, all the more fun for us. Let's kick the knight away from f6.} 10.g5 10...N6d7 {Once it jumps away, the next move is very logical.} 11.h4 {We'll support the g-pawn with h4. The attack rages on and White's got an edge here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Nbd7 {This move is a bit inflexible for Black because the knight can no longer go to c6 in case of a cxd5 trade. Players commit to ...Nbd7 18% of the time, rendering it the third most popular move. Let's get our bishop to b2, as planned.} 6.Bb2 6...Bd6 {Finally, Black activates the bishop to d6. There was no reason to choose the more passive ...Be7 route now. We'll activate the queen. We continue mobilizing and hold back d4 for a more opportune moment. However, we also have the powerful Bayonet strategy which is our real idea!} 7.Qc2 7...e5 {If at any point Black pushes ...e5, we've got a concrete counter attack. Let's capture in the center. Black can recapture both with the pawn and the knight.} 8.cxd5 8...Nxd5 {If the knight recaptures... We'll use the e4-square to attack the bishop on d6.} 9.Ne4 9...Bc7 {Once it moves back to c7, Black's position might be a bit too solid to rush with g4. Let's activate the bishop instead. The difference is we can get it to c4 now, having traded the c4-pawn. If Black goes for ...b5 instantly, we don't even mind trading on d5.} 10.Bc4 10...O-O {Black castles instead, which is a mistake! This move reignites our chances of attacking. We can immediately put pressure on the h7-pawn with the queen and the knight.} 11.Neg5 11...g6 {Black blocks with ...g6, which was basically the only move. However, another idea comes to mind now.} 12.h4 {We'll go h4! The h-pawn supports the knight and we are planning to push h5 in the near future. Meanwhile, the two bishops and the queen are extremely active along the respective diagonals. Keep in mind that if Black plays ...h6, the Ne6 sacrifice is winning immediately with the queen attacking g6.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Nbd7 {This move is a bit inflexible for Black because the knight can no longer go to c6 in case of a cxd5 trade. Players commit to ...Nbd7 18% of the time, rendering it the third most popular move. Let's get our bishop to b2, as planned.} 6.Bb2 6...Bd6 {Finally, Black activates the bishop to d6. There was no reason to choose the more passive ...Be7 route now. We'll activate the queen. We continue mobilizing and hold back d4 for a more opportune moment. However, we also have the powerful Bayonet strategy which is our real idea!} 7.Qc2 7...e5 {If at any point Black pushes ...e5, we've got a concrete counter attack. Let's capture in the center. Black can recapture both with the pawn and the knight.} 8.cxd5 8...cxd5 {If the pawn recaptures... We'll jump to b5 with the standard idea of hitting the bishop on d6.} 9.Nb5 9...Bb8 {Once it retreats, there's a very interesting move to claim advantage.} 10.Ba3 {We take control of the dark squares with Ba3! Black can't castle now, and we are threatening moves like Rc1 and Nc7+ or Nd6+ to trade off Black's dark-squared bishop. A more convincing position for White in any case.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: The Slav Defense Main Lines / Study 2: Black Plays The 5...Nbd7 and 5...Bb4 Sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/2d329302-2114-4986-aba8-638d329a6ac6/studies/78ff0d32-e416-40b4-bbd1-76354fd1e268/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black goes for ...c6. Let's develop the kingside knight first.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {The Slav defense. Once again, we'll support the c4-pawn with the bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black activates the kingside knight as well. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {Black goes for the Semi-Slav. Do you remember our approach? We'll go straight for development with Bb2. It is at this point that Black can choose between ...Bb4 and Nbd7.} 5.b3 5...Bb4 {5...Bb4 is actually the fourth most popular move, played 15% of the time. Black goes after our knight on c3 and it will be hanging if we play d4. This doesn't disturb our plan, however. Our bishop from b2 now supports the knight as well.} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {Black castles. Let's go for our usual idea.} 7.Qc2 {We'll activate the queen. Black hasn't achieved anything particular with ...Bb4 and our ideas remain the same.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 1: Black Moves The Light-Squared Bishop Early / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/ab2ff7e7-fe98-4570-982b-7eeee39cb6c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {At this point, we are already familiar with what Black wants after 1...c6. Instead of placing two pawns in the center right away, we'll play the flexible Nf3. This move can lead to multiple different lines, as you'll see. We'll allow ...d5 by Black and let them make a decision in the center.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black expands in the center... And we'll open up our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most flexible and popular 3...Nf6, occurring around 47% of times. We'll do the same. Just to mention, the second most popular move in this position, 4...e6, leads to Semi-Slav, which is not the topic of this chapter.} 4.Nc3 4...Bf5 {The third most popular move is getting the bishop out to f5, which you will probably face around 15% of the time. A tempting move but Black must always be wary of developing the bishop early in light of Qb3 ideas, as mentioned. We'll capture in the center first. It's important to weaken the d5-pawn so our Qb3 idea gains strength.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {The more logical recapture is ...cxd5. And we finally put our Qb3 plan into motion. The queen hits both b7 and d5 and it's not easy for Black to defend.} 6.Qb3 6...Nc6 {Black might think they're smart and play ...Nc6, offering the b7-pawn. This move's popularity is only 6% so you probably won't face it too often. In many openings an early capture of the b2/b7-pawns is not advised as it is a "poisoned" pawn but here it will not the case due to a precise follow-up. After capturing on b7, we will be attacking the knight.} 7.Qxb7 7...Bd7 {Black defends with ...Bd7. Their idea is probably ...Rb8. However, they haven't accounted for Nb5, threatening Nc7+! We continue crushing Black's queenside and grabbing pawns.} 8.Nb5 8...Rc8 {Now Black is forced to defend with ...Rc8. And lose another pawn on a7. Even ...Rc7 doesn't work here because of the simple Nxc6, with a counter attack on the queen.} 9.Nxa7 9...Nxa7 {So, Black takes on a7. Let's recapture. Having lost two pawns on the queenside with little compensation, Black needs to develop quickly.} 10.Qxa7 10...e6 {Which they try with ...e6. We'll do the same with b3. We've got nothing to worry about.} 11.b3 11...Bc5 {Even if Black attacks our queen, it's nowhere near trapped. We'll just slide to a6 and have got the whole diagonal to escape to if needed.} 12.Qa6 12...O-O {Black castles...} 13.Bb2 {And we'll activate the scary dark-squared bishop. Next, we'll bring the queen back to d3, get the bishop to e2 and castle our king to safety. White is winning comfortably here.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 1: Black Moves The Light-Squared Bishop Early / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/ab2ff7e7-fe98-4570-982b-7eeee39cb6c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {At this point, we are already familiar with what Black wants after 1...c6. Instead of placing two pawns in the center right away, we'll play the flexible Nf3. This move can lead to multiple different lines, as you'll see. We'll allow ...d5 by Black and let them make a decision in the center.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black expands in the center... And we'll open up our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most flexible and popular 3...Nf6, occurring around 47% of times. We'll do the same. Just to mention, the second most popular move in this position, 4...e6, leads to Semi-Slav, which is not the topic of this chapter.} 4.Nc3 4...Bg4 {However, the most popular move, ...Bg4, which is played 38% of the time, definitely is! Black pins our knight immediately. Black's early development of the c8-bishop is often premature due to the weakness on the queenside. Instead of going for the immediate Qb3, we'll capture in the center first, sticking with the formula we'll use against ...Bf5 as well. 5. Qb3 is a logical idea but Black can play 5...Qb6 to safeguard the queenside for the moment.} 5.cxd5 5...Bxf3 {Black's most solid response is to quickly trade the bishop off and utilize the pawns to control the light squares. They are also trying to deflect our queen away from the b3 square. We'll recapture with the queen so as not to double our f-pawns.} 6.Qxf3 6...cxd5 {Now Black recaptures with the pawn, covering the e4-square. The most natural move probably comes to mind, as we shouldn't let Black play ...e5. We'll expand in the center ourselves, with d4!} 7.d4 7...Nc6 {As the c6-square is free, Black develops the knight there. This is a refreshing alternative to the usual ...Nbd7. Let's develop our dark-squared bishop. A nice aggressive alternative I like here is 8. g4!? intending to gain space on the kingside. Following with g5 and h4 would be natural.} 8.Bd2 8...e6 {After Black creates a pawn chain in the center... We can calmly continue development with the advantage of the powerful light-squared bishop.} 9.Bd3 9...Be7 {Black activates the bishop to e7, preparing castling. Let's castle ourselves.} 10.O-O 10...O-O {Black does the same. How should we continue the game now? We are at a crossroads of ideas and Qh3 is the more aggressive option. A good alternative was also Rac1 to prepare play on the queenside. Our position is flexible enough to allow either option and a serious idea is a timely e3-e4 push to open up our bishops.} 11.Qh3 11...Rc8 {In this case, Black is going to be the one to capture the c-file first. Instead of e4, there is another aggressive idea we could go for. It's f4, grabbing more space on the kingside. And there's more to come.} 12.f4 12...g6 {Black could play something like ...g6 to prevent f5.} 13.g4 {And we are all in! Creating a pawn storm on the kingside, we use our queen's early development to start an attack. This line follows the game played between Ferenc Berkes and Ivan Salgado Lopez in 2011, which White won.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 1: Black Moves The Light-Squared Bishop Early / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/ab2ff7e7-fe98-4570-982b-7eeee39cb6c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {At this point, we are already familiar with what Black wants after 1...c6. Instead of placing two pawns in the center right away, we'll play the flexible Nf3. This move can lead to multiple different lines, as you'll see. We'll allow ...d5 by Black and let them make a decision in the center.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black expands in the center... And we'll open up our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most flexible and popular 3...Nf6, occurring around 47% of times. We'll do the same. Just to mention, the second most popular move in this position, 4...e6, leads to Semi-Slav, which is not the topic of this chapter.} 4.Nc3 4...Bf5 {The third most popular move is getting the bishop out to f5, which you will probably face around 15% of the time. A tempting move but Black must always be wary of developing the bishop early in light of Qb3 ideas, as mentioned. We'll capture in the center first. It's important to weaken the d5-pawn so our Qb3 idea gains strength.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {The more logical recapture is ...cxd5. And we finally put our Qb3 plan into motion. The queen hits both b7 and d5 and it's not easy for Black to defend.} 6.Qb3 6...Bc8 {This sad retreat is Black's best way to defend the queenside. Let's strike in the center, as we're allowed to. We're not winning by force but our opponents going ...Bf5-...Bc8 in the opening can only be in our favor!} 7.d4 {We'll continue by playing in the center. This variation transposes to the ...Bf5 line.} 7...Nc6 {If Black develops to c6, let's unleash an interesting idea that takes advantage of their tempo losses. The most aggressive approach as we go for a central Stonewall formation! Black should be punished for their slow opening play. Developing with 8.Bd3 and O-O followed by the e4 pawn break was another logical plan.} 8.Ne5 8...e6 {Black defends the center...} 9.f4 {And we'll grab more space with the Stonewall. Next, our bishop will fill the light squares and we'll castle short. It's fascinating how much space you can get and how much you can transform a position if the other side plays carelessly for just one move!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 1: Black Moves The Light-Squared Bishop Early / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/ab2ff7e7-fe98-4570-982b-7eeee39cb6c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {At this point, we are already familiar with what Black wants after 1...c6. Instead of placing two pawns in the center right away, we'll play the flexible Nf3. This move can lead to multiple different lines, as you'll see. We'll allow ...d5 by Black and let them make a decision in the center.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black expands in the center... And we'll open up our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most flexible and popular 3...Nf6, occurring around 47% of times. We'll do the same. Just to mention, the second most popular move in this position, 4...e6, leads to Semi-Slav, which is not the topic of this chapter.} 4.Nc3 4...Bf5 {The third most popular move is getting the bishop out to f5, which you will probably face around 15% of the time. A tempting move but Black must always be wary of developing the bishop early in light of Qb3 ideas, as mentioned. We'll capture in the center first. It's important to weaken the d5-pawn so our Qb3 idea gains strength.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {The more logical recapture is ...cxd5. And we finally put our Qb3 plan into motion. The queen hits both b7 and d5 and it's not easy for Black to defend.} 6.Qb3 6...b6 {Another big mistake Black players pick 16% of the time. There's a forced winning line that's useful to remember. After our move of Bb5+, Black can't block with ...Nc6 anymore!} 7.Bb5+ 7...Nbd7 {So, they have to settle for ...Nbd7. Do you see a hanging pawn now?} 8.Nxd5 {We are simply winning, a pawn up.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 1: Black Moves The Light-Squared Bishop Early / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/ab2ff7e7-fe98-4570-982b-7eeee39cb6c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {At this point, we are already familiar with what Black wants after 1...c6. Instead of placing two pawns in the center right away, we'll play the flexible Nf3. This move can lead to multiple different lines, as you'll see. We'll allow ...d5 by Black and let them make a decision in the center.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black expands in the center... And we'll open up our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most flexible and popular 3...Nf6, occurring around 47% of times. We'll do the same. Just to mention, the second most popular move in this position, 4...e6, leads to Semi-Slav, which is not the topic of this chapter.} 4.Nc3 4...Bf5 {The third most popular move is getting the bishop out to f5, which you will probably face around 15% of the time. A tempting move but Black must always be wary of developing the bishop early in light of Qb3 ideas, as mentioned. We'll capture in the center first. It's important to weaken the d5-pawn so our Qb3 idea gains strength.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {The more logical recapture is ...cxd5. And we finally put our Qb3 plan into motion. The queen hits both b7 and d5 and it's not easy for Black to defend.} 6.Qb3 6...Qb6 {The most popular move in this position is 6...Qb6, which is a mistake! Believe it or not, you'll face this move almost a quarter of the time.} 7.Nxd5 {Black simply hangs the pawn on d5. If ...Qxb3, we'll capture on f6 with check, and then recapture the queen.} *

