1.c4 1...c6 {At this point, we are already familiar with what Black wants after 1...c6. Instead of placing two pawns in the center right away, we'll play the flexible Nf3. This move can lead to multiple different lines, as you'll see. We'll allow ...d5 by Black and let them make a decision in the center.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black expands in the center... And we'll open up our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most flexible and popular 3...Nf6, occurring around 47% of times. We'll do the same. Just to mention, the second most popular move in this position, 4...e6, leads to Semi-Slav, which is not the topic of this chapter.} 4.Nc3 4...Bf5 {The third most popular move is getting the bishop out to f5, which you will probably face around 15% of the time. A tempting move but Black must always be wary of developing the bishop early in light of Qb3 ideas, as mentioned. We'll capture in the center first. It's important to weaken the d5-pawn so our Qb3 idea gains strength.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {We're happy to see this recapture as we get a strong center for free. We'll continue with the logical d4.} 6.d4 6...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop...} 7.Bd3 {And we'll get our own bishop out. The game is far from over but we have the edge due to free flowing development and greater central control.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 1: Black Moves The Light-Squared Bishop Early / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/ab2ff7e7-fe98-4570-982b-7eeee39cb6c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {At this point, we are already familiar with what Black wants after 1...c6. Instead of placing two pawns in the center right away, we'll play the flexible Nf3. This move can lead to multiple different lines, as you'll see. We'll allow ...d5 by Black and let them make a decision in the center.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black expands in the center... And we'll open up our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most flexible and popular 3...Nf6, occurring around 47% of times. We'll do the same. Just to mention, the second most popular move in this position, 4...e6, leads to Semi-Slav, which is not the topic of this chapter.} 4.Nc3 4...Bg4 {However, the most popular move, ...Bg4, which is played 38% of the time, definitely is! Black pins our knight immediately. Black's early development of the c8-bishop is often premature due to the weakness on the queenside. Instead of going for the immediate Qb3, we'll capture in the center first, sticking with the formula we'll use against ...Bf5 as well. 5. Qb3 is a logical idea but Black can play 5...Qb6 to safeguard the queenside for the moment.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {Black could recapture in the center with the pawn. That invites our common Qb3 idea. In this case, the queen attacks both the pawn on d5 and the pawn on b7. That means it's not convenient for Black to move the queen away from the defense of d5.} 6.Qb3 6...Bc8 {This sad retreat is Black's best way to defend the queenside. Let's strike in the center, as we're allowed to. We're not winning by force but our opponents going ...Bf5-...Bc8 in the opening can only be in our favor!} 7.d4 {We'll continue by playing in the center. This variation transposes to the ...Bf5 line.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 1: Black Moves The Light-Squared Bishop Early / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/ab2ff7e7-fe98-4570-982b-7eeee39cb6c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {At this point, we are already familiar with what Black wants after 1...c6. Instead of placing two pawns in the center right away, we'll play the flexible Nf3. This move can lead to multiple different lines, as you'll see. We'll allow ...d5 by Black and let them make a decision in the center.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black expands in the center... And we'll open up our light-squared bishop.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most flexible and popular 3...Nf6, occurring around 47% of times. We'll do the same. Just to mention, the second most popular move in this position, 4...e6, leads to Semi-Slav, which is not the topic of this chapter.} 4.Nc3 4...Bg4 {However, the most popular move, ...Bg4, which is played 38% of the time, definitely is! Black pins our knight immediately. Black's early development of the c8-bishop is often premature due to the weakness on the queenside. Instead of going for the immediate Qb3, we'll capture in the center first, sticking with the formula we'll use against ...Bf5 as well. 5. Qb3 is a logical idea but Black can play 5...Qb6 to safeguard the queenside for the moment.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {Recapturing with the knight is definitely not the best move for Black. The reason being that there is no pawn on d5 to prevent e4! We'll play d4 first.} 6.d4 6...e6 {If Black tries to strengthen the knight in the center... We can actually kick it out with e4!} 7.e4 7...Nxc3 {Black's best choice is to take on c3.} 8.bxc3 {And we'll recapture with a massive center. Having both pawns on e4 and d4 is always beneficial!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 2: The Chameleon Slav and Others / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/e9864165-347c-4a69-866a-30396955ae9d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...Nbd7 {This move is pretty rare (only 4%) but tricky as Black wants to push ...e5. If we play the natural d4 then Black tricks us with ...e6 and a Semi-Slav! The simplest answer against this rare idea is to take on d5, with a slightly better symmetrical position. 5. Qc2 is also in line with our repertoire when Black has the unique idea of a rapid 5...e5 6. cxd5 Nxd5! with an interesting reverse Sicilian type game.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {Black takes back in the center. Our next move should be very logical to you. We'll play d4, leaving the black minor pieces somewhat cramped.} 6.d4 6...e6 {But they have to keep developing, so they do so with ...e6. We'll get the light-squared bishop out to d3, as there is no reason to develop it to e2. Black can't really play ...Bg4 with their bishop being stuck on c8.} 7.Bd3 7...Bd6 {Black does the same and gets ready to castle. We'll castle first.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {But Black follows us right away.} 9.Bd2 {At last, all our minor pieces are developed and our king is castled. Black has got a passive knight on d7 and a "dead" bishop on c8. A direct positional idea to start the middlegame would be Rc1, getting the rook to the open c-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 2: The Chameleon Slav and Others / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/e9864165-347c-4a69-866a-30396955ae9d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...a6 {4...a6 is only played 4% of the time in the Lichess database, but is very instructive. Also, that's still over 10,000 games. It is called the Chameleon or the Chebanenko Slav. 4..a6 is a generally useful move to control the b5-square and Black is also waiting for some intel on our setup before deciding further development. We also adopt a waiting strategy! With h3, we make it harder for Black to develop the bishop from c8. Having placed the ball back in Black's court, they've got a lot of options here.} 5.h3 5...Nbd7 {As 5...g6, 5...Nbd7 is similar to 4...Nbd7. It comes in handy not to get confused if Black players go for different move-orders, and they'll choose this one only 4% of the time. The core ideas usually stay the same for us. Let's take in the center.} 6.cxd5 6...cxd5 {After Black recaptures, how will we gain space?} 7.d4 {Of course, d4. Here, we don't have to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and it's perfectly fine to get it out to d2 in our setup.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 2: The Chameleon Slav and Others / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/e9864165-347c-4a69-866a-30396955ae9d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...g6 {4...g6, aiming to fianchetto, is a solid system for Black which is a cross between a Grunfeld and a Slav. It's played about one tenth of the time, however. To be honest there is nothing wrong with 5. d4, transposing to mainline theory, and objectively it is even the best move. However if we want to keep things a bit more offbeat in our English flavor, the queenside fianchetto is a good option as well!} 5.b3 5...Bg7 {Black activates the bishop... And so do we. There's a lot of tension on the a1-h8 diagonal now!} 6.Bb2 6...O-O {Having developed the kingside, Black quickly castles. Very solid indeed. We'll develop the light-squared bishop now as well. As Black probably wants to play ...Bg4 anyway, it's best to get it out to e2.} 7.Be2 7...Bg4 {Now our knight isn't even pinned once Black goes for ...Bg4. We'll simply get the king to safety.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {Black will most probably activate the knight to d7 here, with the idea of ...e5. What's the simplest way of stopping that?} 9.d4 {You probably guessed it: d4. Admittedly Black is very solid, but so are we. The difference is that we have a bit more space on a crowded board so we effectively have a free slight advantage and a full game of chess lies ahead. We could try to get the rooks to the center and play for e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 2: The Chameleon Slav and Others / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/e9864165-347c-4a69-866a-30396955ae9d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...a6 {4...a6 is only played 4% of the time in the Lichess database, but is very instructive. Also, that's still over 10,000 games. It is called the Chameleon or the Chebanenko Slav. 4..a6 is a generally useful move to control the b5-square and Black is also waiting for some intel on our setup before deciding further development. We also adopt a waiting strategy! With h3, we make it harder for Black to develop the bishop from c8. Having placed the ball back in Black's court, they've got a lot of options here.} 5.h3 5...Bf5 {A move that's played a whopping 47% of the time is ...Bf5, settling with the f5-square since ...Bg4 is impossible. We've got an interesting idea to uncork here. Let's play g4 immediately! We don't mind weakening our kingside that much, as we'll place a bishop on g2 anyway.} 6.g4 6...Bg6 {Black retreats to g6, which is the most logical move. We'll bring our queen out and attack the soft spot on b7.} 7.Qb3 7...b5 {Once Black defends with ...b5, it should be clear which pawn we want to capture. The one in the center. After all, in chess, the center must be contested at some point!} 8.cxd5 8...Nxd5 {Black is basically forced to take back with the knight here.} 9.Ne5 {And as the last move of this line, we'll centralize the knight and attack the bishop on g6. Of course, Bg2 is in the cards to unleash the strong bishop. An enjoyable position for White.} *

