1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...a6 {4...a6 is only played 4% of the time in the Lichess database, but is very instructive. Also, that's still over 10,000 games. It is called the Chameleon or the Chebanenko Slav. 4..a6 is a generally useful move to control the b5-square and Black is also waiting for some intel on our setup before deciding further development. We also adopt a waiting strategy! With h3, we make it harder for Black to develop the bishop from c8. Having placed the ball back in Black's court, they've got a lot of options here.} 5.h3 5...Bf5 {A move that's played a whopping 47% of the time is ...Bf5, settling with the f5-square since ...Bg4 is impossible. We've got an interesting idea to uncork here. Let's play g4 immediately! We don't mind weakening our kingside that much, as we'll place a bishop on g2 anyway.} 6.g4 6...Bg6 {Black retreats to g6, which is the most logical move. We'll bring our queen out and attack the soft spot on b7.} 7.Qb3 7...Qc7 {The best move here is actually ...Qc7. This move only works with sufficient engine preparation, however. On the surface it seems like Black hangs the d5-pawn... Let's capture it!} 8.cxd5 8...Nxd5 {If Black recaptures with the knight, it's actually not the best for White to capture and win the pawn on d5.} 9.Nh4 {Instead, we'll go for the bishop on g6. If we get the active bishop, we'll easily expand in the center with d4 and strengthen our kingside with Bg2. All in all, White's got a more comfortable position again.} *

