1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...a6 {4...a6 is only played 4% of the time in the Lichess database, but is very instructive. Also, that's still over 10,000 games. It is called the Chameleon or the Chebanenko Slav. 4..a6 is a generally useful move to control the b5-square and Black is also waiting for some intel on our setup before deciding further development. We also adopt a waiting strategy! With h3, we make it harder for Black to develop the bishop from c8. Having placed the ball back in Black's court, they've got a lot of options here.} 5.h3 5...e6 {The third most popular move is ...e6, played 13% of the time. This move does solidify the center. However, I don't think Black would be too excited to block the light-squared bishop after playing 4...a6. As per usual, we'll go for the dark-squared bishop fianchetto.} 6.b3 6...Bd6 {Black develops the bishop to the most active square, d6. If allowed, they'd love to play ...e5. Therefore we'll play d4. We can't go for our Bayonet approach here since Black is threatening to quickly push ..e5 and the a6-pawn prevents our Nb5 jumps.} 7.d4 7...O-O {After Black castles...} 8.Bd3 {We'll get the bishop out to d3. We'll play Bb2 and eventually try to achieve what Black couldn't: play e4! This push would give us the central space we need to reach an advantageous position. Still, White is slightly better even so.} *

