1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...a6 {4...a6 is only played 4% of the time in the Lichess database, but is very instructive. Also, that's still over 10,000 games. It is called the Chameleon or the Chebanenko Slav. 4..a6 is a generally useful move to control the b5-square and Black is also waiting for some intel on our setup before deciding further development. We also adopt a waiting strategy! With h3, we make it harder for Black to develop the bishop from c8. Having placed the ball back in Black's court, they've got a lot of options here.} 5.h3 5...b5 {The immediate ...b5 is actually the second most popular move here, being chosen 16% of the time. Our c4-pawn is under a lot of pressure and we need to make a decision. Capturing on b5 would give Black the center, so we'll take the other pawn! However, 6. b3 was very logical and easy to play but I also have a interesting idea to suggest.} 6.cxd5 6...cxd5 {After Black recaptures on d5, here's our plan.} 7.b4 {We'll go for b4! This move stops Black's advances on the queenside and freezes their a- and b-pawns. Very soon, we'll play a4 and take advantage of them over-extending.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 2: The Chameleon Slav and Others / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/e9864165-347c-4a69-866a-30396955ae9d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...a6 {4...a6 is only played 4% of the time in the Lichess database, but is very instructive. Also, that's still over 10,000 games. It is called the Chameleon or the Chebanenko Slav. 4..a6 is a generally useful move to control the b5-square and Black is also waiting for some intel on our setup before deciding further development. We also adopt a waiting strategy! With h3, we make it harder for Black to develop the bishop from c8. Having placed the ball back in Black's court, they've got a lot of options here.} 5.h3 5...dxc4 {Taking on c4 gives us exactly what we want. You should also face this move only about 4% of the time. Let's recapture with the bishop.} 6.Bxc4 6...b5 {This does walk right into ...b5, but that's okay. We'll keep our bishop on the strong and active diagonal.} 7.Bb3 {After Bb3, we've got a direct plan in the center: go for both d4 and e4! This position is similar to a Queen's Gambit Declined position where we will quickly put our pawns in the center. Our approach is extremely active.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 2: The Chameleon Slav and Others / Variation 11"]
