1.c4 1...c6 {Black "almost mirrors" our first move. Let's get the knight out and prepare to meet ...d5 with e3.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll play our 3. e3 move to be consistent with our repertoire, and support the c4-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black plays the most popular move here, which is ...Nf6. Black players opt for it almost half the time, with 48%. There's definitely nothing wrong with this traditional developing move that supports the center. We'll continue in the same manner with Nc3. From here on, Black has multiple approaches to the position.} 4.Nc3 4...dxc4 {Capturing on c4 isn't the way to go and is only played 3% of the time. We are allowed quick development and an extra pawn in the center.} 5.Bxc4 {We'll just take back and activate the bishop. Next, we'll go d4 and O-O, unbothered.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 3: Stonewall and Other Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/3d820afa-339f-4abc-97f0-0a74b863d9f8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black will play ...c6 to prepare ...d5, as per usual. We'll get the knight out with the idea of e3 to protect c4.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And Black should play ...d5, after all. Let's defend the pawn with e3, as we can't play e4. Now our light-squared bishop is ready to get into the action.} 3.e3 3...e6 {3...e6 is the third most popular move in this position, with a popularity of 13%. Black might be going for a Stonewall structure! The Stonewall is a popular and aggressive option but not without flaws - principally the bad bishop on c8 and potential dark square weaknesses. Our task will be to exploit these drawbacks, starting with b3 to play Bb2 or Ba3.} 4.b3 4...f5 {Black expands with ...f5 now. This move signifies the Stonewall. Their idea will be to get the knight to f6 and often to e4 at some point. We'll simply continue developing and prepare castling.} 5.Be2 5...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6 now, a standard developing move on the kingside. Let's get our king to safety.} 6.O-O 6...Bd6 {Having placed the pawns on the light squares, it's logical for Black to make the dark-squared bishop as active as possible. That's why we'll try to trade it immediately! Trading off the bishops is highly desirable for us as we can invade the central e5 square after.} 7.Ba3 7...Bxa3 {Black could also capture on a3. We'll take with the knight, of course...} 8.Nxa3 8...Qe7 {Black's queen develops with tempo. We'll guard the knight with our queen.} 9.Qc1 9...e5 {The drawback of our plan to exchange bishops is that Black can occupy the center. Let's trade on d5.} 10.cxd5 10...Nxd5 {Black will probably recapture with the knight. To untangle our pieces, we'll have to move the knight first. Although it looks passive, I suggest retreating to b1.} 11.Nb1 11...O-O {Once Black castles, we can throw in a pin. We'll bring our bishop to c4.} 12.Bc4 12...Kh8 {The most logical is for Black to slide the king to h8. And we'll actually trade on d5 as well.} 13.Bxd5 13...cxd5 {After Black captures, here is our point. We'll try to trade the queens, just as we've tried to trade the bishops! The point is to weaken the dark squares yet again.} 14.Qa3 14...Qxa3 {Black doesn't have much of a better move than to capture at this point. We'll recapture. Now the e5-square is vulnerable and Black has got some loose pawns in the center, although they control it.} 15.Nxa3 15...Nc6 {Having allowed Black to grab central space, it's time to fight back now. We'll claim space with d4, attacking the e5-pawn.} 16.d4 16...exd4 {Pushing it forward would only allow Ne5. Instead, Black trades. We'll take back with the knight, as this trade suits us as well.} 17.Nxd4 17...Nxd4 {Black captures.} 18.exd4 {At the end of the day, we've traded Black's most important pieces and successfully fought against their center control. Next, we'll get the rooks to the open files and try to reroute the knight to either f3 or d3. It's extremely important that we have a clamp on the e5-square and it allows us to potentially play for an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 3: Stonewall and Other Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/3d820afa-339f-4abc-97f0-0a74b863d9f8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black will play ...c6 to prepare ...d5, as per usual. We'll get the knight out with the idea of e3 to protect c4.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And Black should play ...d5, after all. Let's defend the pawn with e3, as we can't play e4. Now our light-squared bishop is ready to get into the action.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {A common amateur mistake Black can make in these setups is a premature development of the bishop to either f5 or g4. This move, although an inaccuracy, is played 15% of the time by players rating from 1000-2000 ELO. Our reply of Qb3 is an important move to remember, unpinning and attacking b7.} 4.Qb3 4...Qb6 {...Qb6, offering a trade of queens, is played here 29% of the time. ...Bxf3, a common alternative, fails to the simple Qxb7. Here, capturing allows us to maintain an advantage. } 5.Qxb6 5...axb6 {Black recaptures on b6. Now we'll take in the center as well.} 6.cxd5 6...cxd5 {These two trades have allowed us to isolate Black's doubled pawns on the b-file, which is an enormous positional advantage for us. Now, with Ne5, we get an improving move with tempo as well. Black never got to capture our knight!} 7.Ne5 7...Bd7 {To keep the bishop safe, they retreat to b7. How should we continue development? The most straightforward move is Nc3, attacking the d5-pawn.} 8.Nc3 8...e6 {After ...e6, Black's bishop is stuck! Let's plan the fianchetto of our c1-bishop, with b3. It's more logical to develop it this way, having played e3.} 9.b3 9...Nc6 {Black offers a trade of knights now. If we capture on c6, we'll give them a chance to undouble their b-pawns! Therefore, instead of retreating, we'll take on d7.} 10.Nxd7 10...Kxd7 {Although we traded our active knight for Black's passive bishop, we've made them lose castling rights. Now we'll attack the weak b6-pawn, threatening a fork as well.} 11.Na4 11...Kc7 {So, basically the only move Black has to defend is ...Kc7.} 12.Bb2 {And finally, we get the bishop out to b2. Who would you rather have in this position, White or Black?} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 3: Stonewall and Other Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/3d820afa-339f-4abc-97f0-0a74b863d9f8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black will play ...c6 to prepare ...d5, as per usual. We'll get the knight out with the idea of e3 to protect c4.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And Black should play ...d5, after all. Let's defend the pawn with e3, as we can't play e4. Now our light-squared bishop is ready to get into the action.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {A common amateur mistake Black can make in these setups is a premature development of the bishop to either f5 or g4. This move, although an inaccuracy, is played 15% of the time by players rating from 1000-2000 ELO. Our reply of Qb3 is an important move to remember, unpinning and attacking b7.} 4.Qb3 4...Qb6 {...Qb6, offering a trade of queens, is played here 29% of the time. ...Bxf3, a common alternative, fails to the simple Qxb7. Here, capturing allows us to maintain an advantage. } 5.Qxb6 5...axb6 {Black recaptures on b6. Now we'll take in the center as well.} 6.cxd5 6...Bxf3 {Capturing on f3 is possible here for Black and played 40% of the time, so it's important to cover. We'll take back, of course. Our f-pawns are doubled...} 7.gxf3 7...cxd5 {But not isolated like Black's b-pawns.} 8.b3 {I recommend b3 here. The idea is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, activate the knight and castle long. We've got a position that's easy to play now.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 3: Stonewall and Other Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/3d820afa-339f-4abc-97f0-0a74b863d9f8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black will play ...c6 to prepare ...d5, as per usual. We'll get the knight out with the idea of e3 to protect c4.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And Black should play ...d5, after all. Let's defend the pawn with e3, as we can't play e4. Now our light-squared bishop is ready to get into the action.} 3.e3 3...Bg4 {A common amateur mistake Black can make in these setups is a premature development of the bishop to either f5 or g4. This move, although an inaccuracy, is played 15% of the time by players rating from 1000-2000 ELO. Our reply of Qb3 is an important move to remember, unpinning and attacking b7.} 4.Qb3 4...Qc7 {Another move to defend the b7-pawn is ...Qc7. It's also convenient for Black to place the queen on the c-file and target the bishop on c1. Therefore we'll get our knight out to c3 quickly. Now we are definitely threatening to capture in the center.} 5.Nc3 5...e6 {And Black is forced to defend with ...e6, still not capturing our knight on f3. We offer the knight on e5, but Black mustn't capture it because of the attack on b7.} 6.Ne5 6...Bf5 {So, they'll probably retreat to f5. Let's continue attacking the bishop and prove why the early ...Bg4 wasn't optimal for Black. We'll take the opportunity to play g4 with tempo.} 7.g4 7...Bg6 {Black retreats to g6... And we'll capture on d5, finally.} 8.cxd5 8...exd5 {We captured now because after ...exd5 Black can't retreat the bishop on the c8-h3 diagonal anymore.} 9.h4 {And we can continue hunting it with h4. The idea is, of course, h5. We've got a lot of space and activity here with a slight advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 3: Stonewall and Other Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/3d820afa-339f-4abc-97f0-0a74b863d9f8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black will play ...c6 to prepare ...d5, as per usual. We'll get the knight out with the idea of e3 to protect c4.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And Black should play ...d5, after all. Let's defend the pawn with e3, as we can't play e4. Now our light-squared bishop is ready to get into the action.} 3.e3 3...e6 {3...e6 is the third most popular move in this position, with a popularity of 13%. Black might be going for a Stonewall structure! The Stonewall is a popular and aggressive option but not without flaws - principally the bad bishop on c8 and potential dark square weaknesses. Our task will be to exploit these drawbacks, starting with b3 to play Bb2 or Ba3.} 4.b3 4...f5 {Black expands with ...f5 now. This move signifies the Stonewall. Their idea will be to get the knight to f6 and often to e4 at some point. We'll simply continue developing and prepare castling.} 5.Be2 5...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6 now, a standard developing move on the kingside. Let's get our king to safety.} 6.O-O 6...Bd6 {Having placed the pawns on the light squares, it's logical for Black to make the dark-squared bishop as active as possible. That's why we'll try to trade it immediately! Trading off the bishops is highly desirable for us as we can invade the central e5 square after.} 7.Ba3 7...O-O {Castling only avoids the trade for one move. Now we finally get a pawn to d4! Now Black won't be able to push ...e5 and their bishop will remain stuck for a while.} 8.d4 8...Bxa3 {Black trades.} 9.Nxa3 {After recapturing, we've got strong control of e5 once again. After we get the a3-knight to d3, our position will be comfortable to play.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: The Slav Defense Sidelines / Study 3: Stonewall and Other Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/195b79fb-27d0-4316-9882-fa5835bb07a3/studies/3d820afa-339f-4abc-97f0-0a74b863d9f8/lines"]

1.c4 1...c6 {Black will play ...c6 to prepare ...d5, as per usual. We'll get the knight out with the idea of e3 to protect c4.} 2.Nf3 2...d5 {And Black should play ...d5, after all. Let's defend the pawn with e3, as we can't play e4. Now our light-squared bishop is ready to get into the action.} 3.e3 3...dxc4 {Black can capture on c4 at various points, perhaps mistakenly thinking they are in a Queen's Gambit Accepted position. This move is played only 9% of the time in this position, however. We'll just recapture on c4. Our kingside is already developed.} 4.Bxc4 4...Nf6 {Black might go for the standard ...Nf6...} 5.d4 {Which lets us play d4 with great control of the center, too. You've got nothing to worry about in this position with a slight advantage already in the first five moves.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 1: Black's Main Setup (5...Bg7) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/eeb3e06b-5459-40cb-9229-9aebd2d7f8b3/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black begins with the knight development. We'll bring the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {And here comes the move we are interested in. The early d5-push by Black immediately establishes a pawn in the center. We'll trade, otherwise ...d4 could come and Black will gain the desired central space!} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll advance with h4. The idea is to push h5 and attack the pawn structure.} 5.h4 5...Bg7 {Black ignores our attack and develops the bishop to g7. We continue with our h-file assault, keeping the options for both hxg6 and h6 to cramp Black further!} 6.h5 6...c5 {With 6...c5, which happens in 11% of the games, Black tries to get more central control. We can, however, clamp them on the kingside now with h6!} 7.h6 7...Bf6 {The bishop escapes. But we'll expand in the center with a tempo.} 8.e4 8...Nxc3 {Black doesn't want to be passive, so they trade. We'll recapture with the d-pawn! The endgame is very pleasant with Black's pawn on c5 being weak and the bishop on f6 being short of squares.} 9.dxc3 9...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens. We recapture.} 10.Kxd1 10...Nc6 {Black continues with their development.} 11.Kc2 {Our king finds a safe square on c2. The plan is to expand on the queenside and develop the bishop. The c3-pawn nicely restricts Black's knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 1: Black's Main Setup (5...Bg7) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/eeb3e06b-5459-40cb-9229-9aebd2d7f8b3/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black begins with the knight development. We'll bring the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {And here comes the move we are interested in. The early d5-push by Black immediately establishes a pawn in the center. We'll trade, otherwise ...d4 could come and Black will gain the desired central space!} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll advance with h4. The idea is to push h5 and attack the pawn structure.} 5.h4 5...Bg7 {Black ignores our attack and develops the bishop to g7. We continue with our h-file assault, keeping the options for both hxg6 and h6 to cramp Black further!} 6.h5 6...Nc6 {Continuing development here happens in 10% of the games. It is best for Black: they can even castle queenside in some cases. We'll push in the center here. Another promising path is 7. g3.} 7.d4 7...Bf5 {Black is fighting against our e4 push. But, with Rh4, we have an awesome rook lift to prepare e4! This is a deep concept as it involves an exchange sacrifice.} 8.Rh4 8...Bf6 {Black attacks our rook. We don't care! We ignore the threat of material loss and expand on the kingside instead.} 9.g4 9...Bxh4 {Black accepts the sacrifice. We'll go with the capture on f5.} 10.gxf5 10...e6 {The idea is to solidify the pawn structure on the kingside and protect the bishop on h4. White should trade on e6 and isolate the pawn.} 11.fxe6 11...fxe6 {Black recaptures. We develop the bishop to h3 and put pressure on the e6-pawn.} 12.Bh3 12...Qe7 {The queen defends it. We will trade off Black's active knight on d5.} 13.Nxd5 13...exd5 {Black recaptures. With our next move of Bh6, the bishop is nicely eyeing the weak kingside dark squares.} 14.Bh6 14...Kf7 {Black cannot castle queenside, so they get their king out of the center via f7.} 15.e3 {And we solidify the center. The position is complex but despite Black's extra exchange White has full compensation due to the bishop pair and general piece activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 1: Black's Main Setup (5...Bg7) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/eeb3e06b-5459-40cb-9229-9aebd2d7f8b3/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black begins with the knight development. We'll bring the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {And here comes the move we are interested in. The early d5-push by Black immediately establishes a pawn in the center. We'll trade, otherwise ...d4 could come and Black will gain the desired central space!} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll advance with h4. The idea is to push h5 and attack the pawn structure.} 5.h4 5...Bg7 {Black ignores our attack and develops the bishop to g7. We continue with our h-file assault, keeping the options for both hxg6 and h6 to cramp Black further!} 6.h5 6...Nc6 {Continuing development here happens in 10% of the games. It is best for Black: they can even castle queenside in some cases. We'll push in the center here. Another promising path is 7. g3.} 7.d4 7...Bf5 {Black is fighting against our e4 push. But, with Rh4, we have an awesome rook lift to prepare e4! This is a deep concept as it involves an exchange sacrifice.} 8.Rh4 8...Bf6 {Black attacks our rook. We don't care! We ignore the threat of material loss and expand on the kingside instead.} 9.g4 9...Bxh4 {Black accepts the sacrifice. We'll go with the capture on f5.} 10.gxf5 10...Bf6 {Black retreats the bishop. We'll expand in the center with a tempo.} 11.e4 11...Nxc3 {Black trades. We'll recapture.} 12.bxc3 12...gxf5 {Black wants to isolate our f5-pawn if we recapture... ...so we expand in the center instead!} 13.e5 13...Bxe5 {Black must give up the bishop, otherwise their position will collapse with our advancing pawns coming. We'll recapture with the pawn.} 14.dxe5 14...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens.} 15.Kxd1 {And the two bishop endgame with a pawn up gives White a lot of winning chances. Check out the game between GM Anish Giri and GM Peter Svidler in the chess24.com INT 2020.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 1: Black's Main Setup (5...Bg7) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/eeb3e06b-5459-40cb-9229-9aebd2d7f8b3/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black begins with the knight development. We'll bring the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {And here comes the move we are interested in. The early d5-push by Black immediately establishes a pawn in the center. We'll trade, otherwise ...d4 could come and Black will gain the desired central space!} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll advance with h4. The idea is to push h5 and attack the pawn structure.} 5.h4 5...Bg7 {Black ignores our attack and develops the bishop to g7. We continue with our h-file assault, keeping the options for both hxg6 and h6 to cramp Black further!} 6.h5 6...Nc6 {Continuing development here happens in 10% of the games. It is best for Black: they can even castle queenside in some cases. We'll push in the center here. Another promising path is 7. g3.} 7.d4 7...Bf5 {Black is fighting against our e4 push. But, with Rh4, we have an awesome rook lift to prepare e4! This is a deep concept as it involves an exchange sacrifice.} 8.Rh4 8...Bf6 {Black attacks our rook. We don't care! We ignore the threat of material loss and expand on the kingside instead.} 9.g4 9...Bxh4 {Black accepts the sacrifice. We'll go with the capture on f5.} 10.gxf5 10...Bf6 {Black retreats the bishop. We'll expand in the center with a tempo.} 11.e4 11...Nxc3 {Black trades. We'll recapture.} 12.bxc3 12...gxf5 {Black wants to isolate our f5-pawn if we recapture... ...so we expand in the center instead!} 13.e5 13...Bg7 {If Black retreats... ... the move h6 will come next! Attacking the bishop yet again.} 14.h6 14...Bf8 {The only free square. We'll expand even further!} 15.d5 15...Nb8 {The knight retreats to safety.} 16.Ng5 {This position is just crushing, with e6 and Qh5 coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 1: Black's Main Setup (5...Bg7) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/eeb3e06b-5459-40cb-9229-9aebd2d7f8b3/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black begins with the knight development. We'll bring the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {And here comes the move we are interested in. The early d5-push by Black immediately establishes a pawn in the center. We'll trade, otherwise ...d4 could come and Black will gain the desired central space!} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll advance with h4. The idea is to push h5 and attack the pawn structure.} 5.h4 5...Bg7 {Black ignores our attack and develops the bishop to g7. We continue with our h-file assault, keeping the options for both hxg6 and h6 to cramp Black further!} 6.h5 6...Nc6 {Continuing development here happens in 10% of the games. It is best for Black: they can even castle queenside in some cases. We'll push in the center here. Another promising path is 7. g3.} 7.d4 7...Bg4 {Black might be tempted to attack our h5-pawn here. The h-pawn isn't just a battering ram to open the position, but also a long-term strategic asset to cramp Black's kingside when pushed to h6.} 8.h6 8...Bf6 {Black saves the bishop. White are looking to continue solid development with a space advantage, and we'll play e3.} 9.e3 9...O-O {Black hides their king. We'll prepare to castle and break the pin.} 10.Be2 10...e5 {...e5 is a great move here. Black needs to fight back before we fully develop. We'll trade on d5, forcing a slightly better endgame. 11. Nxe5 was also possible with a similar concept.} 11.Nxd5 11...Qxd5 {The queen recaptures. We'll capture on e5.} 12.dxe5 12...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens. We'll recapture with the bishop.} 13.Bxd1 13...Bxe5 {Black grabs the pawn on e5. Let's trade yet again.} 14.Nxe5 14...Nxe5 {Black recaptures. Instead of trading yet again, we'll harass the bishop with f3.} 15.f3 15...Be6 {The bishop retreats to e6. We'll play b3, with the idea that the dark-squared bishop might be developed to the a1-h8 diagonal later.} 16.b3 16...Rfd8 {Black centralizes their rook.} 17.O-O {We'll castle here. Black is more active for the moment, but the bishop pair and cramping pawn structure on the kingside leave us optimistic.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 1: Black's Main Setup (5...Bg7) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/eeb3e06b-5459-40cb-9229-9aebd2d7f8b3/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black begins with the knight development. We'll bring the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {And here comes the move we are interested in. The early d5-push by Black immediately establishes a pawn in the center. We'll trade, otherwise ...d4 could come and Black will gain the desired central space!} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll advance with h4. The idea is to push h5 and attack the pawn structure.} 5.h4 5...Bg7 {Black ignores our attack and develops the bishop to g7. We continue with our h-file assault, keeping the options for both hxg6 and h6 to cramp Black further!} 6.h5 6...O-O {6... O-O might also occur here, featuring in 15% of the games played, but it is not very wise. We'll open up the h-file.} 7.hxg6 7...hxg6 {Black recaptures. Qa4 is a clever and ambitious move, as we go for mate with the hidden idea of swinging the queen over to the kingside!} 8.Qa4 8...e6 {Black covers the h4-square by pushing their pawn to e6. So we'll go to g4 instead, and threaten Qh3!} 9.Qg4 9...e5 {Black is fighting to prevent an h-file invasion. We'll retreat the queen to g3.} 10.Qg3 10...Re8 {Black centralizes their rook. Let's bring another piece to the kingside attack with Ng5!} 11.Ng5 11...Nc6 {Black continues their development.} 12.e3 {We do as well. The plan is to bring the bishop to c4 from where it will join the attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 1: Black's Main Setup (5...Bg7) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/eeb3e06b-5459-40cb-9229-9aebd2d7f8b3/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black begins with the knight development. We'll bring the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {And here comes the move we are interested in. The early d5-push by Black immediately establishes a pawn in the center. We'll trade, otherwise ...d4 could come and Black will gain the desired central space!} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll advance with h4. The idea is to push h5 and attack the pawn structure.} 5.h4 5...Bg7 {Black ignores our attack and develops the bishop to g7. We continue with our h-file assault, keeping the options for both hxg6 and h6 to cramp Black further!} 6.h5 6...Nxc3 {Black continues with the most popular move with 31% of games played — trading immediately on c3. We'll recapture with the b-pawn.} 7.bxc3 7...c5 {Black controls the d4-square. We'll give a check on a4. It's a clever way to activate our queen. A good alternative was 8. g3 to continue developing.} 8.Qa4+ 8...Bd7 {Black attacks our queen in return. The queen is safe on f4 while remaining active.} 9.Qf4 9...Nc6 {Black develops. Now e4 is a nice move to expand in the center. The queen is well-placed to defend the center and put pressure on Black's kingside.} 10.e4 10...Qb8 {A queen trade would help Black... ...so we'll decline it.} 11.Qe3 11...Qd6 {Black improves their queen.} 12.Bc4 {We'll continue our development. We keep the attacking opportunities on the kingside, and Ba3 to pressure the c5-pawn is a nice idea.} *

