1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black begins with the knight development. We'll bring the knight to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {And here comes the move we are interested in. The early d5-push by Black immediately establishes a pawn in the center. We'll trade, otherwise ...d4 could come and Black will gain the desired central space!} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we develop the knight.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll advance with h4. The idea is to push h5 and attack the pawn structure.} 5.h4 5...Bg7 {Black ignores our attack and develops the bishop to g7. We continue with our h-file assault, keeping the options for both hxg6 and h6 to cramp Black further!} 6.h5 6...O-O {6... O-O might also occur here, featuring in 15% of the games played, but it is not very wise. We'll open up the h-file.} 7.hxg6 7...hxg6 {Black recaptures. Qa4 is a clever and ambitious move, as we go for mate with the hidden idea of swinging the queen over to the kingside!} 8.Qa4 8...e5 {Black plays ...e5 immediately, again with the idea of preventing the queen's access to the h-file. Let's trade the knights.} 9.Nxd5 9...Qxd5 {The queen must recapture. And now we are able to invade the h-file!} 10.Qh4 10...Re8 {Black centralizes the rook and frees up the f8-square for their king. We'll push e4, attacking the enemy queen and gaining space in the center at the same time. This move also prevents Black from pushing ...e4.} 11.e4 11...Qd8 {Black offers a queen trade, which would get them out of trouble. We don't trade of course, but give a check on h7 instead.} 12.Qh7+ 12...Kf8 {The only move.} 13.d4 {d4 is a very powerful move here. The bishop will invade via the h6 square and Black will have a hard time surviving.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...h5, 5...h6, and 5...Nxc3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/c1c3674b-9f29-43d8-8edd-4e6b576abf6e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with a minor piece development. We'll develop the knight as well.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black goes for the Grunfeld approach. We'll trade our flank pawn for Black's central pawn.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the other knight to cover some central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto the bishop. As mentioned in the previous study, we'll play h4 here! The idea is to attack Black's kingside pawn structure with h5 coming next.} 5.h4 5...h6 {Black goes for ...h6. The idea is that if we push h5, Black solidifies with ...g5. This happens nearly 25% of the time. Let's just grab space in the center.} 6.e4 6...Nxc3 {Black trades. As usual, we have a stylistic choice of the more aggressive bxc3 or the endgame with dxc3. Both options are good but I prefer to be more aggressive here.} 7.bxc3 7...Bg7 {Black develops as expected. We'll gain central control with our pawns.} 8.d4 8...c5 {Black attacks the center in standard Grunfeld style. We'll simply prepare to castle kingside.} 9.Be2 9...Nc6 {Black continues with the development and puts pressure on the d4-pawn. We'll push the d-pawn! This is a very standard anti-Grunfeld idea as we bulldoze Black in the center regardless of material investment!} 10.d5 10...Ne5 {Black decides not to go for the exchange and centralizes the knight instead. We'll trade.} 11.Nxe5 11...Bxe5 {Black recaptures. We can safely castle here and offer the c3-pawn.} 12.O-O 12...Bxc3 {Black accepts it. Our rook will occupy the semi-open b-file. Black is suffering, despite the extra pawn, due to the lack of development and central control.} 13.Rb1 13...b6 {Black secures the b-pawn. Our queen comes to the queenside to eye some light squares and allow the rooks to be connected.} 14.Qb3 14...Bg7 {The bishop retreats. We'll offer to trade the dark-squared bishop. It is Black's only good piece, so trading it off is a great idea here.} 15.Bb2 15...Bxb2 {Black trades. We'll recapture with an attack on the rook.} 16.Qxb2 16...O-O {Black castles.} 17.Rbd1 {The rook will get centralized to d1. For the cost of a pawn we have good central control and very active pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...h5, 5...h6, and 5...Nxc3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/c1c3674b-9f29-43d8-8edd-4e6b576abf6e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with a minor piece development. We'll develop the knight as well.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black goes for the Grunfeld approach. We'll trade our flank pawn for Black's central pawn.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the other knight to cover some central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto the bishop. As mentioned in the previous study, we'll play h4 here! The idea is to attack Black's kingside pawn structure with h5 coming next.} 5.h4 5...h5 {Black's response could be 5...h5, which happens 29% of the time. The idea is to completely block our h-pawn and stop the attack. The good news for us is that we have the g5-square for ourselves. e4 is the most straightforward move. We gain space in the center with a tempo on Black's knight.} 6.e4 6...Nxc3 {Black trades the knights, which happens a mind-blowing 79% of the time. We recapture with the b-pawn. This is the most aggressive move, although 7. dxc3 was also possible to offer a slightly better endgame.} 7.bxc3 7...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the dark-squared bishop. The bishop moves so we can castle later. We use the flexibility of the English and don't commit to d4.} 8.Be2 8...c5 {Black takes control over the d4-square. We'll simply castle.} 9.O-O 9...Nc6 {Black continues with their development. We'll place the rook on the semi-open b-file.} 10.Rb1 10...O-O {Black hides their king.} 11.Ng5 {Let's activate the knight! Now we really see the benefit of including h4/h5 as we have great attacking chances on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...h5, 5...h6, and 5...Nxc3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/c1c3674b-9f29-43d8-8edd-4e6b576abf6e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with a minor piece development. We'll develop the knight as well.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black goes for the Grunfeld approach. We'll trade our flank pawn for Black's central pawn.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the other knight to cover some central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto the bishop. As mentioned in the previous study, we'll play h4 here! The idea is to attack Black's kingside pawn structure with h5 coming next.} 5.h4 5...h6 {Black goes for ...h6. The idea is that if we push h5, Black solidifies with ...g5. This happens nearly 25% of the time. Let's just grab space in the center.} 6.e4 6...Nxc3 {Black trades. As usual, we have a stylistic choice of the more aggressive bxc3 or the endgame with dxc3. Both options are good but I prefer to be more aggressive here.} 7.bxc3 7...Bg7 {Black develops as expected. We'll gain central control with our pawns.} 8.d4 8...c5 {Black attacks the center in standard Grunfeld style. We'll simply prepare to castle kingside.} 9.Be2 9...Nc6 {Black continues with the development and puts pressure on the d4-pawn. We'll push the d-pawn! This is a very standard anti-Grunfeld idea as we bulldoze Black in the center regardless of material investment!} 10.d5 10...Bxc3+ {Black accepts our sacrifice. We'll block the check with the bishop.} 11.Bd2 11...Bxa1 {Black goes for the material, and captures the rook. But now our queen will be on a very powerful dark-squared diagonal. Both the knight and the rook are hanging.} 12.Qxa1 12...Nd4 {The only move to defend both. Let's get rid of the knight.} 13.Nxd4 13...cxd4 {Recapturing. The queen captures on d4. We're not even down significant material as we have the bishop pair and a pawn for the exchange.} 14.Qxd4 14...f6 {Black defends the rook. We get castled.} 15.O-O 15...Kf7 {Black brings their king to a safer square. We'll blast open the center!} 16.e5 16...h5 {Black tries to keep the position closed...} 17.Bc4 {But Bc4 seals the deal. Black will not survive this attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...h5, 5...h6, and 5...Nxc3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/c1c3674b-9f29-43d8-8edd-4e6b576abf6e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with a minor piece development. We'll develop the knight as well.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black goes for the Grunfeld approach. We'll trade our flank pawn for Black's central pawn.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the other knight to cover some central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto the bishop. As mentioned in the previous study, we'll play h4 here! The idea is to attack Black's kingside pawn structure with h5 coming next.} 5.h4 5...h5 {Black's response could be 5...h5, which happens 29% of the time. The idea is to completely block our h-pawn and stop the attack. The good news for us is that we have the g5-square for ourselves. e4 is the most straightforward move. We gain space in the center with a tempo on Black's knight.} 6.e4 6...Nb6 {By retreating the knight, Black surrenders the center but is unable to generate enough pressure against it. The second most popular move, with an occurrence of 12%. We'll go ahead and grab the central squares!} 7.d4 7...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll add support to the d4-pawn.} 8.Be3 8...Bg4 {Black's bishop invades the g4-square and pins our knight. We will simply get out of the pin!} 9.Qd2 9...Bxf3 {The doubled pawns aren't a problem... ...since we strengthen the center!} 10.gxf3 10...Nc6 {Black continues their development.} 11.O-O-O {The king will be safe on the queenside. We have the bishop pair, so we'll open up the position with an f4-f5 advance.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...h5, 5...h6, and 5...Nxc3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/c1c3674b-9f29-43d8-8edd-4e6b576abf6e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with a minor piece development. We'll develop the knight as well.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black goes for the Grunfeld approach. We'll trade our flank pawn for Black's central pawn.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the other knight to cover some central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto the bishop. As mentioned in the previous study, we'll play h4 here! The idea is to attack Black's kingside pawn structure with h5 coming next.} 5.h4 5...h6 {Black goes for ...h6. The idea is that if we push h5, Black solidifies with ...g5. This happens nearly 25% of the time. Let's just grab space in the center.} 6.e4 6...Nxc3 {Black trades. As usual, we have a stylistic choice of the more aggressive bxc3 or the endgame with dxc3. Both options are good but I prefer to be more aggressive here.} 7.bxc3 7...Bg7 {Black develops as expected. We'll gain central control with our pawns.} 8.d4 8...c5 {Black attacks the center in standard Grunfeld style. We'll simply prepare to castle kingside.} 9.Be2 9...O-O {Black might get castled. We'll castle as well.} 10.O-O 10...Bg4 {Black develops the bishop. We'll occupy the semi-open b-file.} 11.Rb1 11...b6 {Black protects their b-pawn. Our dark-squared bishop will go to f4 and with Qd2 coming we prepare to pur pressure on the h6-pawn.} 12.Bf4 12...h5 {Black advances the h-pawn to keep it safe. We can now advance in the center with the typical d5!} 13.d5 13...Bxc3 {This might look like a free pawn for Black...} 14.e5 {...but we cut off Black's bishop with e5. Suddenly, the king lacks a defender.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...h5, 5...h6, and 5...Nxc3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/c1c3674b-9f29-43d8-8edd-4e6b576abf6e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with a minor piece development. We'll develop the knight as well.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black goes for the Grunfeld approach. We'll trade our flank pawn for Black's central pawn.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the other knight to cover some central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto the bishop. As mentioned in the previous study, we'll play h4 here! The idea is to attack Black's kingside pawn structure with h5 coming next.} 5.h4 5...h5 {Black's response could be 5...h5, which happens 29% of the time. The idea is to completely block our h-pawn and stop the attack. The good news for us is that we have the g5-square for ourselves. e4 is the most straightforward move. We gain space in the center with a tempo on Black's knight.} 6.Qb3 {6. Qb3 is also a good option when 6...Nb6 could be met with 7. d4 or the direct 7. Ng5.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 2: Black Plays 5...h5, 5...h6, and 5...Nxc3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/c1c3674b-9f29-43d8-8edd-4e6b576abf6e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with a minor piece development. We'll develop the knight as well.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black goes for the Grunfeld approach. We'll trade our flank pawn for Black's central pawn.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the other knight to cover some central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto the bishop. As mentioned in the previous study, we'll play h4 here! The idea is to attack Black's kingside pawn structure with h5 coming next.} 5.h4 5...Nxc3 {Yet another variation on the fifth move with the same statistic as 5...h6 (6%) is ...Nxc3. Black immediately trades the knights.} 6.bxc3 {We'll recapture and avoid the queen trade. After Black's next decision (...h5/...h6/...Bg7) we are transposing to one of these lines.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nxc3 {Black could go for the trade on the fifth move. We'll recapture with the d-pawn. This offers an endgame, but it is a very effective approach. The problem for Black is that the pawn on c5 causes queenside and central weaknesses.} 6.dxc3 6...Qxd1+ {Black goes for the queen trade. We'll recapture.} 7.Kxd1 7...Nc6 {Black is developing naturally.} 8.Kc2 {But our king finds a safe square on c2. Definitely a better position for White. Black's knight on c6 is nicely restricted by our c-pawn. We plan to develop the pieces (the bishops will go to c4 and e3) and gain space on the queenside with a4.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nc7 {We might encounter 5...Nc7 by Black. It has been played in 14% of games from this position. Our pawn break in the center is really strong.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black trades. We'll recapture with the knight.} 7.Nxd4 7...e5 {Black kicks us away, intending to trade the queens. We can invade the b5-square.} 8.Ndb5 8...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens. We'll recapture.} 9.Kxd1 9...Nxb5 {Black's knight is attacked, so they trade it off. We'll recapture, threatening Nc7+.} 10.Nxb5 10...Na6 {Black defends against the fork on c7.} 11.Be3 {And we bring the dark-squared bishop into the game. The light-squared bishop will be developed to c4, and our pieces will put pressure on the weak a7 and c7-squares, plus, we have the open c-file for our rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...Nc6 {We might also encounter an early ...Nc6 by Black, which occurs 13% of the time — just going for the development. 4...Nxc3 5.bxc3 g6 6.h4 is a popular move order that can transpose into our previous studies. We'll push e4, gaining space in the center with a tempo.} 5.e4 5...Nxc3 {Black trades. We'll recapture with the b-pawn to support d4.} 6.bxc3 6...e5 {Black prevents d4. We'll pin the knight on c6 to fight for the d4-square!} 7.Bb5 7...Bd6 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop.} 8.d4 {And we establish a strong central formation with d4. In this rare position, our center gives us slightly better chances in the ensuing struggle.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nb4 {With 5...Nb4 Black wants to make use of the weakness on d3. Retreating is passive and allow us to push d4 easily. We'll develop the bishop to a strong light-square diagonal.} 6.Bc4 6...Nd3+ {Black gives a check on d3. We don't mind bringing the king forward. The knight on d3 is attacked, and since we are so far ahead in development, Ke2 is a good move.} 7.Ke2 7...Nxc1+ {Black decides to trade for the bishop on c1. We'll just recapture. Black gains the bishop pair but has literally zero development. Black has to be careful now. For example, 8...a6 9.d4 and suddenly we are very active in the center!} 8.Rxc1 8...Nc6 {Black tries to catch up with their development. We'll pin the knight.} 9.Bb5 9...Bd7 {Black breaks it. And we'll gain space in the center!} 10.d4 10...cxd4 {Black trades. Firstly, we'll capture on c6.} 11.Bxc6 11...Bxc6 {And after ...Bxc6...} 12.Nxd4 {...we'll recapture on d4. Our position is very comfortable. The knight is very strong on d4 and we have the space and development advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nb4 {With 5...Nb4 Black wants to make use of the weakness on d3. Retreating is passive and allow us to push d4 easily. We'll develop the bishop to a strong light-square diagonal.} 6.Bc4 6...Nd3+ {Black gives a check on d3. We don't mind bringing the king forward. The knight on d3 is attacked, and since we are so far ahead in development, Ke2 is a good move.} 7.Ke2 7...Nf4+ {Black is playing beginner chess, but the solo knight is very disruptive! We'll tuck the king on f1, protecting g2. We plan to push d4 and try to realize the better development of our pieces.} 8.Kf1 8...Ne6 {The move d4 would come with an attack on Black's f4-knight, so Black retreats to e6 and eyes the d4-square. We push d4 anyway! Since this line is pretty rare, I will give you a simple suggestion to get into a safe two-result endgame.} 9.d4 9...Nxd4 {Black might opt for 9...Nxd4. We'll simply develop the dark-squared bishop.} 10.Bf4 10...Nbc6 {Black supports the knight on d4. We'll trade it. 11...Nxd4 allows us 12.Nd5, threatening to arrive on c7 with check.} 11.Nxd4 11...cxd4 {Black recaptures with a tempo... We don't have to save the knight! Instead, Qb3 creates a queen-bishop battery that eyes the weak f7-pawn!} 12.Qb3 12...e6 {Black blocks it.} 13.Nb5 {And we go for the c7-square yet again! Black cannot defend it.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nb4 {With 5...Nb4 Black wants to make use of the weakness on d3. Retreating is passive and allow us to push d4 easily. We'll develop the bishop to a strong light-square diagonal.} 6.Bc4 6...Nd3+ {Black gives a check on d3. We don't mind bringing the king forward. The knight on d3 is attacked, and since we are so far ahead in development, Ke2 is a good move.} 7.Ke2 7...Nf4+ {Black is playing beginner chess, but the solo knight is very disruptive! We'll tuck the king on f1, protecting g2. We plan to push d4 and try to realize the better development of our pieces.} 8.Kf1 8...Ne6 {The move d4 would come with an attack on Black's f4-knight, so Black retreats to e6 and eyes the d4-square. We push d4 anyway! Since this line is pretty rare, I will give you a simple suggestion to get into a safe two-result endgame.} 9.d4 9...Nxd4 {Black might opt for 9...Nxd4. We'll simply develop the dark-squared bishop.} 10.Bf4 10...e6 {In the case of ...e6, to open up the dark-squared bishop... We'll trade on d4.} 11.Nxd4 11...cxd4 {Black recaptures. And White's knight jumps to b5, threatening a fork on c7 and pressuring the d4-pawn.} 12.Nb5 12...Na6 {Black defends against the fork.} 13.Be5 {The pawn on d4 is lost and White has superior development.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nb4 {With 5...Nb4 Black wants to make use of the weakness on d3. Retreating is passive and allow us to push d4 easily. We'll develop the bishop to a strong light-square diagonal.} 6.Bc4 6...Nd3+ {Black gives a check on d3. We don't mind bringing the king forward. The knight on d3 is attacked, and since we are so far ahead in development, Ke2 is a good move.} 7.Ke2 7...Nf4+ {Black is playing beginner chess, but the solo knight is very disruptive! We'll tuck the king on f1, protecting g2. We plan to push d4 and try to realize the better development of our pieces.} 8.Kf1 8...Ne6 {The move d4 would come with an attack on Black's f4-knight, so Black retreats to e6 and eyes the d4-square. We push d4 anyway! Since this line is pretty rare, I will give you a simple suggestion to get into a safe two-result endgame.} 9.d4 9...cxd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll get rid of the Black defender of the d4-pawn.} 10.Bxe6 10...Bxe6 {Black recaptures. We'll recapture the pawn and centralize our knight.} 11.Nxd4 11...Bc4+ {Black checks us on c4. We'll hide the king on g1. It's completely safe there.} 12.Kg1 12...Nc6 {Black has a couple of choices here. The first is to challenge our knight on d4. We'll go ahead and trade.} 13.Nxc6 13...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens. We'll recapture.} 14.Nxd1 14...bxc6 {Black recaptures the piece. And we'll strengthen the e4-pawn. Black has the worse structure but the bishop pair should be enough to hold the balance. Still, if we get mobilized the Black queenside is very vulnerable.} 15.f3 15...e5 {Black controls some central squares. We'll place the bishop on a good diagonal, from where it's eyeing the isolated a7-pawn.} 16.Be3 16...Bb4 {Black places their dark-squared bishop on the most active square. We'll prepare to connect the rooks.} 17.Kf2 17...h5 {Black gains some space on the kingside. We can push the dark-squared bishop back.} 18.a3 18...Ba5 {Black wants to stay on the e1-a5 diagonal and perhaps retreat to b6. We can punish this move by bringing the rook to the semi-open c-file with an attack on the light-squared bishop.} 19.Rc1 19...Bb5 {Black retreats... ...but we have a4 now! This is a very strong move which comes with tempo.} 20.a4 20...Bxa4 {Black accepts our "sacrifice". We'll put pressure on both of the bishops.} 21.Ra1 21...Bxd1 {Black must trade, otherwise they would lose a piece.} 22.Rxa5 {We'll capture the bishop on a5. Both the bishop on d1 and the pawn on e5 are hanging. White is doing great here. The opposite-color bishops increase our advantage as we dominate the dark squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nb4 {With 5...Nb4 Black wants to make use of the weakness on d3. Retreating is passive and allow us to push d4 easily. We'll develop the bishop to a strong light-square diagonal.} 6.Bc4 6...Nd3+ {Black gives a check on d3. We don't mind bringing the king forward. The knight on d3 is attacked, and since we are so far ahead in development, Ke2 is a good move.} 7.Ke2 7...Nf4+ {Black is playing beginner chess, but the solo knight is very disruptive! We'll tuck the king on f1, protecting g2. We plan to push d4 and try to realize the better development of our pieces.} 8.Kf1 8...Ne6 {The move d4 would come with an attack on Black's f4-knight, so Black retreats to e6 and eyes the d4-square. We push d4 anyway! Since this line is pretty rare, I will give you a simple suggestion to get into a safe two-result endgame.} 9.d4 9...cxd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll get rid of the Black defender of the d4-pawn.} 10.Bxe6 10...Bxe6 {Black recaptures. We'll recapture the pawn and centralize our knight.} 11.Nxd4 11...Nc6 {Black develops the knight instead of playing ...Bc4+. We'll get rid of the light-squared bishop.} 12.Nxe6 12...Qxd1+ {Black trades the queens. We'll recapture.} 13.Nxd1 13...fxe6 {Black recaptures on e6.} 14.Bd2 {And White has the better pawn structure here and should be comfortable. This happened in a game from 2018 played in London between Levon Aronian and Maxime Vachier-Lagrave.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nb4 {With 5...Nb4 Black wants to make use of the weakness on d3. Retreating is passive and allow us to push d4 easily. We'll develop the bishop to a strong light-square diagonal.} 6.Bc4 6...Nd3+ {Black gives a check on d3. We don't mind bringing the king forward. The knight on d3 is attacked, and since we are so far ahead in development, Ke2 is a good move.} 7.Ke2 7...Nf4+ {Black is playing beginner chess, but the solo knight is very disruptive! We'll tuck the king on f1, protecting g2. We plan to push d4 and try to realize the better development of our pieces.} 8.Kf1 8...Ne6 {The move d4 would come with an attack on Black's f4-knight, so Black retreats to e6 and eyes the d4-square. We push d4 anyway! Since this line is pretty rare, I will give you a simple suggestion to get into a safe two-result endgame.} 9.d4 9...cxd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll get rid of the Black defender of the d4-pawn.} 10.Bxe6 10...Bxe6 {Black recaptures. We'll recapture the pawn and centralize our knight.} 11.Nxd4 11...Bc4+ {Black checks us on c4. We'll hide the king on g1. It's completely safe there.} 12.Kg1 12...e5 {The second option is 12...e5. We can give a check on a4 and put pressure on the bishop.} 13.Qa4+ 13...Qd7 {Black blocks the check while keeping an eye on the d4-knight. Our knight jumps forward, threatening both Nc7+ and Qxc4!} 14.Ndb5 14...Bxb5 {Black trades. We'll recapture with the knight, still threatening Nc7+.} 15.Nxb5 15...Nc6 {Black avoids this threat.} 16.Be3 {And we pressurize the a7-pawn with Rd1 coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nb6 {Another move for Black is to retreat the knight to b6. As usual, we'll grab space with d4.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black trades. We recapture with the queen, as we don't object to a further trade.} 7.Qxd4 7...Qxd4 {...since after Black trades the queens...} 8.Nxd4 {...our pieces are much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 6: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 1) / Study 3: Black's 4th Move Options (4...c5, 4...Nxc3, 4...Nc6) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/6fb2a59b-000d-46da-8037-cb1d54d7e4b9/studies/d7ea1509-3b19-4077-b7cf-64135ccbd5d9/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight first and fights for the central squares. We develop our queen's knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black plays in the style of the Grunfeld. We'll trade. Trading a flank pawn for Black's central pawn is a good idea for sure.} 3.cxd5 3...Nxd5 {Black recaptures with the knight. And we develop the knight to fight for central squares.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {With 4...c5, Black wants to prevent us from pushing d4. So we fight for the center with tempo! The variations in this line are pretty unusual and you are most likely to face them against experienced players.} 5.e4 5...Nb4 {With 5...Nb4 Black wants to make use of the weakness on d3. Retreating is passive and allow us to push d4 easily. We'll develop the bishop to a strong light-square diagonal.} 6.Bc4 6...Nd3+ {Black gives a check on d3. We don't mind bringing the king forward. The knight on d3 is attacked, and since we are so far ahead in development, Ke2 is a good move.} 7.Ke2 7...Nf4+ {Black is playing beginner chess, but the solo knight is very disruptive! We'll tuck the king on f1, protecting g2. We plan to push d4 and try to realize the better development of our pieces.} 8.Kf1 8...Nd3 {If Black decides to jump back into the d3-square... ...we can attack it with our queen, since ...Nd3 didn't come with a check.} 9.Qe2 9...Nxc1 {Black avoids yet another knight move and trades for our bishop on c1. We'll recapture.} 10.Rxc1 10...e6 {Black develops by opening up the dark-squared bishop.} 11.h4 {And here comes h4! The plan is to gain space with e5 and develop the rook via the h3-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 1: Black Plays Nf6, g6, and d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/da91ed3b-d734-4ce6-948e-76e357211212/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black answers with development. We'll bring the knight out to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll do likewise.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll trade off the flank pawn.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll fianchetto with an attack on the knight.} 5.Bg2 5...Nxc3 {And here, the most popular move,(51% of games) is to trade the knights on c3. We'll recapture with the b-pawn and keep the queens on.} 6.bxc3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Let's stick to the concept we used in the last chapter, the rapid h-pawn advance!} 7.h4 7...c5 {Black adds control to the d4-square immediately. Black can try to ignore our play but this will allow us to start squeezing their position on both flanks, a plan we will initiate with h5.} 8.h5 8...Nc6 {Black is just developing. Our rook gets out of the bishop's scope and occupies the semi-open b-file.} 9.Rb1 9...Qc7 {Black gets their queen out and supports the b7-pawn. With h6 we attack the bishop but more importantly, we cramp Black's kingside!} 10.h6 10...Bf6 {Black saves the bishop. Let's improve the knight via h3. It aims at the f4-square.} 11.Nh3 11...O-O {Black gets their king out of the center. Our knight will be well-placed on f4. ...e5, to kick it out, would weaken the d5-square.} 12.Nf4 12...e6 {Black protects the d5-square.} 13.O-O {We get castled. The h6-pawn grants us a space advantage on the kingside, and the semi-open b-file is advantageous for us. Also, Black's light-squared bishop has a hard time finding activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 1: Black Plays Nf6, g6, and d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/da91ed3b-d734-4ce6-948e-76e357211212/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black answers with development. We'll bring the knight out to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll do likewise.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll trade off the flank pawn.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll fianchetto with an attack on the knight.} 5.Bg2 5...c6 {Black supports the knight. This is a bit passive and we can proceed with occupying the center by playing d4. Black usually needs to challenge our d4 pawn with ..c5, but this will cost at least an extra tempo, due to the premature ...c6.} 6.d4 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll continue the natural development of our pieces.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Black gets castled. We'll do likewise.} 8.O-O 8...Nd7 {Black keeps developing. We'll expand in the center with tempo!} 9.e4 9...Nxc3 {Black trades, otherwise they would be too passive.} 10.bxc3 {Our center is just beautiful.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 1: Black Plays Nf6, g6, and d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/da91ed3b-d734-4ce6-948e-76e357211212/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black answers with development. We'll bring the knight out to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll do likewise.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll trade off the flank pawn.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll fianchetto with an attack on the knight.} 5.Bg2 5...Nxc3 {And here, the most popular move,(51% of games) is to trade the knights on c3. We'll recapture with the b-pawn and keep the queens on.} 6.bxc3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Let's stick to the concept we used in the last chapter, the rapid h-pawn advance!} 7.h4 7...h6 {Black intends ...g5 if we push h5. We'll get our rook out of the bishop's gaze - a typical inclusion to activate the rook - and put pressure on Black's queenside.} 8.Rb1 8...Nc6 {Black develops the knight to c6. With d4, we gain some central control. Black misses the ...c5 break to challenge our center.} 9.d4 9...O-O {Black hides their king.} 10.f4 {We'll push f4. It's not the only move, but it is very ambitious to rule out all of Black's pawn breaks!} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 1: Black Plays Nf6, g6, and d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/da91ed3b-d734-4ce6-948e-76e357211212/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black answers with development. We'll bring the knight out to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll do likewise.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll trade off the flank pawn.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll fianchetto with an attack on the knight.} 5.Bg2 5...Nxc3 {And here, the most popular move,(51% of games) is to trade the knights on c3. We'll recapture with the b-pawn and keep the queens on.} 6.bxc3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Let's stick to the concept we used in the last chapter, the rapid h-pawn advance!} 7.h4 7...h6 {Black intends ...g5 if we push h5. We'll get our rook out of the bishop's gaze - a typical inclusion to activate the rook - and put pressure on Black's queenside.} 8.Rb1 8...Nd7 {Black might decide to develop the knight to d7, but it is passive and doesn't put any pressure on the center. We'll ignore the capture on b7, and proceed with normal development. 9.Bxb7 Bxb7 10.Rxb7 Nb6 would leave our rook trapped.} 9.Nf3 9...O-O {Black gets castled.} 10.d4 {We expand in the center. Black's pieces are suddenly very passive.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 1: Black Plays Nf6, g6, and d5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/da91ed3b-d734-4ce6-948e-76e357211212/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black answers with development. We'll bring the knight out to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll do likewise.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll trade off the flank pawn.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll fianchetto with an attack on the knight.} 5.Bg2 5...c6 {Black supports the knight. This is a bit passive and we can proceed with occupying the center by playing d4. Black usually needs to challenge our d4 pawn with ..c5, but this will cost at least an extra tempo, due to the premature ...c6.} 6.d4 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll continue the natural development of our pieces.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Black gets castled. We'll do likewise.} 8.O-O 8...Bf5 {If ...Bf5 comes...} 9.Ng5 {...we have Ng5 with a quick e4 coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 1: Black Plays Nf6, g6, and d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/da91ed3b-d734-4ce6-948e-76e357211212/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black answers with development. We'll bring the knight out to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll do likewise.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll trade off the flank pawn.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll fianchetto with an attack on the knight.} 5.Bg2 5...Nxc3 {And here, the most popular move,(51% of games) is to trade the knights on c3. We'll recapture with the b-pawn and keep the queens on.} 6.bxc3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Let's stick to the concept we used in the last chapter, the rapid h-pawn advance!} 7.h4 7...O-O {Black doesn't believe in our attack! But we do! Let's push forward with h5!} 8.h5 8...c5 {Black stakes a claim to the center. We'll open up the h-file.} 9.hxg6 9...hxg6 {Black recaptures. With Qa4, we borrow an idea from the previous chapter! The queen will slide to the h-file.} 10.Qa4 10...e5 {Black discourages our queen from coming to the h4-square.} 11.Rb1 {The rook occupies the semi-open b-file. Yet again, our pieces are very active compared to Black and the open h-file poses a great threat.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 1: Black Plays Nf6, g6, and d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/da91ed3b-d734-4ce6-948e-76e357211212/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black answers with development. We'll bring the knight out to c3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares to fianchetto. We'll do likewise.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We'll trade off the flank pawn.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll fianchetto with an attack on the knight.} 5.Bg2 5...Nxc3 {And here, the most popular move,(51% of games) is to trade the knights on c3. We'll recapture with the b-pawn and keep the queens on.} 6.bxc3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes. Let's stick to the concept we used in the last chapter, the rapid h-pawn advance!} 7.h4 7...h5 {Black stops our h-pawn from advancing. We'll develop our knight and take aim at the weakened g5-square.} 8.Nf3 8...c5 {Black adds some control to the center.} 9.Ng5 {White improves the knight. The Ng5 makes Black's future castling uncomfortable. We'll play Rb1 and pressure the b7-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Last chapter we examined Black going for the central thrust a move earlier but some players stick to the standard...Nf6/...g6/...d5. We'll trade the flank c-pawn for Black's central d-pawn, and lure out their knight.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll fianchetto with tempo.} 5.Bg2 5...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight. Our next move of d3 is a very important move for our Anti-Grunfeld! The pawn on d3 is very solid and Black will not have an easy way to counterattack the center.} 6.d3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes as well. We'll bring the bishop to e3. It will help to launch a kingside attack with h4 and h5 coming.} 7.Be3 7...O-O {Black gets their king out of the center. We'll create a queen-bishop battery and aim it at Black's kingside.} 8.Qd2 8...e5 {Black expands in the center. We'll follow up with a common plan here — kingside expansion.} 9.h4 9...h5 {Black stops it. But we persist in our plan of expanding! 10...hxg4 11.h5 allows us to open up lines. Black is in a lot of trouble if they capture our pawns!} 10.g4 10...Bxg4 {This exchange sacrifice isn't good. ...e5 has added nothing for Black. We'll simply take on b7.} 11.Bxb7 11...N8d7 {Even though our light squares may seem weak, they aren't. We'll take.} 12.Bxa8 12...Qxa8 {And after the recapture...} 13.f3 {Our pawn triangle defends the light squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Last chapter we examined Black going for the central thrust a move earlier but some players stick to the standard...Nf6/...g6/...d5. We'll trade the flank c-pawn for Black's central d-pawn, and lure out their knight.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll fianchetto with tempo.} 5.Bg2 5...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight. Our next move of d3 is a very important move for our Anti-Grunfeld! The pawn on d3 is very solid and Black will not have an easy way to counterattack the center.} 6.d3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes as well. We'll bring the bishop to e3. It will help to launch a kingside attack with h4 and h5 coming.} 7.Be3 7...O-O {Black gets their king out of the center. We'll create a queen-bishop battery and aim it at Black's kingside.} 8.Qd2 8...Re8 {Black centralizes the rook. We'll go for the attack!} 9.h4 9...h5 {Black stops our idea. But we're determined to attack on the kingside and push g4. 10...hxg4 11.h5 allows us to open up lines.} 10.g4 10...Bxg4 {If Black captures on g4 with the bishop... ...we'll get the b7-pawn with the rook falling next.} 11.Bxb7 11...N8d7 {Black develops the knight. Let's win the material.} 12.Bxa8 12...Qxa8 {The queen recaptures.} 13.f3 {And White saves the rook by blocking the diagonal. The rook on e8 does nothing to help.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...c6 {Not a very common move order, but it could transpose to a main line (and very solid) "Fianchetto Grunfeld" which we are interested in avoiding with our repertoire. We'll try to make the ...d5-push more difficult.} 4.e4 4...d6 {This is an unusual move order that leads to more of a King's Indian position. Nevertheless, it happens 35% of the time. We are a bit "tricked" out of our usual setups, but Black is playing pretty passively. Let's grab space in the center.} 5.d4 5...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the bishop. We do as well.} 6.Bg2 6...O-O {Black gets their king into safety. We'll prepare to castle as well. We have a very imposing center and it seems like Black isn't able to generate sufficient pressure against it. Let's look at one typical variation where both sides follow logical development.} 7.Nge2 7...e5 {Black attacks the center in King's Indian fashion. We'll get our king into safety.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {Black continues their development. We'll take the g4-square away from Black, so Be3 can be played next.} 9.h3 9...Re8 {Black centralizes the rook. e3 is a nice square for the dark-squared bishop. It supports the center and it cannot be attacked, since we pushed h3.} 10.Be3 10...exd4 {Black should throw this move in at some point to activate some of their pieces. We'll recapture and centralize our knight.} 11.Nxd4 11...Nc5 {Black invades the c5-square. Our queen goes to c2 to support the e4-pawn and to connect the rooks.} 12.Qc2 12...a5 {Black controls the b4-square. We'll centralize the rooks now.} 13.Rad1 13...Qe7 {Black adds pressure to the e4-pawn. As planned, we'll get the other rook to the center.} 14.Rfe1 14...a4 {Black expands further on the queenside. With b4, we gain space and also attack the knight.} 15.b4 15...axb3 {Black captures en passant.} 16.axb3 {We'll recapture. We have a very strong and solid center. Black will hope for a counterattack once we start advancing with ideas such as b4/f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Last chapter we examined Black going for the central thrust a move earlier but some players stick to the standard...Nf6/...g6/...d5. We'll trade the flank c-pawn for Black's central d-pawn, and lure out their knight.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll fianchetto with tempo.} 5.Bg2 5...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight. Our next move of d3 is a very important move for our Anti-Grunfeld! The pawn on d3 is very solid and Black will not have an easy way to counterattack the center.} 6.d3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes as well. We'll bring the bishop to e3. It will help to launch a kingside attack with h4 and h5 coming.} 7.Be3 7...O-O {Black gets their king out of the center. We'll create a queen-bishop battery and aim it at Black's kingside.} 8.Qd2 8...Nc6 {Black might opt for the knight development, intending ...Nd4. So we'll get rid of it, damaging Black's pawn structure.} 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 {Black recaptures. We'll try to trade off the main defender of the Black king.} 10.Bh6 10...c5 {Black wants to fianchetto their other bishop. We'll trade as planned.} 11.Bxg7 11...Kxg7 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the knight to a natural square and control the center.} 12.Nf3 12...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes and pins our knight. We'll improve the queen and attack the c5-pawn.} 13.Qe3 13...c4 {Black pushes the c-pawn.} 14.O-O-O {And we'll castle queenside. White has better piece placement, the rooks are connected and the pawn structure is also better than Black's.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Last chapter we examined Black going for the central thrust a move earlier but some players stick to the standard...Nf6/...g6/...d5. We'll trade the flank c-pawn for Black's central d-pawn, and lure out their knight.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll fianchetto with tempo.} 5.Bg2 5...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight. Our next move of d3 is a very important move for our Anti-Grunfeld! The pawn on d3 is very solid and Black will not have an easy way to counterattack the center.} 6.d3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes as well. We'll bring the bishop to e3. It will help to launch a kingside attack with h4 and h5 coming.} 7.Be3 7...O-O {Black gets their king out of the center. We'll create a queen-bishop battery and aim it at Black's kingside.} 8.Qd2 8...Nc6 {Black might opt for the knight development, intending ...Nd4. So we'll get rid of it, damaging Black's pawn structure.} 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 {Black recaptures. We'll try to trade off the main defender of the Black king.} 10.Bh6 10...c5 {Black wants to fianchetto their other bishop. We'll trade as planned.} 11.Bxg7 11...Kxg7 {Black recaptures. We'll develop the knight to a natural square and control the center.} 12.Nf3 12...Bh3 {Black could try to prevent our king from castling kingside. We'll attack the bishop right away.} 13.Ng5 13...Bg2 {In return, Black attacks our rook. Rg1 is the only way to save it. We have lost the rights to castle kingside, but that doesn't bother us!} 14.Rg1 14...Bb7 {Black retreats back to b7. We'll play our queen to e3, where it is more active and attacks the c5-pawn.} 15.Qe3 15...c4 {Black advances the c-pawn so as to not lose it.} 16.O-O-O {We'll castle queenside! The rook is dangerously lined up with Black's queen, and our knight on g5 is very active.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Last chapter we examined Black going for the central thrust a move earlier but some players stick to the standard...Nf6/...g6/...d5. We'll trade the flank c-pawn for Black's central d-pawn, and lure out their knight.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll fianchetto with tempo.} 5.Bg2 5...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight. Our next move of d3 is a very important move for our Anti-Grunfeld! The pawn on d3 is very solid and Black will not have an easy way to counterattack the center.} 6.d3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes as well. We'll bring the bishop to e3. It will help to launch a kingside attack with h4 and h5 coming.} 7.Be3 7...Nc6 {I think this is a bit more precise for Black than committing to immediate castling and giving us a target. We'll develop logically and keep an eye on Nd4 ideas.} 8.Nf3 8...O-O {Black gets their king into safety. We'll aim a queen-bishop battery at the kingside.} 9.Qd2 9...e5 {Black expands in the center. But with h4, we'll start a kingside attack. The idea is to push h5 and open up the h-file!} 10.h4 10...h5 {Black decides to stop the h-pawn advance. The kingside attack is blocked, so we'll switch to more positional waters as we look to generate pressure against Black's queenside through our powerful bishop on g2.} 11.O-O 11...Nd4 {Black centralizes the knight. Let's prod at the black position with Bg5 and try to induce further weaknesses.} 12.Bg5 12...f6 {Black saves the queen and attacks our bishop.} 13.Bh6 {We'll trade off the dark-squared bishops. We can get our knight to a very nice c5-outpost and attack along the c-file with Rc1.} *

