[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...c6 {Not a very common move order, but it could transpose to a main line (and very solid) "Fianchetto Grunfeld" which we are interested in avoiding with our repertoire. We'll try to make the ...d5-push more difficult.} 4.e4 4...d5 {Black goes for central activity. You won't encounter this very often, with about 16% of games, but it should be covered anyway. We'll trade once.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll gain space in the center, which also comes with an attack on the knight.} 6.e5 6...Ne4 {Black saves their knight instead. We'll add pressure to it by fianchettoing the bishop.} 7.Bg2 7...Nc6 {Black counter-attacks the e5-square. We'll increase our central control with d4.} 8.d4 8...Nxc3 {Black trades the knights.} 9.bxc3 {White recaptures. An original position. Our slightly weak c3 pawn is easily protected by Ne2 and with our e5 spearhead we can aim to generate a kingside attack in the middlegame.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 2"]
