1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Last chapter we examined Black going for the central thrust a move earlier but some players stick to the standard...Nf6/...g6/...d5. We'll trade the flank c-pawn for Black's central d-pawn, and lure out their knight.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll fianchetto with tempo.} 5.Bg2 5...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight. Our next move of d3 is a very important move for our Anti-Grunfeld! The pawn on d3 is very solid and Black will not have an easy way to counterattack the center.} 6.d3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes as well. We'll bring the bishop to e3. It will help to launch a kingside attack with h4 and h5 coming.} 7.Be3 7...O-O {Black gets their king out of the center. We'll create a queen-bishop battery and aim it at Black's kingside.} 8.Qd2 8...Re8 {Black centralizes the rook. We'll go for the attack!} 9.h4 9...Nc6 {Black develops the knight instead. We'll keep attacking!} 10.h5 10...Bf5 {Black defends g6 with their bishop. We'll trade in order to open up the h-file.} 11.hxg6 11...Bxg6 {Black recaptures with the bishop. Once again, we're going for the dark-squared bishops trade to weaken the dark squares.} 12.Bh6 12...Bh8 {Black doesn't want to trade. Let's develop the knight and prepare to jump to h4!} 13.Nf3 13...e6 {Black opens up lines for their queen.} 14.Nh4 {As planned, the knight is amazing on h4 and Black's king doesn't feel safe.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...d5 {Last chapter we examined Black going for the central thrust a move earlier but some players stick to the standard...Nf6/...g6/...d5. We'll trade the flank c-pawn for Black's central d-pawn, and lure out their knight.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {Black recaptures. We'll fianchetto with tempo.} 5.Bg2 5...Nb6 {Black retreats the knight. Our next move of d3 is a very important move for our Anti-Grunfeld! The pawn on d3 is very solid and Black will not have an easy way to counterattack the center.} 6.d3 6...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes as well. We'll bring the bishop to e3. It will help to launch a kingside attack with h4 and h5 coming.} 7.Be3 7...O-O {Black gets their king out of the center. We'll create a queen-bishop battery and aim it at Black's kingside.} 8.Qd2 8...c6 {Black blocks the diagonal. We'll use our classic plan here — trade off the dark-squared bishops.} 9.Bh6 9...Bxh6 {Black trades. We'll recapture.} 10.Qxh6 10...Qd4 {Black's idea is to defend the dark-squares with ...Qg7. We'll go for expansion on the kingside.} 11.h4 11...Qg7 {Black offers a queen trade.} 12.Qe3 {Trading the queens would help Black. We'll retreat the queen and try to make use of our superior development and space on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 13"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black continues in more of a King's Indian style. This happens a mindblowing 90% of the time! We'll quickly fianchetto the light-squared bishop.} 4.Bg2 4...c6 {Black tries to restrict the activity of our light-squared bishop.} 5.e4 {But we'll gain central space in return! Black can't really go into a Grunfeld now and should go for a more King's Indian approach. We examined a similar case after 3...c6.} *

[Event "Chapter 7: Beating The Grunfeld (Part 2) / Study 2: Alternatives to 3...d5 and the 5...Nb6 Retreat / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/5068d78a-da55-4009-a839-21ca76acb35f/studies/7caea895-584b-404d-9281-aa3f9be45d1d/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds by developing the king's knight. We'll bring our knight out as well.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {And Black goes for 2...g6 which happens 36% of the time, looking to bring the bishop to g7. We'll respond in a similar manner, aiming to bring the bishop to g2 and control the d5-square.} 3.g3 3...c6 {Not a very common move order, but it could transpose to a main line (and very solid) "Fianchetto Grunfeld" which we are interested in avoiding with our repertoire. We'll try to make the ...d5-push more difficult.} 4.e4 4...d5 {Black goes for central activity. You won't encounter this very often, with about 16% of games, but it should be covered anyway. We'll trade once.} 5.cxd5 5...cxd5 {Black recaptures. And we'll gain space in the center, which also comes with an attack on the knight.} 6.e5 6...d4 {Black doesn't move the knight, but instead counter-attacks our knight. We'll capture on f6 to double up the f-pawns.} 7.exf6 7...dxc3 {Black regains the material. We'll recapture with the b-pawn, so d4 can be pushed next.} 8.bxc3 8...exf6 {Black recaptures the pawn.} 9.d4 {White expands in the center. We have the better center, our dark-squared bishop nicely aims at the kingside, and the light-squared bishop will control the h1-a8 diagonal.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...h6 {8...h6 is the fourth most popular move in this position. Black doesn't want our pieces coming to g5. That's fine, we'll still expand on the queenside!} 9.b4 9...Be6 {Black activates the bishop, and Black proves the point of ...h6. There's no Ng5 now. But there is still b5!} 10.b5 10...Ne7 {Black retreats to e7. And we'll jump to d2. Our bishop is unleashed with an attack on the b7-pawn. Black's got two real ways of defending it, since c6 weakens d6.} 11.Nd2 11...Rb8 {11...Rb8 is a slight mistake. It simply allows us to develop the queen with tempo, hitting the a7-pawn. Black can't push ...a6.} 12.Qa4 12...Nc8 {Their best defense is ...Nc8, and now their pieces look passive on the back rank.} 13.Nd5 {Meanwhile, our knight can centralize to d5 and grab even more space. White is better here with a comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {The most popular move for Black here is to directly prevent our b4 idea with ...a5. However, a lot of people won't play that move, especially in faster time controls. We'll insist on playing b4, with a3! This is the most consistent approach.} 9.a3 9...h6 {The most popular here is ...h6, controlling the g5-square. Black often wants to develop the light-squared bishop to e6 and doesn't want our knight attacking it. We'll proceed with our planned b4.} 10.b4 10...axb4 {Black captures... And we'll take back, naturally. The a-file is now open, but we're threatening b5.} 11.axb4 11...Be6 {And ...Be6, the most common approach here from Black, does nothing to stop it. We'll simply continue with b5.} 12.b5 12...Ne7 {Black retreats to e7... And we'll develop the bishop to b2. Activating it to a3 might generally be better, but when Black has played ...a5 and opened up the a-file, we can't develop it there.} 13.Bb2 13...Qd7 {Another common idea by Black, creating a battery. Black wants ...Bh3, trading the light-squared bishop. We'll fight for the a-file with Ra1. Re1 was also logical to anticipate ...Bh3 but it seems like we can ignore the bishop trade here.} 14.Ra1 14...Rab8 {Black could also avoid the trade with ...Rab8. This gives us the a-file right away. We'll invade and put pressure on b7.} 15.Ra7 15...Bh3 {Black can try to trade bishops. We'll play e4, which is not the only move but an interesting idea since Black is about to trade light-squared bishops. We put our pawns on the light squares in anticipation of the trade.} 16.e4 16...Bxg2 {Black takes... And we'll recapture.} 17.Kxg2 17...Nh7 {The knight retreats to h7 now, possibly preparing ...f5.} 18.Qb3 {Qb3 is an antidote for that. A great improving move is to centralize the knight on d5, as well as to push d4 at some point. We want to open up our bishop on b2.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {The most popular move for Black here is to directly prevent our b4 idea with ...a5. However, a lot of people won't play that move, especially in faster time controls. We'll insist on playing b4, with a3! This is the most consistent approach.} 9.a3 9...Nh5 {As on move 8, Black could jump to h5 with a similar idea, i.e. to push the f-pawn. Let's go for b4 anyway.} 10.b4 10...axb4 {Black trades. We recapture.} 11.axb4 11...f5 {Of course, they'll push ...f5. But as in the variation on move 8, we should focus on our own queenside attack rather than react to Black's not-so-dangerous one. Let's attack the knight on c6.} 12.b5 12...Ne7 {It jumps to e7... And we've got another active move with the queen. There could be some fireworks on the diagonal.} 13.Qb3 13...Kh8 {That's why Black sidesteps with ...Kh8. Usually, we would develop our bishop to a3. But since the a-file has opened up it might be safer to activate it to b2 instead.} 14.Bb2 14...h6 {Pushing ...h6 is always useful for Black to get some breathing room and prevent our knight from jumping to g5. Also, they might want to push ...g5 at some point to expand on the kingside! We'll continue playing on the queenside. Let's offer a trade of rooks, seeking to gain control of the open a-file.} 15.Ra1 15...Rb8 {They slide to b8 instead. We've captured the a-file now, and we'll place the rook deep into Black's territory with Ra7. Black's strong suit is the kingside here.} 16.Ra7 16...g5 {And that's where they'll advance, this time gaining space with ...g5. They'd love to push one of their pawns to continue the attack.} 17.Nd2 {But we'll just play Nd2 to prevent them pushing with tempo. On the queenside, we'd love to double up and play Ra8, while pushing c5 and b6! It's hard for Black to continue the attack as the pawns are semi-frozen. For example ...f4 will surrender the wonderful e4-outpost to our knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {The most popular move for Black here is to directly prevent our b4 idea with ...a5. However, a lot of people won't play that move, especially in faster time controls. We'll insist on playing b4, with a3! This is the most consistent approach.} 9.a3 9...h6 {The most popular here is ...h6, controlling the g5-square. Black often wants to develop the light-squared bishop to e6 and doesn't want our knight attacking it. We'll proceed with our planned b4.} 10.b4 10...axb4 {Black captures... And we'll take back, naturally. The a-file is now open, but we're threatening b5.} 11.axb4 11...Be6 {And ...Be6, the most common approach here from Black, does nothing to stop it. We'll simply continue with b5.} 12.b5 12...Ne7 {Black retreats to e7... And we'll develop the bishop to b2. Activating it to a3 might generally be better, but when Black has played ...a5 and opened up the a-file, we can't develop it there.} 13.Bb2 13...Qd7 {Another common idea by Black, creating a battery. Black wants ...Bh3, trading the light-squared bishop. We'll fight for the a-file with Ra1. Re1 was also logical to anticipate ...Bh3 but it seems like we can ignore the bishop trade here.} 14.Ra1 14...Rxa1 {If Black trades, that can only play into our hands. We'll recapture with the queen, of course. Now it can infiltrate on the a-file.} 15.Qxa1 15...Bh3 {We can even allow Black to trade light-squared bishops here. We've got Qa7, targeting the b7-pawn.} 16.Qa7 16...Bxg2 {Black takes on g2. We'll recapture.} 17.Kxg2 17...Qc8 {And the queen retreats now, defending the pawn.} 18.c5 {There have been a lot of trades, but c5 is the key move to claim a small advantage. Our queen is very active, and the rook is coming to c1.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {The most popular move for Black here is to directly prevent our b4 idea with ...a5. However, a lot of people won't play that move, especially in faster time controls. We'll insist on playing b4, with a3! This is the most consistent approach.} 9.a3 9...Re8 {You are probably already familiar with this move. Black activates the rook on the e-file and wants to push ...e4. It shouldn't distract us from achieving our queenside plans. Let's push forward with b4.} 10.b4 10...axb4 {Black trades. And we'll take back.} 11.axb4 11...e4 {Black plays their intended ...e4 push. We don't mind the trades in the center in these positions. Our queenside potential remains.} 12.dxe4 12...Nxe4 {Black takes. We'll trade again.} 13.Nxe4 13...Rxe4 {Black captures again. We've got time for b5 now.} 14.b5 14...Nd4 {The knight is under attack, but it doesn't have to retreat to e7 as usual. It jumps to d4. We'll trade.} 15.Nxd4 15...Rxd4 {Black recaptures with the rook, hitting our queen. We'll play the natural Qb3.} 16.Qb3 16...Be6 {If Black absent-mindedly develops the bishop to e6, hitting our c4-pawn... We can actually give it up to capture on b7!} 17.Bxb7 17...Bxc4 {Black takes on c4, so we don't have time to take the rook on a8. Let's slide to f3. The rook is still under attack on a8.} 18.Qf3 18...Ra2 {Black brings it to a2, attacking our e2-pawn. But we've got a great countermeasure.} 19.Bb2 {We'll block their attack with Bb2! The bishop also attacks the rook on d4 and the bishop behind it. Black could be in a lot of trouble here. The computer already suggests it's best for them to sacrifice the exchange with ...Rxb2.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {The most popular move for Black here is to directly prevent our b4 idea with ...a5. However, a lot of people won't play that move, especially in faster time controls. We'll insist on playing b4, with a3! This is the most consistent approach.} 9.a3 9...h6 {The most popular here is ...h6, controlling the g5-square. Black often wants to develop the light-squared bishop to e6 and doesn't want our knight attacking it. We'll proceed with our planned b4.} 10.b4 10...axb4 {Black captures... And we'll take back, naturally. The a-file is now open, but we're threatening b5.} 11.axb4 11...Be6 {And ...Be6, the most common approach here from Black, does nothing to stop it. We'll simply continue with b5.} 12.b5 12...Ne7 {Black retreats to e7... And we'll develop the bishop to b2. Activating it to a3 might generally be better, but when Black has played ...a5 and opened up the a-file, we can't develop it there.} 13.Bb2 13...Qd7 {Another common idea by Black, creating a battery. Black wants ...Bh3, trading the light-squared bishop. We'll fight for the a-file with Ra1. Re1 was also logical to anticipate ...Bh3 but it seems like we can ignore the bishop trade here.} 14.Ra1 14...Bh3 {Black goes for the trade. We'll trade rooks instead.} 15.Rxa8 15...Rxa8 {Once Black recaptures... We've got Qb3. The point is to free up the rook to slide to a1.} 16.Qb3 16...Bxg2 {Black finally chops off the bishop.} 17.Kxg2 {We'll recapture. We're going to trade off the rooks and then try to invade down the a-file with our queen. Black doesn't have much control of the light squares so we have ideas such as Nd5 and Nd2-e4. We can also push e4 first to support Nd5. Another plan to look out for is to push for b6 or c5 (or d4) if it can open up the position favorably.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...Nh5 {You could also see ...Nh5. The point is that Black wants the quick ...f5, which is Black's main pawn break in the KID. We should push b4 without delay.} 9.b4 9...f5 {Black pushes forward as well. But we are quicker with b5!} 10.b5 10...Ne7 {Once the knight retreats... The Qb3 idea is useful to remember. We line up the queen with Black's king and threaten some potential discoveries.} 11.Qb3 11...Kh8 {Black slides to h8 to evade this threat. The next piece we should develop is the dark-squared bishop. We'll activate it where we're the strongest: on the queenside! On a3, the bishop monitors the c5-square and the d6-pawn. We want to push c5.} 12.Ba3 12...f4 {If Black is aggressive with ...f4, we can actually keep ignoring them! Let's push c5. Black's d6-pawn is under pressure, and playing for ideas like Ng5-Nf7+ is possible.} 13.c5 13...Nf5 {Black improves their knight... And we'll take on d6.} 14.cxd6 14...cxd6 {Black recaptures, and now their d6-pawn is weak. And the opponents' weak points should be attacked!} 15.Ne4 {That's what we'll do with Ne4. The pawn on d6 is weak and pinned. There are still ideas of placing a knight on g5 and threatening Nf7+. White is close to winning here.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {The most popular move for Black here is to directly prevent our b4 idea with ...a5. However, a lot of people won't play that move, especially in faster time controls. We'll insist on playing b4, with a3! This is the most consistent approach.} 9.a3 9...Nd4 {In this line, Black logically relocates the knight before the b-file steamroller arrives. The knight is good on d4. We'll remain consistent with our plan although Nxd4 and Nd2 were possible alternatives.} 10.b4 10...axb4 {It's convenient for Black to trade, as they open up the rook. We recapture, still intending b5.} 11.axb4 11...c6 {Black can play ...c6. Should this stop our plan? No! We control the b5-square with everything we've got on the queenside.} 12.b5 12...Bg4 {As per usual, Black can't do much about the queenside. The KID is mostly Black playing on the kingside! Let's capture on c6.} 13.bxc6 13...bxc6 {Black recaptures, and now we've got the b-file. However, there's some pressure on the knight on f3. Let's relieve it by trading on d4, unleashing an attack on the c6-pawn.} 14.Nxd4 14...exd4 {We can't capture it right away though, because our knight is under fire. Let's centralize it and offer another trade.} 15.Ne4 15...Nxe4 {Black takes. We'll take back with an attack on the c6-pawn. Which bishop would you say is better, ours on e4, having an actual target or Black's on g7, staring at its own pawn?} 16.Bxe4 16...Qd7 {Black has to defend. Their main idea here is ...d5. We'll activate the rook, since there isn't too much we can do to prevent it.} 17.Re1 17...d5 {Black pushes in the center. Let's take, naturally. We want to keep their d-pawns doubled.} 18.cxd5 18...cxd5 {Black recaptures, still with tempo. I suggest we offer a trade here, as Black has restricted our light-squared bishop anyway.} 19.Bf3 19...Bxf3 {They will probably trade.} 20.exf3 {And we'll recapture, of course. After all is said and done, the position is simplified but our king will be very safe. Black's doubled pawns are more vulnerable than ours, and we have the better minor piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {The most popular move for Black here is to directly prevent our b4 idea with ...a5. However, a lot of people won't play that move, especially in faster time controls. We'll insist on playing b4, with a3! This is the most consistent approach.} 9.a3 9...Re8 {You are probably already familiar with this move. Black activates the rook on the e-file and wants to push ...e4. It shouldn't distract us from achieving our queenside plans. Let's push forward with b4.} 10.b4 10...axb4 {Black trades. And we'll take back.} 11.axb4 11...e4 {Black plays their intended ...e4 push. We don't mind the trades in the center in these positions. Our queenside potential remains.} 12.dxe4 12...Nxe4 {Black takes. We'll trade again.} 13.Nxe4 13...Rxe4 {Black captures again. We've got time for b5 now.} 14.b5 14...Nd4 {The knight is under attack, but it doesn't have to retreat to e7 as usual. It jumps to d4. We'll trade.} 15.Nxd4 15...Rxd4 {Black recaptures with the rook, hitting our queen. We'll play the natural Qb3.} 16.Qb3 16...Bf5 {The best developing square for the bishop is f5, hitting our rook. But we can give up the exchange with Bb2, counter-attacking the black rook! Our bishop on b2 is strong.} 17.Bb2 17...Bxb1 {Black accepts the rook offer, and we won't even capture on d4 right away.} 18.Rxb1 {We'll just recapture on b1. We will regain our exchange soon and the powerful light-squared bishop continues to give us small nagging pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...h6 {8...h6 is the fourth most popular move in this position. Black doesn't want our pieces coming to g5. That's fine, we'll still expand on the queenside!} 9.b4 9...Be6 {Black activates the bishop, and Black proves the point of ...h6. There's no Ng5 now. But there is still b5!} 10.b5 10...Ne7 {Black retreats to e7. And we'll jump to d2. Our bishop is unleashed with an attack on the b7-pawn. Black's got two real ways of defending it, since c6 weakens d6.} 11.Nd2 11...Qc8 {A better, multipurpose way of defending the b7-pawn is ...Qc8. Black lines up the bishop with the queen and wants to trade light-squared bishops in the future. Let's play Re1 and preserve our powerful bishop on g2.} 12.Re1 12...Bh3 {The most aggressive approach from Black, going all out at our king. We are objectively better but it will take accurate play to beat back Black's ideas while progressing on the queenside. We'll retain our bishop with Bh1.} 13.Bh1 13...Ng4 {Black jumps forward with the knight, hitting some weak spots in our camp and planning tricks with the ...e4 pawn push. Now Qb3 comes in handy, defending the knight. Also, if the f-pawn moves, there could be a discovered check lurking with c5.} 14.Qb3 14...Kh7 {Black plays the prophylactic ...Kh7 to reintroduce possibilities of ...f5.} 15.Ba3 {We develop naturally with Ba3, supporting a c5 break. 15...Qf5 looks dangerous, but we will defend with Nd1. Queen to f5 also loses contact of the b7 pawn, so we can take it and then fall back to f3 or h1.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...Re8 {8...Re8 is only the sixth most popular move in the position. Black wants to push ...e4 and create trouble in the center and on the diagonal. We'll play b4 anyway.} 9.b4 9...e4 {Black forces ...e4. But we'll still be completely fine. We'll capture on e4.} 10.dxe4 10...Nxe4 {Black recaptures. Let's trade once again.} 11.Nxe4 11...Rxe4 {Black takes back. We've forced some trades, and now we've got time for b5!} 12.b5 12...Na5 {The knight jumps to a5, attacking the c4-pawn. We can't really push it. But we can defend and attack the rook at the same time. Where should the rook go?} 13.Nd2 13...Re8 {Well, the e8-square seems the safest. The rook shouldn't wander in the center this early.} 14.Qa4 {Now we can activate the queen, hit the knight and defend the pawn. Again, our bishop is very active and White is slightly better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 1: The Classical Setup (...Nc6 and ...Nf6) / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/0e4c0cbf-5654-4308-9854-db35494c2831/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. They want the King's Indian setup with ...g6 and ...Bg7. Having moved the c-pawn, we'll develop behind it with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We have similar ideas, going for a fianchetto of our own.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {And both bishops get developed. Our bishop stands strong on g2.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {In the KID, Black's first 4 moves almost always look like this. The king is safely castled. We'll play d3 and reinforce our light-square control.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. It's natural to prepare the ...e5-push. We'll develop the knight and prepare to castle, awaiting ...e5.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {And Black plays it. We'll castle. Although we are playing "less ambitiously" with a d3 setup instead of d4, we have a couple of advantages to our setup. We have great light square control with our bishop on g2, while our plan is very simple and straightforward with a queenside pawn storm.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {The most popular move in this position is ...Nc6, developing the knight. It's played 41% of the time, which is around 120 thousand games. The knight will jump to d4 in some variations and controls the b4-square. We'll prepare b4 with Rb1.} 8.Rb1 8...Be6 {8...Be6 is fairly popular and natural, but it does absolutely nothing to stop our plans. With b4, we'll create a strong pawn storm, and we're not faced with any threats. A good alternative here was also Ng5, to hit the bishop right away.} 9.b4 9...a6 {Black wants to take control of the b5-square, preventing the immediate b5 by us. But we can use our a-pawn to help out.} 10.a4 10...Qd7 {Black improves the queen, and we don't need to hurry with b5 right away. There might be threats of ...Bh3.} 11.Ng5 {We can jump to g5 and hit the bishop first, also controlling the h3-square. Our bishop is great and there's no problems.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Nh5 {7...Nh5 is only played 1% of the time, but has an instructive idea behind it. Black wants to make way for f5-f4. We'll push in the center with e4, and take control of the f5 right away. We also allow our queen to eye h5. It blocks our bishop but we can't allow Black's plan to proceed unopposed.} 8.e4 8...f5 {Black could, logically, still try to advance the f-pawn to f5. But this gives White the advantage. We played e4 so we could capture on f5.} 9.exf5 9...Bxf5 {Recapturing with the bishop and activating it makes more sense. Let's attack with Ng5, a multipurpose move! The queen sees the knight on h5 and the pawn on b7 is under attack.} 10.Ng5 10...c6 {Black protects against the most urgent threat which was the attack on the b7-pawn. But we aren't done creating threats. Let's play Qb3, attacking b7 again, and planning a discovery.} 11.Qb3 11...Qd7 {If Black defends the b7-pawn... We've got this discovered check. Capturing on d6 would be an amazing positional benefit for us.} 12.c5+ 12...d5 {That's why Black pushes ...d5 and blocks, thinking they've solved all their problems. We break through by sacrificing the knight!} 13.Nxd5 13...cxd5 {Black has to capture. We've already regained two pawns for the piece, and we'll capture on b7 as well.} 14.Bxd5+ 14...Kh8 {The king has to step to h8. We'll capture on b7 and trap the rook!} 15.Bxb7 15...Nc6 {The best they can do is activate the knight... And we'll take, acquiring a rook and three pawns for the two minor pieces. Not the worst of trades!} 16.Bxa8 16...Nd4 {Before recapturing, Black has an improving move in the center to hit our queen.} 17.Qd5 {And we'll offer a trade in the center. Black will probably get the bishop on a8. Nevertheless, our position is much better because of the material advantage and the passed c-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Nbd7 {7...Nbd7 is played 11% of the time and seems natural just like 7...Nc6, but is actually a bit passive. Black does little to delay our queenside expansion and might have different plans, namely in the center. We'll expand immediately on the queenside with b4.} 8.b4 8...a5 {Black going for ...a5 now doesn't really do anything. We wanted to push b5 anyway.} 9.b5 9...Nc5 {Black's point was that we give up the c5-square, so they can jump there with the knight. But we are the ones with the space on the queenside.} 10.Nd2 {And we'll try to get rid of their strong piece with Nd2 and Nb3. Our bishop is extremely active now as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Nh5 {7...Nh5 is only played 1% of the time, but has an instructive idea behind it. Black wants to make way for f5-f4. We'll push in the center with e4, and take control of the f5 right away. We also allow our queen to eye h5. It blocks our bishop but we can't allow Black's plan to proceed unopposed.} 8.e4 8...Nc6 {A better move for Black than 8...f5 is activating the knight to c6. This is more solid for Black, but doesn't threaten anything. We can just continue developing with Bg5, provoking f6.} 9.Bg5 9...f6 {Once they play it... We'll just retreat to the next best square, e3.} 10.Be3 10...f5 {And Black advances to f5 now, having grabbed a tempo themselves. Nothing to be afraid of. We'll capture.} 11.exf5 11...Bxf5 {The bishop recaptures. We'd love to fork Black's pieces with g4, so we'll prepare it with h3! Black has to move one of the minor pieces.} 12.h3 12...Nf6 {And they retreat to f6.} 13.Qd2 {After Qd2, there are a lot of instructive ideas in this position. On the kingside, we can advance with g4 and push our knight into g5 at some point. On the queenside, we have ideas of b4 and b5 as usual. In the center, our rook can come to e1.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Nbd7 {7...Nbd7 is played 11% of the time and seems natural just like 7...Nc6, but is actually a bit passive. Black does little to delay our queenside expansion and might have different plans, namely in the center. We'll expand immediately on the queenside with b4.} 8.b4 8...e4 {If Black tries to hit us in the center, we're ready. Let's take.} 9.dxe4 9...Nxe4 {Black recaptures with the knight. Our knight on c3 is pinned and attacked twice. We'll sacrifice the exchange!} 10.Nxe4 10...Bxa1 {Black has won a rook for a piece, but we've got a trick. Let's go for Bg5, attacking the bishop and the queen at the same time. Black blocking ...Bf6 doesn't help their cause as we capture with the knight and continue applying pressure, for example with Qd4.} 11.Bg5 11...f6 {So, they block with the pawn, counter-attacking our bishop.} 12.Bh6 {But now our bishop can slide to h6 and there's no ...Bg7 to block. Both of Black's rook and bishop are under attack. We'll be getting some material back with a much better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Bg4 {It definitely makes sense to activate the light-squared bishop next, namely to g4. However, it doesn't grant Black any edge whatsoever. We'll attack the bishop.} 8.h3 8...Bxf3 {At this point, it only makes sense for Black to capture. We'll recapture with the bishop and attack the b7-pawn. Should Black block with the knight or the pawn?} 9.Bxf3 9...c6 {Blocking with the pawn makes their position more solid.} 10.b4 {We'll try to undermine that structure right away with the instructive b4. Our bishop really is the key piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...c6 {7...c6 is the second most popular move in this position, played 16% of the time. It is logical to build a big center and oppose our bishop on g2. Let's slide our rook to b1, with our standard idea of advancing on the queenside.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {Black going for the setup with ...c6 and ...a5, preventing our b4 idea. This is the most solid approach for them. We'll play a3, and keep trying for b4.} 9.a3 9...Re8 {Black is eager to let us push b4 if they get ...d5 in exchange. Now the e5-pawn is protected. Let's pin the knight, which helps support ...d5 and ...e4.} 10.Bg5 10...h6 {Black attacks our bishop. We take. We don't mind giving up this bishop since the knight was useful for Black's central play.} 11.Bxf6 11...Bxf6 {The bishop recaptures. We'll redirect our knight to d2. Having solved the problem of Black pushing ...d5, we can finally focus on our queenside expansion. Our d-knight could also jump to e4 at some point.} 12.Nd2 12...Bg7 {The bishop goes back to g7.} 13.Qc2 {And we'll make sure the knight on c3 is well protected and there are no ...e4 tactics. The position is roughly balanced but we have great light-square control and a straightforward b4-b5 bulldozer.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Re8 {7...Re8 is a challenging approach by Black, played 5% of the time. Black wants to advance in the center, and prepares it with the rook. Rb1 makes sense, getting off the long diagonal before it opens up, and also preparing b4.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {Black stops b4 with ...a5 before proceeding with potential center operations. But we are persistent and play a3! An alternative to this move was e4 to grab more central control.} 9.a3 9...c6 {Black prepares ...d5 now. We don't have to go b4 immediately, we can address Black's threats first. Now Bg5 transposes into a line we've already seen.} 10.Bg5 10...h6 {Black attacks our bishop. We take. We don't mind giving up this bishop since the knight was useful for Black's central play.} 11.Bxf6 11...Bxf6 {The bishop recaptures. We'll redirect our knight to d2. Having solved the problem of Black pushing ...d5, we can finally focus on our queenside expansion. Our d-knight could also jump to e4 at some point.} 12.Nd2 12...Bg7 {The bishop goes back to g7.} 13.Qc2 {And we'll make sure the knight on c3 is well protected and there are no ...e4 tactics. The position is roughly balanced but we have great light-square control and a straightforward b4-b5 bulldozer.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Re8 {7...Re8 is a challenging approach by Black, played 5% of the time. Black wants to advance in the center, and prepares it with the rook. Rb1 makes sense, getting off the long diagonal before it opens up, and also preparing b4.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {Black stops b4 with ...a5 before proceeding with potential center operations. But we are persistent and play a3! An alternative to this move was e4 to grab more central control.} 9.a3 9...e4 {If Black is aggressive with ...e4, we don't have to capture and let them activate their pieces. We'll retreat the knight to e1, and encourage Black to exchange in the center.} 10.Ne1 10...exd3 {Black takes on d3.} 11.Nxd3 {And we'll recapture conveniently with the knight. Our bishop comes to life now.} *

