1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Re8 {7...Re8 is a challenging approach by Black, played 5% of the time. Black wants to advance in the center, and prepares it with the rook. Rb1 makes sense, getting off the long diagonal before it opens up, and also preparing b4.} 8.Rb1 8...a5 {Black stops b4 with ...a5 before proceeding with potential center operations. But we are persistent and play a3! An alternative to this move was e4 to grab more central control.} 9.a3 9...Nc6 {Black going for ...Nc6 on move 9 transposes to the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 2: Black's Alternatives to 7...Nc6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/627228de-d724-4e12-ae58-fd28cb8d91c8/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for ...Nf6. We'll activate the knight, aiming for g3 next.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black wants to fianchetto. We'll prepare to do likewise.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black develops... And so will we.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {Time for Black to castle. With d3, we'll connect the pawn chain on the light squares. Our dark-squared bishop is now ready to come into the game.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. They want ...e5, of course. We'll develop the kingside knight and prepare castling.} 6.Nf3 6...e5 {Black strikes in the center. And we'll castle.} 7.O-O 7...Nh5 {7...Nh5 is only played 1% of the time, but has an instructive idea behind it. Black wants to make way for f5-f4. We'll push in the center with e4, and take control of the f5 right away. We also allow our queen to eye h5. It blocks our bishop but we can't allow Black's plan to proceed unopposed.} 8.e4 8...f5 {Black could, logically, still try to advance the f-pawn to f5. But this gives White the advantage. We played e4 so we could capture on f5.} 9.exf5 9...gxf5 {Black recapturing with the g-pawn is a mistake. Here's why the queen being on the d1-h5 diagonal is important.} 10.Nxe5 {We can sacrifice the knight in the center. If Black takes we'll regain the piece immediately. This is a winning position for White.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/b795c04d-0440-4357-a4b3-abde22ec5079/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...e5 {Black could try to surprise us here and go for the quick ...e5, although it is played extremely rarely in the database, less than 1%. Still, it's positionally instructive how to play against this. We'll push d4 and open the position.} 4.d4 4...d6 {Supporting the e5-pawn with the d6-pawn is a mistake that leads to a forcing line, in which we get the advantage. It's played only 12% of the time here. We'll take on e5.} 5.dxe5 5...dxe5 {Black has to recapture, and now the queens see each other. We'll trade them off.} 6.Qxd8+ 6...Kxd8 {The king recaptures. And now the pin on the knight is powerful. We'd love to play something like Nd5 to keep applying pressure.} 7.Bg5 7...Be7 {Not when Black unpins immediately, though. We'll just develop the kingside knight and hit the pawn in the center.} 8.Nf3 8...Nbd7 {It's more solid for Black to defend with the knight on d7 than with the knight on c6. However, you can see that Black's pieces are stuck together on the back ranks. Let's pin that knight as well with Rd1!} 9.Rd1 9...Ke8 {Black moves the king away and unpins, but it doesn't help them a whole lot.} 10.Nb5 {That gives us a new front of attack, threatening a fork. Black is probably even lost here.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/b795c04d-0440-4357-a4b3-abde22ec5079/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black completes the fianchetto 91% of the time, so be prepared to see this move by far the most often. We'll also fianchetto.} 4.Bg2 4...e5 {Black could go for ...e5 here as well, although it's played only 1% of the time. We'll challenge that pawn immediately.} 5.d4 5...exd4 {Black will most likely capture. And we'll take back. The difference with the bishop on g7 is that Black can now castle and avoid the uncomfortable check on e3.} 6.Qxd4 6...Nc6 {Again, if Black attacks our queen... We'll just slide to e3, with check.} 7.Qe3+ 7...Qe7 {Once the queen blocks...} 8.Nb5 {We've got the potent Nb5 once again. If Black's queen leaves d8, this move is often strong to threaten a fork on c7.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/b795c04d-0440-4357-a4b3-abde22ec5079/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...e5 {Black could try to surprise us here and go for the quick ...e5, although it is played extremely rarely in the database, less than 1%. Still, it's positionally instructive how to play against this. We'll push d4 and open the position.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {The most reasonable move is for Black to capture, which they do 71% of the time (173 games). We'll recapture. The alternative of pushing ...e4 is a mistake after 5.Bg5 pinning the knight.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nc6 {One could argue that the most logical move for Black is to develop and attack our queen. But the check on e3 is a big part of plans to ruin Black's coordination.} 6.Qe3+ 6...Qe7 {As Black wants to fianchetto, they'll probably block with the queen.} 7.Bg2 {We don't have to trade ourselves. Let's take a move to develop and prepare to recapture with the bishop if needed.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/b795c04d-0440-4357-a4b3-abde22ec5079/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black completes the fianchetto 91% of the time, so be prepared to see this move by far the most often. We'll also fianchetto.} 4.Bg2 4...e5 {Black could go for ...e5 here as well, although it's played only 1% of the time. We'll challenge that pawn immediately.} 5.d4 5...d6 {Once again, ...d6 here is not the best idea. Black simply surrenders to an inferior position from the get-go. Let's capture in the center.} 6.dxe5 6...dxe5 {Black recaptures. And we'll trade queens.} 7.Qxd8+ 7...Kxd8 {The king must capture. In the last line where a similar position happened, I recommended Bg5. Here, the knight on f6 is already defended, so we'll play differently. Let's develop the knight with an attack on the e5-pawn.} 8.Nf3 8...Nbd7 {Black defends. And we've got time to castle.} 9.O-O 9...c6 {9...c6 grabs control of some squares in the center. As previously, we'll pin the knight on d7.} 10.Rd1 10...Kc7 {Once Black unpins...} 11.b3 {11.b3 is a good move here to claim the advantage. We'll get the bishop to b2 and jump to g5 with the knight to attack the f7-pawn. A good idea to keep expanding on the queenside at some point would be b4-b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/b795c04d-0440-4357-a4b3-abde22ec5079/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black completes the fianchetto 91% of the time, so be prepared to see this move by far the most often. We'll also fianchetto.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {And Black castles. ...d6 transposes to positions we cover elsewhere in more depth, e.g. 5.Nf3 0-0 6.0-0 e5 7.d3 and we're back into known territory. 5.d3 keeps our pawns together. Black's got multiple options here, but we'll cover only one of them in this study.} 5.d3 5...c6 {It's the peculiar-looking ...c6. This move is played 29% of the time. Black plays against our bishop and simply wants ...d5 in the center. We'll take the Botvinnik formation against this move, placing the e-pawn in the center, grabbing more space and preventing ...d5. It is of bigger importance to restrict Black's central expansion than to keep our bishop active.} 6.e4 6...d6 {Black has to settle for ...d6. They can change their plans here. Once we activate our knight...} 7.Nge2 7...e5 {Black can go for ...e5 instead of ...d5, which is a flexible approach that keeps the bishop on c8 open for development. We castle, and arrive at the "Botvinnik Formation." This is a possible setup (g3, Bg2, c4, d3, e4, Nge2) against many of Black's options. We are aiming to discourage ...d5, while retaining Rb1 and b4 as an option.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {If Black just develops lazily and traditionally for one move, we know what to do. Let's go for the immediate b4, aiming for b5.} 9.b4 9...a5 {Black challenging us now is "too late". Of course, we won't capture on a5 and open up their rook. We'll push b5 instead, just as we intended. And Black has done nothing to stop us.} 10.b5 10...Nc5 {Yes, we do give up the c5-square. But our aggression on the b-file is what's important to us.} 11.Rb1 11...Be6 {Black activates the bishop, and here's a calm move to demonstrate our plans for the future.} 12.h3 {I like h3 here to grab some more control of the g4-square and tuck the king on h2 at some point. Remember to activate the dark-squared bishop on a3 to x-ray the dark diagonal. Our rook will become a monster on the b-file. All in all, White stands slightly better.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/b795c04d-0440-4357-a4b3-abde22ec5079/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black completes the fianchetto 91% of the time, so be prepared to see this move by far the most often. We'll also fianchetto.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {And Black castles. ...d6 transposes to positions we cover elsewhere in more depth, e.g. 5.Nf3 0-0 6.0-0 e5 7.d3 and we're back into known territory. 5.d3 keeps our pawns together. Black's got multiple options here, but we'll cover only one of them in this study.} 5.d3 5...c6 {It's the peculiar-looking ...c6. This move is played 29% of the time. Black plays against our bishop and simply wants ...d5 in the center. We'll take the Botvinnik formation against this move, placing the e-pawn in the center, grabbing more space and preventing ...d5. It is of bigger importance to restrict Black's central expansion than to keep our bishop active.} 6.e4 6...d6 {Black has to settle for ...d6. They can change their plans here. Once we activate our knight...} 7.Nge2 7...e5 {Black can go for ...e5 instead of ...d5, which is a flexible approach that keeps the bishop on c8 open for development. We castle, and arrive at the "Botvinnik Formation." This is a possible setup (g3, Bg2, c4, d3, e4, Nge2) against many of Black's options. We are aiming to discourage ...d5, while retaining Rb1 and b4 as an option.} 8.O-O 8...a5 {Don't be discouraged if Black directly plays against our queenside expansion. There are still developing and improving moves we can make in the center/on the kingside. First of all, I suggest taking away the g4-square from Black's bishop.} 9.h3 9...Na6 {Black is going to build behind their queenside structure now, planning ...Nc5. They can't stop us on both sides of the board. Time to get the bishop out with Be3.} 10.Be3 10...Nc5 {Black's knight hops into the outpost on c5. It might seem logical to take the knight on c5 and double Black's pawns...} 11.f4 {But we have got other ideas! We'll advance with f4, creating tension in the center. Of course, we are threatening to capture on e5 and then capture the knight. White is better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/b795c04d-0440-4357-a4b3-abde22ec5079/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black completes the fianchetto 91% of the time, so be prepared to see this move by far the most often. We'll also fianchetto.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {And Black castles. ...d6 transposes to positions we cover elsewhere in more depth, e.g. 5.Nf3 0-0 6.0-0 e5 7.d3 and we're back into known territory. 5.d3 keeps our pawns together. Black's got multiple options here, but we'll cover only one of them in this study.} 5.d3 5...c6 {It's the peculiar-looking ...c6. This move is played 29% of the time. Black plays against our bishop and simply wants ...d5 in the center. We'll take the Botvinnik formation against this move, placing the e-pawn in the center, grabbing more space and preventing ...d5. It is of bigger importance to restrict Black's central expansion than to keep our bishop active.} 6.e4 6...d6 {Black has to settle for ...d6. They can change their plans here. Once we activate our knight...} 7.Nge2 7...e5 {Black can go for ...e5 instead of ...d5, which is a flexible approach that keeps the bishop on c8 open for development. We castle, and arrive at the "Botvinnik Formation." This is a possible setup (g3, Bg2, c4, d3, e4, Nge2) against many of Black's options. We are aiming to discourage ...d5, while retaining Rb1 and b4 as an option.} 8.O-O 8...Be6 {One of the most challenging lines arises after 8...Be6. Black happily develops the light-squared bishop, controlling the d5-square for later. They haven't done much to prevent us from achieving our goals on the queenside, however. Now b4 is the most straightforward choice amongst the other options such as 9. h3 or 9. b3.} 9.b4 9...d5 {It's the central ...d5-push! Black creates chaos in the center. We don't want to let them capture and win material/grab the initiative. We'll take once.} 10.exd5 10...cxd5 {Black recaptures, and there's still some tension left, with c4 a target. But instead of dealing with that, I recommend a more venomous move. We'll pin the knight on f6 and threaten to capture on d5! Our bishops are extremely strong.} 11.Bg5 11...dxc4 {Black takes on c4. We'll recapture once. They don't have time to take again because of the hanging b7-pawn.} 12.dxc4 12...Nc6 {...Nc6 is natural for Black.} 13.Qb3 {And as the last move of the line, we'll avoid the queen trade with Qb3. The queen supports our queenside operations and opens up the d1-square for the rook. In the center, Nd5 would be great to continue applying the pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/a50b85ce-63c2-4e02-a213-663a097cd2bc/studies/b795c04d-0440-4357-a4b3-abde22ec5079/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black completes the fianchetto 91% of the time, so be prepared to see this move by far the most often. We'll also fianchetto.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {And Black castles. ...d6 transposes to positions we cover elsewhere in more depth, e.g. 5.Nf3 0-0 6.0-0 e5 7.d3 and we're back into known territory. 5.d3 keeps our pawns together. Black's got multiple options here, but we'll cover only one of them in this study.} 5.d3 5...c6 {It's the peculiar-looking ...c6. This move is played 29% of the time. Black plays against our bishop and simply wants ...d5 in the center. We'll take the Botvinnik formation against this move, placing the e-pawn in the center, grabbing more space and preventing ...d5. It is of bigger importance to restrict Black's central expansion than to keep our bishop active.} 6.e4 6...d6 {Black has to settle for ...d6. They can change their plans here. Once we activate our knight...} 7.Nge2 7...e5 {Black can go for ...e5 instead of ...d5, which is a flexible approach that keeps the bishop on c8 open for development. We castle, and arrive at the "Botvinnik Formation." This is a possible setup (g3, Bg2, c4, d3, e4, Nge2) against many of Black's options. We are aiming to discourage ...d5, while retaining Rb1 and b4 as an option.} 8.O-O 8...a6 {Black could try to counter our queenside plan by "mirroring" it, with ...a6, preparing ...b5. This is an inaccuracy of sorts, however, since we are faster. Let's bulldoze the queenside with b4.} 9.b4 9...b5 {Black expands with ...b5, thinking they've grabbed crucial space and stopped our idea. But we'll put pressure on their queenside. Let's use the b-pawn as a hook with a4! If the a-file opens up, that would be amazing for us.} 10.a4 10...bxc4 {It's likely that Black will capture on c4. We'll take back, of course, and we've gotten rid of the weak backwards d3-pawn.} 11.dxc4 11...Be6 {Black can target the c4-pawn immediately...} 12.Qd3 {But we can defend it with the queen. Although the computer claims equality in this position, I'd argue White's position is much simpler to play.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 10"]
