1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...e5 {Black could try to surprise us here and go for the quick ...e5, although it is played extremely rarely in the database, less than 1%. Still, it's positionally instructive how to play against this. We'll push d4 and open the position.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {The most reasonable move is for Black to capture, which they do 71% of the time (173 games). We'll recapture. The alternative of pushing ...e4 is a mistake after 5.Bg5 pinning the knight.} 5.Qxd4 5...Bg7 {If Black develops and threatens a discovery, we can retreat with tempo. We'll check on e3.} 6.Qe3+ 6...Qe7 {Black basically has to block with the queen. We have a couple of reasonable ways to develop here, but I'll suggest a funny-looking move.} 7.Nh3 {Let's jump to h3, with the idea of Nf4 and Nfd5, if possible. Our bishop will activate to g2 and we don't mind a queen trade as we'll recapture with the bishop, of course.} *

[Event "Chapter 8: The King's Indian Defense / Study 3: The Botvinnik Setup / Variation 5"]
