1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black goes for 1...Nf6. The King's Indian formation includes the fianchetto of the dark-squared bishop, quick castling and often pawns on the dark squares like ...d6 and ...e5. We'll just continue developing here with Nc3.} 2.Nc3 2...g6 {Black prepares the fianchetto. We'll do the same. Black won't be the only one with a strong bishop.} 3.g3 3...Bg7 {Black completes the fianchetto 91% of the time, so be prepared to see this move by far the most often. We'll also fianchetto.} 4.Bg2 4...O-O {And Black castles. ...d6 transposes to positions we cover elsewhere in more depth, e.g. 5.Nf3 0-0 6.0-0 e5 7.d3 and we're back into known territory. 5.d3 keeps our pawns together. Black's got multiple options here, but we'll cover only one of them in this study.} 5.d3 5...c6 {It's the peculiar-looking ...c6. This move is played 29% of the time. Black plays against our bishop and simply wants ...d5 in the center. We'll take the Botvinnik formation against this move, placing the e-pawn in the center, grabbing more space and preventing ...d5. It is of bigger importance to restrict Black's central expansion than to keep our bishop active.} 6.e4 6...d6 {Black has to settle for ...d6. They can change their plans here. Once we activate our knight...} 7.Nge2 7...e5 {Black can go for ...e5 instead of ...d5, which is a flexible approach that keeps the bishop on c8 open for development. We castle, and arrive at the "Botvinnik Formation." This is a possible setup (g3, Bg2, c4, d3, e4, Nge2) against many of Black's options. We are aiming to discourage ...d5, while retaining Rb1 and b4 as an option.} 8.O-O 8...Nh5 {We might come across ...Nh5 here. The idea is to free up the f-pawn, trying to play ...f5. After all, it is the main pawn break for Black in the KID. We are unconcerned and simply continue with our plan.} 9.Rb1 9...f5 {Once Black pushes... We'll take here. This opens up our bishop on g2 again and prevents Black from acquiring too much space in the center.} 10.exf5 10...gxf5 {Also, their king position is now a touch weakened.} 11.b4 {Let's continue with our queenside plan, aiming for b5. White has an advantage here, but be prepared to face a sharp position with a lot of fireworks. Often times, Black will look to play ...f4 here, to which we can answer with something like Bf3.} *

