[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black will mirror us 25% of the time. How do we break this chain of identical moves being played on both sides? We'll play Qd2! We want to play b3, fianchetto the bishop on b2 and make sure it's protected by the queen on d2.} 6.Qd2 6...Nf6 {The most common move at this point is the natural 6...Nf6. Black wants to castle and develop traditionally. Let's demonstrate our setup in a line where both sides simply develops to the optimal squares. We'll play b3.} 7.b3 7...O-O {Black castles. And we'll fianchetto. There could be some tension on the long diagonal soon!} 8.Bb2 8...Bd7 {Quicker than ...b6 and ...Bb7 is ...Bd7, getting the bishop out from its home square. Qc8 and Bh3 is one idea. The e2-square is usually more comfortable for the knight here, since the bishop is left active and the knight still controls the center, so we'll play e3.} 9.e3 9...a6 {An idea Black could have is queenside expansion and aggression, so be prepared to face that. Black wants to push ...b5. We'll continue to develop.} 10.Nge2 10...Rb8 {They'll prepare it with ...Rb8. But we're okay with them pushing ...b5. Let's castle and prepare to fight against it.} 11.O-O 11...b5 {Black pushes on the queenside. We'll simply centralize the a-rook. If Black takes, we'll recapture with the d-pawn and have an open d-file for us.} 12.Rad1 12...Qc8 {They could go for the aforementioned ...Qc8 idea. But we have more important ideas than dealing with that right away. I like centralizing the knight here and offering some trades.} 13.Nd5 13...Nxd5 {If Black takes in the center, we don't have to hurry and capture the bishop. In fact, we'd like Black to capture us on b2! We'll recapture in the center first, hitting the knight.} 14.cxd5 14...Nd8 {They retreat to d8... And now we can relinquish the bishop tension. The idea is that we can get rid of our doubled d-pawns by pushing d4.} 15.Bxg7 15...Kxg7 {Once the king captures...} 16.d4 {That's exactly what we'll do. Black capturing on d4 would most certainly play in our favor. This line actually follows the game between A.Rakhmanov and J.Sarkar in 2013, which White won. All in all, White is better here with more activity and the key d4-pawn break.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black will mirror us 25% of the time. How do we break this chain of identical moves being played on both sides? We'll play Qd2! We want to play b3, fianchetto the bishop on b2 and make sure it's protected by the queen on d2.} 6.Qd2 6...Qd7 {If they do continue copying us, we've got an answer for that as well. And one of the lines includes a game of none other than Magnus Carlsen. We'll play b3 and go for our planned setup.} 7.b3 7...b6 {Black will do the same, of course. We'll fianchetto, and the queen protects the bishop.} 8.Bb2 8...Bb7 {And so will Black. We'll centralize the knight! To break the copying, we offer a trade of dark-squared bishops. Black going for ...Nd4 now isn't the best.} 9.Nd5 9...Bxb2 {In the game between Magnus Carlsen and Dmitry Jakovenko in 2019, Jakovenko captured on b2. That's already a slight mistake, as Black is going to have to react to some threats now. The queen recaptured, hitting the rook on h8.} 10.Qxb2 10...e5 {Jakovenko blocked with ...e5... But had to face f4 right away. The e5-pawn was pinned to the rook.} 11.f4 11...O-O-O {However, he just brought the king to safety, which was the best move. Magnus activated the knight, preparing castling. There was more pressure on e5 now, and he was threatening to capture.} 12.Nf3 12...f6 {Which provoked ...f6 from Jakovenko, weakening his structure even more.} 13.O-O {Magnus castled here, and you can see how appealing White's position is. He went on to win the game in a pretty convincing fashion.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black will mirror us 25% of the time. How do we break this chain of identical moves being played on both sides? We'll play Qd2! We want to play b3, fianchetto the bishop on b2 and make sure it's protected by the queen on d2.} 6.Qd2 6...Qd7 {If they do continue copying us, we've got an answer for that as well. And one of the lines includes a game of none other than Magnus Carlsen. We'll play b3 and go for our planned setup.} 7.b3 7...b6 {Black will do the same, of course. We'll fianchetto, and the queen protects the bishop.} 8.Bb2 8...Bb7 {And so will Black. We'll centralize the knight! To break the copying, we offer a trade of dark-squared bishops. Black going for ...Nd4 now isn't the best.} 9.Nd5 9...e5 {It's better for Black to avoid the trade and block with ...e5. However, we can immediately apply pressure against that e5-pawn now! And we'll do that with f4. The pawn is pinned to the bishop.} 10.f4 10...Nge7 {If Black develops the knight, they can castle either way. That doesn't change our setup, however.} 11.Nh3 {We'll still go for Nh3, with plans of castling and going for e3. Positionally speaking, White is better here. From a human standpoint, it's probably not best for Black to keep mirroring you with 6...Qd7!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 16"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...e5 {5...e5 is played only 7% of the time, but it's definitely a move worth considering. This gives us full control of the d5-square. Black has blocked their dark-squared bishop for a moment, and thus we'll try to expand on the queenside.} 6.a3 6...Nge7 {The knight activates to e7. And we'll get the rook off the diagonal and prepare b4 once again. If we put it into motion, we'll gain a lot of space and challenge Black's unstable structure.} 7.Rb1 7...a5 {That's why ...a5 is a good idea, designed to prevent b4. Remember the d5-square we are controlling? Well, the knight on e7 is the only piece Black has that controls that square. Let's pin it. Black might find themselves tied down here.} 8.Bg5 8...O-O {If Black doesn't play ...f6 and let's us keep the pin, we aren't just going to keep it in place. Let's actually capture on e7.} 9.Bxe7 9...Nxe7 {Black recaptures with the knight to keep some control of the d5-square.} 10.e3 {And I'd recommend e3 here. We can still place a knight on d5 in the future, while developing naturally and looking for that b4-break. White is slightly better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 15"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...e5 {5...e5 is played only 7% of the time, but it's definitely a move worth considering. This gives us full control of the d5-square. Black has blocked their dark-squared bishop for a moment, and thus we'll try to expand on the queenside.} 6.a3 6...Nge7 {The knight activates to e7. And we'll get the rook off the diagonal and prepare b4 once again. If we put it into motion, we'll gain a lot of space and challenge Black's unstable structure.} 7.Rb1 7...a5 {That's why ...a5 is a good idea, designed to prevent b4. Remember the d5-square we are controlling? Well, the knight on e7 is the only piece Black has that controls that square. Let's pin it. Black might find themselves tied down here.} 8.Bg5 8...f6 {A sound chance to escape is ...f6, kicking our bishop away. But it always weakens the king and blocks the bishop on g7. Let's just retreat safely to d2.} 9.Bd2 9...O-O {Once Black castles...} 10.e3 {We'll simply continue developing. The knight is coming to e2, and we are going to castle short. In the future, still trying to play for b4 would be a good idea.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {5...Nf6 is the most popular move in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, played 35% of the time. We'll play Qd2. We want to play b3 and Bb2, and the queen from d2 will cover the bishop on b2.} 6.Qd2 6...O-O {Black castles. They can't continue mirroring our moves. Let's continue with our setup.} 7.b3 7...e6 {The most challenging is for Black to prepare ...d5 with ...e6. Let's uncork our anti-e6 idea. We'll actually capture on c6! The knight is removed from controlling the d4-square.} 8.Bxc6 8...bxc6 {A natural recapture is ...bxc6, recapturing towards the center, still intending ...d5. Having traded our light-squared bishop, we play to control the light squares with our pawns, starting with f3! They will minimize the influence of Black's light-squared bishop. Also, our dark-squared bishop can roam free between the pawns.} 9.f3 9...d5 {Black strikes in the center.} 10.Nh3 {And I suggest we activate the knight to h3. It can reroute to f2 from where it's controlling the center. Having doubled Black's c-pawns, it would be a great idea to target the weak c5-pawn with something like Na4.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...e6 {Just like 5...d6, 5...e6 is played a quarter of the time, aiming for a future ...d5. As in the 5...Nf6 line, there's an important idea to remember here. We take on c6! We intend to bury Black's bishop on c8. An alternative in our style is 6. h4!?.} 6.Bxc6 6...bxc6 {If Black recaptures with the b-pawn... We'll keep expanding on the dark squares, controlling e5 and preventing expansion by Black. The light-squared bishop is completely stuck and useless behind its own pawns!} 7.f4 7...Ne7 {The best they can do is continue to develop and castle. Of course, ...d5 is the main pawn break for them. In the meantime, we've got time for e4 as well! Now two of our pawns control the d5-square.} 8.e4 8...O-O {Black castles out of the way. Having achieved what we wanted, we'll just develop.} 9.Nf3 9...d5 {Even if Black plays ...d5, there's no need to capture and open up the position. That only helps Black's bishop break free.} 10.O-O {We'll just castle and laugh in the bishop's face.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black will mirror us 25% of the time. How do we break this chain of identical moves being played on both sides? We'll play Qd2! We want to play b3, fianchetto the bishop on b2 and make sure it's protected by the queen on d2.} 6.Qd2 6...e6 {Black going for 6...e6 looks natural, but it blocks the light-squared bishop. And Black can't go for ...d5 as that would simply lose a pawn. As planned, we'll continue with our setup, and play b3.} 7.b3 7...Nge7 {Black develops and prepares castling. We'll fianchetto...} 8.Bb2 8...O-O {And they'll castle. We've got a real, non-mirroring middlegame on our hands. I suggest h4 here, expanding on the kingside and trying to push h5. Since we haven't castled and Black has, if the h-file opens up we might get a nice attack.} 9.h4 9...h6 {Black might not commit to ...h5 and play ...h6, with more control of the g5-square. The idea is that they want to push ...g5 in case we go h5 now. That stops our plans for the moment, but we'll just carry on developing with Nf3.} 10.Nf3 10...b6 {It's convenient for Black to prepare a fianchetto here... But we aren't afraid of a four-bishop fianchetto. We'll just castle to safety.} 11.O-O 11...Bb7 {Black activates the bishop to b7. 12. e3 is next, freeing up the e2-square for the knight. Our idea is to trade the dark-squared bishops if possible, since that would make it much easier to attack the king. Removing your opponent's defenders is key in chess!} 12.e3 12...d5 {If Black strikes in the center, that's no problem. We'll take once.} 13.cxd5 13...exd5 {Once they recapture...} 14.Ne2 {I like Ne2, creating tension between the bishops before Black can continue pushing or do anything else. If they take on b2, our queen will be happy to recapture and take the diagonal.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...Nf6 {5...Nf6 is the most popular move in the Lichess database under 2000 ELO, played 35% of the time. We'll play Qd2. We want to play b3 and Bb2, and the queen from d2 will cover the bishop on b2.} 6.Qd2 6...O-O {Black castles. They can't continue mirroring our moves. Let's continue with our setup.} 7.b3 7...e6 {The most challenging is for Black to prepare ...d5 with ...e6. Let's uncork our anti-e6 idea. We'll actually capture on c6! The knight is removed from controlling the d4-square.} 8.Bxc6 8...dxc6 {We'd love to see Black recapture with the d-pawn, as it means they've given up on playing ...d5. We'll just continue developing, and fianchetto.} 9.Bb2 9...e5 {Always watch out for ...e5 from Black's side, as it opens up the light-squared bishop.} 10.h3 {We'll continue with h3 to prevent ...Bg4, and might even play g4 ourselves. Not having to worry about ...d5, our knight can develop naturally to f3 and attack the e5-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 17"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nf6 {You could definitely see 4...Nf6 here, as it's played 24% of the time. Already on move 4, Black wants to castle and stops copying us. It's a path for imbalance, and if we are well prepared, we'll be fine. Let's prepare to fianchetto the second bishop.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll get the bishop out.} 6.Bb2 6...Nc6 {The queenside knight activates to c6. I suggest we simply continue developing pieces to reach our intended setup. Black can't do much to stop us.} 7.e3 7...d6 {They open up their bishop as well.} 8.Nge2 {And let's get our knight to e2, not to block the bishop. We want to expand with d4 but it's also possible to play more slowly with d3, Qd2, Rd1 and then go for some d4/e4/f4 expansion later depending on Black's moves. We will see a similar situation in the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black will mirror us 25% of the time. How do we break this chain of identical moves being played on both sides? We'll play Qd2! We want to play b3, fianchetto the bishop on b2 and make sure it's protected by the queen on d2.} 6.Qd2 6...e6 {Black going for 6...e6 looks natural, but it blocks the light-squared bishop. And Black can't go for ...d5 as that would simply lose a pawn. As planned, we'll continue with our setup, and play b3.} 7.b3 7...Nge7 {Black develops and prepares castling. We'll fianchetto...} 8.Bb2 8...O-O {And they'll castle. We've got a real, non-mirroring middlegame on our hands. I suggest h4 here, expanding on the kingside and trying to push h5. Since we haven't castled and Black has, if the h-file opens up we might get a nice attack.} 9.h4 9...h5 {If Black stops us right away, that's fine.} 10.Nf3 {We'll develop the knight to f3, when the g5-square can be used as a great improving jump for our knight or any other piece. In the meantime, we'll castle and play for d4, supported by e3.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...Nc6 {The most popular move for Black in this position is 2...Nc6. It's played almost half the time, with 48%! Black builds behind the c-pawn. We'll continue with the fianchetto.} 3.Bg2 3...e5 {Black plays ...e5, with the idea being to fianchetto and adopt the "Botvinnik setup" that some players may prefer, as they follow a set plan of development. However, Black also creates some tough light square weaknesses. We'll focus on them with Nc3.} 4.Nc3 4...g6 {Black goes for the aforementioned idea, aiming for ...Bg7. Let's push a3, with the simple idea of b4! We want to open up the b-file and unite our rook and light-squared bishop, aiming at the queenside together.} 5.a3 5...Bg7 {If Black just develops, which they will 82% of the time... We'll push b4 as planned.} 6.b4 6...d6 {You might see the passive ...d6 here, keeping the structure together but not really doing anything about our push. We'll support the b-pawn with the rook, also getting it off the dangerous diagonal.} 7.Rb1 7...Nge7 {Black might develop and prepare castling. And we'll strengthen our pawn chain with d3. After grabbing space on the queenside, we are going to focus on development.} 8.d3 8...O-O {Once Black castles...} 9.e3 {We've got e3 to open up the e2-square for the knight. Our bishop on g2 is amazing, as well as our rook on b1. They might become the key trouble makers for Black soon.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...d6 {Black will mirror us 25% of the time. How do we break this chain of identical moves being played on both sides? We'll play Qd2! We want to play b3, fianchetto the bishop on b2 and make sure it's protected by the queen on d2.} 6.Qd2 6...e5 {6...e5 is definitely an another interesting idea by Black. They want to avoid any tension on the dark diagonal and grab more central space, while activating their light-squared bishop. We'll continue with our setup no matter what, and play b3.} 7.b3 7...Nge7 {Black develops naturally and prepares castling... And we'll complete the fianchetto.} 8.Bb2 8...O-O {The king castles. And we'll continue with e3, with the knight probably coming to e2.} 9.e3 9...Be6 {An idea you definitely might face is ...Be6. Black wants to take advantage of their setup and push ...d5. If we lose control of that square, we might get in trouble. That's why we'll shove the knight into d5, not allowing Black to achieve that push!} 10.Nd5 10...Qd7 {With the bishop on e6, Black might try to go for ...Qd7, with the idea of ...Bh3. We'll play h4, and now the rook and the bishop both control the h3-square, cutting out the bishop exchange. In the future, we might consider h5 if we want to start an attack, and if Black lets it.} 11.h4 11...h5 {And to be honest, it's better for them if they don't. Locking any kingside expansion in place keeps Black's king safe.} 12.Ne2 {That's fine, we'll continue developing. Our knight on d5 is the star piece right now, and we'll even accompany it with another knight on c3!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 14"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...e6 {Just like 5...d6, 5...e6 is played a quarter of the time, aiming for a future ...d5. As in the 5...Nf6 line, there's an important idea to remember here. We take on c6! We intend to bury Black's bishop on c8. An alternative in our style is 6. h4!?.} 6.Bxc6 6...dxc6 {If Black captures with the d-pawn, they won't be playing ...d5, but might want to go for ...e5 to open up the bishop on c8. That's why it's important to control the e5-square with a pawn.} 7.f4 7...Ne7 {If Black abstains from the active ...e5-push and chooses to keep their bishop in prison... No problem. Our moves play themselves.} 8.Nf3 8...O-O {Once Black castles, they've done nothing to stop e4.} 9.e4 {So we'll play it. Their bishop is still stuck and we've "won" the opening. However, there's still chess to be played.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...Nc6 {The most popular move for Black in this position is 2...Nc6. It's played almost half the time, with 48%! Black builds behind the c-pawn. We'll continue with the fianchetto.} 3.Bg2 3...e5 {Black plays ...e5, with the idea being to fianchetto and adopt the "Botvinnik setup" that some players may prefer, as they follow a set plan of development. However, Black also creates some tough light square weaknesses. We'll focus on them with Nc3.} 4.Nc3 4...g6 {Black goes for the aforementioned idea, aiming for ...Bg7. Let's push a3, with the simple idea of b4! We want to open up the b-file and unite our rook and light-squared bishop, aiming at the queenside together.} 5.a3 5...a5 {5...a5 is played in only 83 games in the database, and seems logical to stop b4. However, Black completely surrenders control of the b5- and d5-squares, which we will gladly use. Let's go d3 first to open up the dark-squared bishop.} 6.d3 6...Bg7 {Once Black develops their bishop... I suggest immediately jumping in with Nd5. Nothing can challenge our knight for now and if Black goes ...Nf6 we've got Bg5.} 7.Nd5 7...h6 {That's why Black plays ...h6 to take control of the g5-square. We have other ways to develop, and we'll play e3, securing the d4 square, and with the idea of future expansion.} 8.e3 8...Nge7 {Black gets the knight to e7...} 9.Ne2 {And we'll get our knight to e2. We'll transfer it to c3 and still be aiming for the b4-break, with Rb1 to support it. Black has made some severe weaknesses in their position that we'll be able to take advantage of.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 13"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...g6 {Black may fianchetto. This move is played 11% of the time, which is still over 280,000 games. However, it's the most popular move among master games. Of course, we'll finish the fianchetto first.} 3.Bg2 3...Bg7 {And so does Black. We'll get the b1-knight out. Nothing out of the ordinary so far.} 4.Nc3 4...Nc6 {At this point, you can just expect Black to keep copying you. 4...Nc6 is played 54% of the time. A natural move is d3, opening up the bishop. You'll see our idea very soon.} 5.d3 5...e6 {Just like 5...d6, 5...e6 is played a quarter of the time, aiming for a future ...d5. As in the 5...Nf6 line, there's an important idea to remember here. We take on c6! We intend to bury Black's bishop on c8. An alternative in our style is 6. h4!?.} 6.Bxc6 6...dxc6 {If Black captures with the d-pawn, they won't be playing ...d5, but might want to go for ...e5 to open up the bishop on c8. That's why it's important to control the e5-square with a pawn.} 7.f4 7...e5 {If Black goes for it, we are prepared, although they have managed to open up the bishop. It comes at a certain price, though. Let's take and force their hand.} 8.fxe5 8...Bxe5 {The bishop recaptures. Which allows us to develop with tempo, hitting it.} 9.Nf3 9...Bg7 {It slides back to g7, naturally... And we can continue targeting Black's weaknesses with Be3. For the cost of them being able to activate their bishop, we are developing with tempo and causing trouble.} 10.Be3 10...b6 {Black defends with the b-pawn.} 11.Qd2 {And finally, we can make a queen-bishop battery and try to trade the dark-squared bishops, as the center has opened up and Black's bishop is strong. Castling queenside is a good idea, with an equal but not a peaceful position.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 1: The Popular 2...Nc6 and 2...g6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/de2c4be7-40d4-43ab-93d0-fff52026593d/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move. You can never go wrong by copying your opponent's pawn push on move 1. With g3, we'll take up the option of playing a fianchetto to control the light squares.} 2.g3 2...Nc6 {The most popular move for Black in this position is 2...Nc6. It's played almost half the time, with 48%! Black builds behind the c-pawn. We'll continue with the fianchetto.} 3.Bg2 3...e5 {Black plays ...e5, with the idea being to fianchetto and adopt the "Botvinnik setup" that some players may prefer, as they follow a set plan of development. However, Black also creates some tough light square weaknesses. We'll focus on them with Nc3.} 4.Nc3 4...g6 {Black goes for the aforementioned idea, aiming for ...Bg7. Let's push a3, with the simple idea of b4! We want to open up the b-file and unite our rook and light-squared bishop, aiming at the queenside together.} 5.a3 5...Bg7 {If Black just develops, which they will 82% of the time... We'll push b4 as planned.} 6.b4 6...cxb4 {Capturing on b4 actually grants us a winning advantage! Let's take back and continue to offer the pawn.} 7.axb4 7...Nxb4 {If Black is greedy and takes one more time...} 8.Nb5 {I suggest Nb5, with ideas of both Nd6+ and Ba3 to attack the knight. Black is in some trouble and might not even be able to castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...e6 {Expect to see 3...e6 almost 20% of the time. By playing the setup with ...c5, ...Nf6, ...e6, and ...d5 Black is setting up a Tarrasch Defense. The play resembles our QGD repertoire where Black has an isolated queen's pawn (IQP). We'll continue to develop with Nf3.} 4.Nf3 4...d5 {Black plays ...d5. Our idea is to create that IQP anyway, so we'll take.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black takes back with the pawn. We don't have to strike in the center immediately. Black pushing any of the c- or d-pawns would be over-extending.} 6.O-O 6...Nc6 {Instead, they develop... And now we'll go for the active d4. The Tarrasch is a reasonably popular opening for Black with a fair amount of theory but it's pretty easy to play for us, as we should aim for IQP positions with straightforward plans.} 7.d4 7...Be7 {We have to know what to do in case Black completely ignores the tension in the center and develops the bishop. Let's make them lose a tempo! We'll take on c5. This leaves Black with an IQP as well.} 8.dxc5 8...Bxc5 {Black has to take again and thus move the bishop twice in two turns. We'll use that to try to push for b4 with tempo and develop with Bb2. This is a rare but simple sideline!} 9.a3 9...Ne4 {According to the computer, the best move for Black is to immediately centralize the knight with ...Ne4. That shouldn't stop us in our tracks, though. We'll attack the bishop anyway.} 10.b4 10...Be7 {Black retreats to e7. Since the diagonal has opened, we'll play Bb2 right away so that ...Bf6 isn't too dangerous.} 11.Bb2 11...Bf6 {Black will play it anyway. I suggest defending with the queen. We want to keep control of the diagonal if Black captures, and we'll bring a rook to d1.} 12.Qc1 12...O-O {Black castles... And let's develop the knight first. The d2-square is more natural, not to block the diagonal.} 13.Nbd2 13...Bf5 {Black activates the bishop now, to the most natural square. And we'll bring a rook to the center. It's always useful to have a rook on the same file as the enemy queen!} 14.Rd1 14...Rc8 {Knowing that, Black places the rook on the c-file, where our queen is. Let's trade a pair of knights. We are removing Black's centralized piece and opening up our queen's scope.} 15.Nxe4 15...Bxe4 {Black recaptures...} 16.Qd2 {And finally, we'll bring the queen to d2, sliding it off the c-file. There's still tension between the bishops and we are starting to play against that IQP.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...Nc6 {At this point, Black going for the Two Knights is the most popular variation. It's played 37% of the time. We'll develop naturally for now and see what Black wants to do. 4...g6, played 31% of the time, is a transposition. 5.d3 Bg7 6.Qd2.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {The natural 4...e6 is played 37% of the time. We'll play a rare idea but recently played by the German prodigy Keymer against Aronian. We are playing to nullify the bishop on c8.} 5.Bxc6 5...dxc6 {In the game between Vincent Keymer and Levon Aronian, Levon captured on c6 with the d-pawn. He wanted to give some scope to his light-squared bishop. If he could achieve ...e5, that would be good for him. That's why f4 made sense by Keymer.} 6.f4 6...Bd6 {Aronian activated the bishop to d6. Both players simply kept developing.} 7.Nf3 7...Bc7 {What was the idea of ...Bc7? It seemed like a slow, prophylactic move. However, after d3...} 8.d3 8...e5 {Aronian sacrificed the pawn on e5! He wanted to free his bishop at any cost. The point of ...Bc7 was that there wouldn't be a fork after fxe5. Naturally, Vincent captured in the center.} 9.fxe5 9...Ng4 {And it jumped to g4, attacking the e5-pawn. It was Aronian's idea to give it up and then try to get it back however he could. If the idea works, then maybe Bxc6 is a mistake! Keymer defended by developing.} 10.Bf4 10...Qe7 {But the queen attacked the pawn as well, and there was no direct way of defending it. Still, Qd2 is the best move. If Black chooses to trade in the center, White's queen becomes active. Otherwise, Keymer was preparing long castling.} 11.Qd2 11...O-O {Instead of capturing right away, Aronian castled. He was fine with leaving things in the center as they were. Vincent occupied the f-file...} 12.Rf1 12...b6 {And Aronian played ...b6, strengthening the c5-pawn and probably preparing ...Bb7.} 13.O-O-O {White castled long, and the position was unclear but equal according to the computer. It ended in a draw. All in all, a very interesting idea by Vincent Keymer that you could also use in your own games!} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...e6 {Expect to see 3...e6 almost 20% of the time. By playing the setup with ...c5, ...Nf6, ...e6, and ...d5 Black is setting up a Tarrasch Defense. The play resembles our QGD repertoire where Black has an isolated queen's pawn (IQP). We'll continue to develop with Nf3.} 4.Nf3 4...d5 {Black plays ...d5. Our idea is to create that IQP anyway, so we'll take.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black takes back with the pawn. We don't have to strike in the center immediately. Black pushing any of the c- or d-pawns would be over-extending.} 6.O-O 6...Nc6 {Instead, they develop... And now we'll go for the active d4. The Tarrasch is a reasonably popular opening for Black with a fair amount of theory but it's pretty easy to play for us, as we should aim for IQP positions with straightforward plans.} 7.d4 7...Be7 {We have to know what to do in case Black completely ignores the tension in the center and develops the bishop. Let's make them lose a tempo! We'll take on c5. This leaves Black with an IQP as well.} 8.dxc5 8...Bxc5 {Black has to take again and thus move the bishop twice in two turns. We'll use that to try to push for b4 with tempo and develop with Bb2. This is a rare but simple sideline!} 9.a3 9...a5 {Black might see our plans and go for the immediate ...a5. However, that weakens the b5-square severely. I suggest we develop the bishop the other way now, pinning the knight on f6.} 10.Bg5 10...O-O {Black castles, and we aren't done trying to target the bishop on c5. Let's do that with Qc2. This move is also convenient because we are getting the queen off the d-file, where we can place a rook.} 11.Qc2 11...Be7 {The most natural move for Black is to retreat.} 12.Rd1 {And we'll bring the rook to d1, finally. Black is going to have to defend that isolated pawn against our wrath. We'll continue with Nc3, and our g2-bishop might also come to life.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...Nc6 {At this point, Black going for the Two Knights is the most popular variation. It's played 37% of the time. We'll develop naturally for now and see what Black wants to do. 4...g6, played 31% of the time, is a transposition. 5.d3 Bg7 6.Qd2.} 4.Nc3 4...e6 {The natural 4...e6 is played 37% of the time. We'll play a rare idea but recently played by the German prodigy Keymer against Aronian. We are playing to nullify the bishop on c8.} 5.Bxc6 5...bxc6 {Recapturing in the center feels more natural, although that is not what happened in the aforementioned game. The bishop on c8 is "dead" and Black plans ...d5. We'll continue with f4 before playing Nf3. When Black chooses this approach with ...d5, it comes in handy to take control of the e5-square.} 6.f4 6...d5 {Black strikes in the center. We don't have to capture, as Black capturing on c4 would triple their pawns. We'll just develop instead. Our plan is to castle and solidify the pawn chain with d3.} 7.Nf3 7...d4 {But what if Black pushes ...d4? Well, they have grabbed space, but we'll attack the c5-pawn. Black's pawn structure on the c- and d-files is to be targeted.} 8.Na4 8...Bd6 {Black prepares castling. We'll play d3.} 9.d3 9...O-O {Black castles...} 10.O-O {And so do we. Our bishop will still develop along the a3-h8 diagonal, probably to a3. On the other hand, the e4-break is actually a possibility in the center. If Black takes en passant, we'll just recapture at any moment. Otherwise, we've grabbed space.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...e6 {Expect to see 3...e6 almost 20% of the time. By playing the setup with ...c5, ...Nf6, ...e6, and ...d5 Black is setting up a Tarrasch Defense. The play resembles our QGD repertoire where Black has an isolated queen's pawn (IQP). We'll continue to develop with Nf3.} 4.Nf3 4...d5 {Black plays ...d5. Our idea is to create that IQP anyway, so we'll take.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black takes back with the pawn. We don't have to strike in the center immediately. Black pushing any of the c- or d-pawns would be over-extending.} 6.O-O 6...Nc6 {Instead, they develop... And now we'll go for the active d4. The Tarrasch is a reasonably popular opening for Black with a fair amount of theory but it's pretty easy to play for us, as we should aim for IQP positions with straightforward plans.} 7.d4 7...cxd4 {If Black takes in the center, we've managed to get an IQP position. Let's just recapture and develop normally around the pawn.} 8.Nxd4 8...Be7 {Black prepares castling... And we'll get our knight to c3, attacking the pawn. Note that trading on c6 wouldn't be great for us, since it would connect Black's c- and d-pawns. Their IQP wouldn't be isolated anymore.} 9.Nc3 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.Be3 {And we'll go for Be3. Developing naturally, we'll probably slide the rook to c1 next. At some point, trying to win that pawn is definitely the way to go. For now, we'll just blockade.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...e6 {Expect to see 3...e6 almost 20% of the time. By playing the setup with ...c5, ...Nf6, ...e6, and ...d5 Black is setting up a Tarrasch Defense. The play resembles our QGD repertoire where Black has an isolated queen's pawn (IQP). We'll continue to develop with Nf3.} 4.Nf3 4...d5 {Black plays ...d5. Our idea is to create that IQP anyway, so we'll take.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black takes back with the pawn. We don't have to strike in the center immediately. Black pushing any of the c- or d-pawns would be over-extending.} 6.O-O 6...Nc6 {Instead, they develop... And now we'll go for the active d4. The Tarrasch is a reasonably popular opening for Black with a fair amount of theory but it's pretty easy to play for us, as we should aim for IQP positions with straightforward plans.} 7.d4 7...Be7 {We have to know what to do in case Black completely ignores the tension in the center and develops the bishop. Let's make them lose a tempo! We'll take on c5. This leaves Black with an IQP as well.} 8.dxc5 8...Bxc5 {Black has to take again and thus move the bishop twice in two turns. We'll use that to try to push for b4 with tempo and develop with Bb2. This is a rare but simple sideline!} 9.a3 9...O-O {The most traditional move that does absolutely nothing about our threats is simply castling. Let's demonstrate the setup we'd like to achieve. We'll put pressure on the bishop first.} 10.b4 10...Bb6 {Once it retreats... Now we can start targeting the IQP. A good idea here would even be Bg5, pinning its defender!} 11.Nc3 11...h6 {That's why Black stops Bg5 with ...h6.} 12.Bb2 {In that case, we'll just activate the bishop to b2. Black can't really push the pawn to d4, and our rooks are coming to c1 and d1 after the queen moves. White is definitely better here with an enjoyable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...d5 {Black can try to fight for the center while we're playing on the flanks with ...d5. This move is played 12% of the time. Let's remove their central pawn right away.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures, naturally. And we'll propose a trade. Black's got a decision to make now.} 5.Nc3 5...Nc7 {Black retreating to c7 looks unorthodox, but is actually a sound and solid move for Black. They avoid the trade and place the knight on a convenient square from where it can reroute. I like this rare double fianchetto approach against this move, similar to the main line.} 6.b3 6...e5 {Black can gain control of the center... But we'll aim to target the c5- and the e5-pawns while our snipers on g2 and b2 make Black's coordination difficult.} 7.Bb2 7...Be7 {Black develops to e7... And we'll slide to the c-file, improving the rook.} 8.Rc1 8...O-O {If Black plays in a more traditional manner and castles here, we can continue with our plan of targeting the pawns. After our Na4, both pawns are under attack!} 9.Na4 9...Nd7 {Unfortunately, Black can defend both. But their knight is pretty tied down on the d7-square, and so is the bishop.} 10.Nh3 {Making Nh3 possible. Now, both of our bishops stand strong, and we'll play f4 to completely open up the bishop on b2. After castling short, an attack on Black's king might follow.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...d5 {Black can try to fight for the center while we're playing on the flanks with ...d5. This move is played 12% of the time. Let's remove their central pawn right away.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures, naturally. And we'll propose a trade. Black's got a decision to make now.} 5.Nc3 5...Nc7 {Black retreating to c7 looks unorthodox, but is actually a sound and solid move for Black. They avoid the trade and place the knight on a convenient square from where it can reroute. I like this rare double fianchetto approach against this move, similar to the main line.} 6.b3 6...e5 {Black can gain control of the center... But we'll aim to target the c5- and the e5-pawns while our snipers on g2 and b2 make Black's coordination difficult.} 7.Bb2 7...Be7 {Black develops to e7... And we'll slide to the c-file, improving the rook.} 8.Rc1 8...f6 {Black has got the interesting ...f6 option here. The idea is to strengthen the center and neutralize our bishop on b2. However, ...f6 weakens the king. We'll play Nh3 with the idea of f4, chipping away at Black's dark-square pawn chain.} 9.Nh3 9...O-O {Once Black castles...} 10.f4 {That's exactly what we'll do. The Nh3-Nf2 maneuver is the way to go after this, coupled with castling short. Black's pawn chain has been destabilized by our f4 move and our bishop on b2 might come to light again.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...d5 {Black can try to fight for the center while we're playing on the flanks with ...d5. This move is played 12% of the time. Let's remove their central pawn right away.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures, naturally. And we'll propose a trade. Black's got a decision to make now.} 5.Nc3 5...Nxc3 {Taking on c3 leads us back to the Grünfeld, so Black hasn't achieved much of independent value.} 6.bxc3 {Let's recapture with the b-pawn.} 6...g6 {After ...g6, we end up back in the Grünfeld.} 7.h4 {I suggest the aggressive h4 here, going for a flank push right away. In the future, we might be able to push h5 and attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 9: The Symmetrical English / Study 2: Other Sidelines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/770c85f2-7a58-4d20-b53d-2cb29c04f2af/studies/03e569ff-92ea-4371-a17e-227861633993/lines"]

1.c4 1...c5 {Black goes for the Symmetrical English, copying our first move.} 2.g3 2...Nf6 {Black players choose 2...Nf6 11% of the time. Like the 2...e6 line, some positions here resemble the Tarrasch variations. As usual we'll play a fianchetto in the English to control the light squares.} 3.Bg2 3...d5 {Black can try to fight for the center while we're playing on the flanks with ...d5. This move is played 12% of the time. Let's remove their central pawn right away.} 4.cxd5 4...Nxd5 {The knight recaptures, naturally. And we'll propose a trade. Black's got a decision to make now.} 5.Nc3 5...e6 {You might see Black defend with ...e6. The idea is that if we capture, they'll regain two pawns in the center.} 6.Nxd5 {However, capturing is exactly what we'll do!} 6...exd5 {The pawn recaptures, but we've got a nice move here that Black hasn't accounted for.} 7.Qb3 {We'll play Qb3, hitting the d5-pawn and the b7-pawn at the same time! Black actually can't defend both. If they push ...c4, we've got Qb5+ with the same threats. Black is going to lose a pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This option doesn't give us a clear way to the IQP, so we'll have to look for another plan. h4 is an unconventional approach I've found for you. Let's go for the king!} 8.h4 8...b6 {8...b6 is another way to develop. It works out well because there isn't a cxd5 move anymore. We'll play 9. Qb1 to start putting pressure on h7.} 9.Qb1 9...Nc6 {Nc6 seeks to develop yet another piece and control the center with it. We should attack the knight with Bb5 and develop with a tempo. We're trying to commit the c8-bishop.} 10.Bb5 10...Qc7 {The natural 10...Bb7 is incorrect. This move is ideal to defend the knight. 11. Ng5 is still good, threatening h7.} 11.Ng5 11...f5 {In this variation, 11...f5 is possible because the e6-pawn is defended. With Bc4, we start applying pressure on the knight and the e6-pawn indirectly.} 12.Bc4 12...Rd8 {Black will obviously bring the rook to the d-file.} 13.g4 {And despite the fact that material is equal, White's attack is tough to defend against. You'll want to play moves like gxf5 and Nb5. The more lines you can open up for your pieces, the better.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This option doesn't give us a clear way to the IQP, so we'll have to look for another plan. h4 is an unconventional approach I've found for you. Let's go for the king!} 8.h4 8...Nc6 {Black should just develop and not stress excessively. Our plan of putting pressure on h7 should still be played. We avoid the c2-square because of Nb4 jumps.} 9.Qb1 9...h6 {9...h6 creates a hook for us to attack. With g4, we are going to attack the hook and open up lines.} 10.g4 10...e5 {One of the few moves to not lose. Black has to counter in the center. Our next move of Rg1 both defends g4 and supports the g5-pawn push. Black can get overrun if they don't do anything. We are offering the h-pawn, but this isn't a major concern.} 11.Rg1 11...Nxc3 {To win the pawn, Black would have to make this trade which activates our bishop. This trade is necessary because the queen can't defend the knight and the bishop going to h4 at the same time. We'll of course recapture.} 12.Bxc3 12...Bxh4 {Black wins a pawn. But we'll win the bishop pair.} 13.Nxh4 13...Qxh4 {The queen recaptures.} 14.Qe4 {And we centralize our queen, with ideas of Bd3 or Bc4 if favorable. We'll also be castling queenside and bringing our light-squared bishop back to b1 to defend a2 in case of ...Nb4. We'll have pressure against the kingside whenever the queen leaves h4. Do keep in mind taking on e5 is bad because of pins on the e-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This option doesn't give us a clear way to the IQP, so we'll have to look for another plan. h4 is an unconventional approach I've found for you. Let's go for the king!} 8.h4 8...Nc6 {Black should just develop and not stress excessively. Our plan of putting pressure on h7 should still be played. We avoid the c2-square because of Nb4 jumps.} 9.Qb1 9...f5 {This is the most direct response. But we'll keep on going with our ideas of provoking weaknesses.} 10.h5 10...h6 {This is the best move to stop everything. Now e2 is the safest square for our bishop. Our plan is to enable castling, when the bishop on e2 indirectly defends h5.} 11.Be2 11...Bf6 {This is a normal continuation that can serve to illustrate some points about the position. Black wants to defend the kingside, and ...Bf6 is a logical way to try and do that. We'll trade on d5.} 12.Nxd5 12...exd5 {Black recaptures. We'll trade on f6.} 13.Bxf6 13...Qxf6 {The queen recaptures.} 14.O-O {And we castle. If Black wastes time trying to attack h5, we'll move the knight away and defend. We're going Qc2, Rad1 and d4 to expand in the center and our hopes are to have a little bit more space and create weaknesses on Black's position, while remaining solid ourselves.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This option doesn't give us a clear way to the IQP, so we'll have to look for another plan. h4 is an unconventional approach I've found for you. Let's go for the king!} 8.h4 8...b6 {8...b6 is another way to develop. It works out well because there isn't a cxd5 move anymore. We'll play 9. Qb1 to start putting pressure on h7.} 9.Qb1 9...Nc6 {Nc6 seeks to develop yet another piece and control the center with it. We should attack the knight with Bb5 and develop with a tempo. We're trying to commit the c8-bishop.} 10.Bb5 10...Bb7 {This, for example, is an error. Ng5 begins the attack. Note that f5 can't be played because we've drawn the defensive bishop to b7.} 11.Ng5 11...g6 {Black blocks the attack on h7. But we can sacrifice on h7!} 12.Nxh7 12...Kxh7 {The king takes.} 13.Nxd5 {But before h5 we play Nxd5 to open up our bishop. Our attack is pretty strong, and we're playing h5 to great effect.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This option doesn't give us a clear way to the IQP, so we'll have to look for another plan. h4 is an unconventional approach I've found for you. Let's go for the king!} 8.h4 8...Nc6 {Black should just develop and not stress excessively. Our plan of putting pressure on h7 should still be played. We avoid the c2-square because of Nb4 jumps.} 9.Qb1 9...Bf6 {9...Bf6 seeks to add a defender to the kingside.} 10.Bd3 {We attack h7, and should Black respond with ...g6 or ...h6, we have attacking ideas either with Ne4 or g2-g4.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...exd5 {This is the most popular option, but we can actually give Black an IQP that they may not be comfortable with. And they're pretty much guaranteed an IQP unless they find the odd 8...b6, which creates a lot of weaknesses on the light squares. The bishop wouldn't be able to cover them all.} 8.d4 8...Nc6 {That's why you'll often see regular development. We'll just develop, waiting to set up a favorable IQP position.} 9.Be2 9...Bg4 {To illustrate the point, let's say Black brings their bishop out to g4. We can engineer the IQP position by capturing on c5.} 10.dxc5 10...Bxc5 {Black recaptures, and suddenly they're in an IQP position they may not be so ready to handle. We have some ideas. First we castle. Before anything else, king safety is necessary.} 11.O-O 11...a6 {Black creates a retreat square for their bishop. We get our rook on the open file.} 12.Rc1 12...Ba7 {Black brings the bishop back. And we're guaranteed a slight edge after h3.} 13.h3 13...Bh5 {We want the bishop to go to h5 so we can force a trade. If they capture on f3, this is only good for us because we get the bishop pair. Now Nh4 is a really strong move. Our knight is coming to f5.} 14.Nh4 14...Bxe2 {Black trades.} 15.Nxe2 {And we recapture with the knight to open up our bishop and control the d4-square. We're going to have great control of the dark squares leading to the g7-square, and when our knight hops to f5, it'll also put some kingside pressure.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This option doesn't give us a clear way to the IQP, so we'll have to look for another plan. h4 is an unconventional approach I've found for you. Let's go for the king!} 8.h4 8...Bf6 {This move is not good. We could contemplate 9. g4, but Qc2 is good enough.} 9.Qc2 9...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. With Ng5, we attack the h7-pawn and threaten checkmate.} 10.Ng5 10...g6 {10...g6 defends and blocks our view of h7.} 11.Nge4 {Not only are we threatening the pawn, but we're threatening Nxf6+ to remove the defender of the dark squares and control them ourselves with our bishop. Black is in trouble.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...b6 {Many QGD players use this idea. Since the e6-pawn blocks the bishop on c8, many beginners and intermediates are taught that ...b6 to fianchetto is useful. However, ...b6 doesn't solve all their issues. We'll take on d5, now that the bishop has committed itself to b7.} 7.cxd5 7...exd5 {Black recaptures. Black's ...Bb7 idea is countered, in a way. We should now place a pawn on d4.} 8.d4 8...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes, or else b6 created light-squared weaknesses. I like the aggressive placement of our bishop with Bd3.} 9.Bd3 9...c5 {9...c5 is another common idea. Castling is good. There's no need to do anything about the central tension.} 10.O-O 10...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and places pressure on d4. Since there's more pressure, taking on c5 is a good idea.} 11.dxc5 11...bxc5 {This is Black's best recapture, avoiding the IQP. Volunteering for an isolated pawn isn't in their best interest.} 12.Rc1 {And now the very natural 12. Rc1 is a move you should look to play in these positions and against the hanging pawns. Na4 is a strong maneuver to start putting pressure on the c5-pawn, and we should enjoy a solid middlegame, trying to attack those central pawns however we can.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This option doesn't give us a clear way to the IQP, so we'll have to look for another plan. h4 is an unconventional approach I've found for you. Let's go for the king!} 8.h4 8...b6 {8...b6 is another way to develop. It works out well because there isn't a cxd5 move anymore. We'll play 9. Qb1 to start putting pressure on h7.} 9.Qb1 9...h6 {The pawn controls g5, trying to stop our knight jump. But we can play g4 and target h6!} 10.g4 10...Bb7 {The bishop is fianchettoed.} 11.Rh3 {11. Rh3 was played by Nepo in the 2018 Olympiad and he went on to win with a great attack. Rh3 gets the rook out of potential danger. g5 is coming with great effect.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 1: Black Plays 4...Be7 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/f3e536bd-c8e5-4383-9183-c259efbdcd48/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {Black begins to see if we'll agree to go into another opening. We'll develop, controlling d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black expands. And now we push the e-pawn, controlling d4. In case you missed it, 3...d4 would not make sense because we can capture and if Black recaptures with the queen, we gain a tempo by playing Nf3.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Be7 {4...Be7 is a standard way to play the QGD. 25% of players go for this. There are 55,000 games in the database from this position. Black is saying they're not committing to any more pawn moves yet, and they're just going for quick development. We'll go for 5.b3 here to fianchetto the bishop and get it on a strong diagonal.} 5.b3 5...O-O {Black castles. And we fianchetto, of course. Our bishop will aim across the dark squares in the center.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is the most natural continuation. Against ...c5, we'll take on d5 first.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This option doesn't give us a clear way to the IQP, so we'll have to look for another plan. h4 is an unconventional approach I've found for you. Let's go for the king!} 8.h4 8...b6 {8...b6 is another way to develop. It works out well because there isn't a cxd5 move anymore. We'll play 9. Qb1 to start putting pressure on h7.} 9.Qb1 9...f5 {This move seeks to block the diagonal right away. Now the trade on d5 is good for us because the knight was a useful and active piece.} 10.Nxd5 10...exd5 {Black recaptures, but they are lacking a crucial defender of the kingside. We'll push forward, with the aim of provoking weaknesses.} 11.h5 11...Bf6 {Black may think there's nothing for us here. But we'll just keep on playing and create weaknesses.} 12.h6 12...g6 {Supposing Black pushes, now we can improve our queen.} 13.Qc2 {And with d4 coming up next, we'll open things up favorably for us. We'll play moves like Rd1 and Be2, and we of course don't mind a trade of dark-squared bishops because Black will have dark square weaknesses around the king.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recapturing with the pawn is the most common move. It is played 78% of times. The reason for this is that players are taught that recapturing with pawns in the center helps them control more squares with the pawns. Against pawn duos like this, it's useful to establish a central pawn as well, to stop their advance.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and puts some pressure on d4. Let's pin it. We generally want to play ideas like b3 and Bb2, and then dxc5 after the bishop on f8 moves.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd6 {If Black develops the bishop, we'll take on c5.} 8.dxc5 8...Bxc5 {Black recaptures but has now moved the bishop twice in the opening. We'll castle.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles, too. And we're getting everything we want with our fianchetto plan. If Black allows Bb2, they'll be in big trouble because we'll have the perfect setup against the IQP.} 10.b3 10...Bg4 {One of the moves that you may see here and that presents the biggest challenge is ...Bg4. Our knight, which used to control d4, is pinned. Continuing to develop is a good idea. 11...d4 isn't possible due to 12. Na4.} 11.Bb2 11...a6 {This is a useful waiting move. However, it's kind of useful for White, too. We can now change the structure from an IQP to weak hanging pawns. We take the knight.} 12.Bxc6 12...bxc6 {Black recaptures. Our strategy against the hanging pawns is to contain them. We will do this with our rook on the c-file and our knight moving away from c3.} 13.Rc1 13...Bd6 {The bishop avoids tactics since the rook x-rayed it. h3 is a useful move for us. We attack the bishop and remove the h2-target.} 14.h3 14...Bh5 {The bishop retreats. Now Ne2 targets control of d4. This is similar to the 7...a6 line, but the difference is that the rook is on the c-file already.} 15.Ne2 15...Bxf3 {This doesn't trouble us. We'll recapture and can use our rook on the g-file in the future.} 16.gxf3 16...Rc8 {Black defends c6. We'll develop our queen to d3, connecting our rooks.} 17.Qd3 17...Nd7 {Black may try to activate the knight.} 18.f4 {But we'll control e5 and block the bishop's view towards our kingside. We'll continue with Ng3 and if 18...Nc5 you can play Qd1 to take the queen to the kingside after Ng3. You can check out So(2812)-Nakamura (2785) Paris 2017. So went on to win a nice game.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recapturing with the pawn is the most common move. It is played 78% of times. The reason for this is that players are taught that recapturing with pawns in the center helps them control more squares with the pawns. Against pawn duos like this, it's useful to establish a central pawn as well, to stop their advance.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and puts some pressure on d4. Let's pin it. We generally want to play ideas like b3 and Bb2, and then dxc5 after the bishop on f8 moves.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd6 {If Black develops the bishop, we'll take on c5.} 8.dxc5 8...Bxc5 {Black recaptures but has now moved the bishop twice in the opening. We'll castle.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles, too. And we're getting everything we want with our fianchetto plan. If Black allows Bb2, they'll be in big trouble because we'll have the perfect setup against the IQP.} 10.b3 10...Bg4 {One of the moves that you may see here and that presents the biggest challenge is ...Bg4. Our knight, which used to control d4, is pinned. Continuing to develop is a good idea. 11...d4 isn't possible due to 12. Na4.} 11.Bb2 11...Rc8 {It's possible that Black should try 11...Rc8 to support the knight and enter the open c-file. We should play the natural move h3 to attack the bishop.} 12.h3 12...Bh5 {Our aim isn't to push g4 but to control the g4-square using our h3-pawn.} 13.Be2 {At this point there are many plans. The best ones are those that focus on the d4-square. Nb5 is good because it aims to open up the bishop. You'll also want to play Nbd4 to have a knight sitting on d4, blockading.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recapturing with the pawn is the most common move. It is played 78% of times. The reason for this is that players are taught that recapturing with pawns in the center helps them control more squares with the pawns. Against pawn duos like this, it's useful to establish a central pawn as well, to stop their advance.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and puts some pressure on d4. Let's pin it. We generally want to play ideas like b3 and Bb2, and then dxc5 after the bishop on f8 moves.} 7.Bb5 7...a6 {This is the most popular move. Black seeks to get rid of our pin. We'll capture on c6.} 8.Bxc6+ 8...bxc6 {Black gets an open b-file, but we'll get to accomplish everything we want out of the opening. First we'll castle. If 9...cxd4, 10. exd4 helps us control the c6- and d5-pawns with the d4-pawn alone.} 9.O-O 9...Bd6 {If Black develops without the capture on d4, we'll now capture to make the bishop move again. We win a tempo with dxc5.} 10.dxc5 10...Bxc5 {Black recaptures. And we can proceed with our b3 plan.} 11.b3 11...O-O {Black castles. This is expected. We'll play Bb2.} 12.Bb2 12...Bg4 {Black may pin us with the light-squared bishop. Our reply of Ne2 may seem surprising, but it allows us to control the d4 square, and the engine approves.} 13.Ne2 13...Bxf3 {This capture isn't bad for White. The doubled pawns aren't weak and the g-file may be useful.} 14.gxf3 {We recapture. Since Black may want to attack h2, the move f4 is good to block a bishop's view of h2 and to control e5. Ng3 is also a great move because the f5-square may end up being a strong square for us.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recapturing with the pawn is the most common move. It is played 78% of times. The reason for this is that players are taught that recapturing with pawns in the center helps them control more squares with the pawns. Against pawn duos like this, it's useful to establish a central pawn as well, to stop their advance.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and puts some pressure on d4. Let's pin it. We generally want to play ideas like b3 and Bb2, and then dxc5 after the bishop on f8 moves.} 7.Bb5 7...cxd4 {When this capture is played, we'll want to give Black their IQP and have them defend that position. The pressure on c6 is secondary; Black can easily defend. Mostly, we want to fight the IQP in the long run.} 8.Nxd4 8...Bd7 {Black defends c6. And we can castle.} 9.O-O 9...Bd6 {Black develops their bishop. The move e4 works well for us here because we're castled and Black's king is in the center.} 10.e4 10...dxe4 {Black can take with the pawn. Our approach here will be to take on c6 and create a weakness.} 11.Nxc6 11...Bxc6 {This move is forced to defend the d6-bishop. We'll trade again.} 12.Bxc6+ 12...bxc6 {The c6-pawn is weak. Bg5 pins the knight, further weakening e4.} 13.Bg5 13...Be7 {Black unpins. And we go ahead and take the knight, which will allow us to take on e4.} 14.Bxf6 14...Bxf6 {We have a better structure. We can now make use of our pieces and start targeting weaknesses. We'll take on e4.} 15.Nxe4 15...O-O {Black has the option of castling. They may do this to keep the king safe because the position has opened up too much. The queen trade takes us to a good endgame.} 16.Qxd8 16...Bxd8 {Black has to recapture with the bishop to avoid an even worse structure.} 17.Rac1 {And we can start putting pressure on the weakness. b3 will be played to keep the pawn safe from any b-file pressure, and we'll probably want to attack the c-pawn while also creating some luft for our king with g3 if there's ever some danger.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recapturing with the pawn is the most common move. It is played 78% of times. The reason for this is that players are taught that recapturing with pawns in the center helps them control more squares with the pawns. Against pawn duos like this, it's useful to establish a central pawn as well, to stop their advance.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and puts some pressure on d4. Let's pin it. We generally want to play ideas like b3 and Bb2, and then dxc5 after the bishop on f8 moves.} 7.Bb5 7...cxd4 {When this capture is played, we'll want to give Black their IQP and have them defend that position. The pressure on c6 is secondary; Black can easily defend. Mostly, we want to fight the IQP in the long run.} 8.Nxd4 8...Bd7 {Black defends c6. And we can castle.} 9.O-O 9...Bd6 {Black develops their bishop. The move e4 works well for us here because we're castled and Black's king is in the center.} 10.e4 10...dxe4 {Black can take with the pawn. Our approach here will be to take on c6 and create a weakness.} 11.Nxc6 11...Bxc6 {This move is forced to defend the d6-bishop. We'll trade again.} 12.Bxc6+ 12...bxc6 {The c6-pawn is weak. Bg5 pins the knight, further weakening e4.} 13.Bg5 13...Be7 {Black unpins. And we go ahead and take the knight, which will allow us to take on e4.} 14.Bxf6 14...Bxf6 {We have a better structure. We can now make use of our pieces and start targeting weaknesses. We'll take on e4.} 15.Nxe4 15...Bxb2 {This capture is also feasible. Nevertheless, there are many complications and it probably isn't safe for a human to play. We give a check. This ensures the Black king cannot castle.} 16.Nd6+ 16...Kf8 {Black has to move their king. We get on the open b-file.} 17.Rb1 17...Be5 {The bishop retreats and attacks the knight. We'll move away and attack the bishop.} 18.Nc4 18...Bd4 {The bishop is centralized.} 19.Rb7 {But our rook invades the 7th rank. This position is equal according to the engine but I like White's practical chances. It's difficult for Black to defend. Qb3 is a strong move to start looking at the f7-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recapturing with the pawn is the most common move. It is played 78% of times. The reason for this is that players are taught that recapturing with pawns in the center helps them control more squares with the pawns. Against pawn duos like this, it's useful to establish a central pawn as well, to stop their advance.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and puts some pressure on d4. Let's pin it. We generally want to play ideas like b3 and Bb2, and then dxc5 after the bishop on f8 moves.} 7.Bb5 7...cxd4 {When this capture is played, we'll want to give Black their IQP and have them defend that position. The pressure on c6 is secondary; Black can easily defend. Mostly, we want to fight the IQP in the long run.} 8.Nxd4 8...Bd7 {Black defends c6. And we can castle.} 9.O-O 9...Bd6 {Black develops their bishop. The move e4 works well for us here because we're castled and Black's king is in the center.} 10.e4 10...Nxe4 {Against 10...Nxe4, we'll take on c6 as well. This opens up some lines favorably for us because we have castled.} 11.Nxc6 11...Bxc6 {This is the correct recapture. We'll capture on d5 with the queen. Tricks like Bxh2+ don't work because the queen is defended by the c3-knight.} 12.Qxd5 12...O-O {This is the only move that doesn't lose for Black. We'll trade on c6.} 13.Bxc6 13...Nxc3 {Black trades on c3. And instead of capturing on c3, we'll retreat the queen, keeping our structure intact.} 14.Qf3 14...bxc6 {Black takes our bishop.} 15.Qxc3 {But we keep our pawns together and aim at the weak c-pawn for the rest of the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recapturing with the pawn is the most common move. It is played 78% of times. The reason for this is that players are taught that recapturing with pawns in the center helps them control more squares with the pawns. Against pawn duos like this, it's useful to establish a central pawn as well, to stop their advance.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and puts some pressure on d4. Let's pin it. We generally want to play ideas like b3 and Bb2, and then dxc5 after the bishop on f8 moves.} 7.Bb5 7...cxd4 {When this capture is played, we'll want to give Black their IQP and have them defend that position. The pressure on c6 is secondary; Black can easily defend. Mostly, we want to fight the IQP in the long run.} 8.Nxd4 8...Bd7 {Black defends c6. And we can castle.} 9.O-O 9...Bd6 {Black develops their bishop. The move e4 works well for us here because we're castled and Black's king is in the center.} 10.e4 10...Nxe4 {Against 10...Nxe4, we'll take on c6 as well. This opens up some lines favorably for us because we have castled.} 11.Nxc6 11...bxc6 {One could consider this the incorrect capture. Black doesn't lose or anything, but we can get a nice edge.} 12.Nxe4 {We can take a piece and Black cannot capture any of our hanging pieces yet because of the d6-bishop!} 12...Be7 {The right move is 12...Be7.} 13.Bd3 {I like recovering our pawn here. We will have equalized materially but we will be better structurally.} 13...dxe4 {Black takes.} 14.Bxe4 {And we recapture. We have, once again, long-term pressure on the c-pawn and we can expect to win it. Be3 and Qd3 or Qf3 are great moves, depending on how Black plays this. If the h7-pawn stays a target, you want to play Qd3 with tempo. If the h-pawn moves, Qf3 is better.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {Black recapturing with the pawn is the most common move. It is played 78% of times. The reason for this is that players are taught that recapturing with pawns in the center helps them control more squares with the pawns. Against pawn duos like this, it's useful to establish a central pawn as well, to stop their advance.} 6.d4 6...Nc6 {The knight comes out to c6 and puts some pressure on d4. Let's pin it. We generally want to play ideas like b3 and Bb2, and then dxc5 after the bishop on f8 moves.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd7 {7...Bd7 is very popular as well. It aims to simply break the pin. We should castle kingside.} 8.O-O 8...a6 {If Black prompts us, we will not capture, as we don't want to help Black activate their bishop. I find that Be2 present a strategical challenge. We're asking Black what they'll do with the d7-bishop.} 9.Be2 9...cxd4 {If this trade happens, let's give Black an IQP. The IQP is a weakness in the endgame.} 10.Nxd4 10...Bd6 {Black may get ready to castle. But we can start building pressure against the pawn.} 11.Bf3 11...Be6 {Black defends d5. Obviously, we don't want to play Nxe6 because that would help Black fix their structure.} 12.Nce2 {Instead, we want to go for a regular blockade against the isolated d-pawn by having our knights defend each other. We also want to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop, which will strengthen our d4-control.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {This move is an option for Black. It is rare, but you may still see it. If we trade here, Black will be forced to recapture with either a pawn or queen. I argue that both options give us a small advantage.} 6.Nxd5 6...Qxd5 {Black is advised not to play this way. The bishop on c8 would still be blocked and the queen is a target.} 7.b3 {7. b3 prepares to attack the queen. Our bishops are way more powerful with the open lines we have. We should have nice and stable pressure in the middlegame since Black cannot develop both bishops comfortably.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {This move is an option for Black. It is rare, but you may still see it. If we trade here, Black will be forced to recapture with either a pawn or queen. I argue that both options give us a small advantage.} 6.Nxd5 6...exd5 {This is the correct recapture. There are no tempi on Black's queen, and the pawns control the center. But we've got the unconventional strike b4! We're undermining the c-pawn.} 7.b4 7...c4 {Black may avoid the capture if they want to preserve the c-pawn and its control of the center. However, this helps us control d4.} 8.Bb2 8...Bxb4 {Black may take on b4. But this lets us capture on g7.} 9.Bxg7 9...Rg8 {The rook will have pressure on the g-file.} 10.Be5 {But that's why Be5 is designed to bring the bishop to g3 to neutralize the rook's influence on the g-file. After 10...Nc6 and 11.Bg3, there's c3 to worry about. It's not a big deal: all we have to do is if 11...c3, 12. a3, forcing Ba5. The bishop on a5 takes away the Qa5 move from Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {The 2...d5 pawn push puts us in Queen's Gambit Declined territory. I don't want us to go to direct Queen's Gambit territory with 3. d4. Instead, consider 3. e3 as an "Anti-QGD".} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...c5 {4...c5 is thematic in the Queen's Gambit Declined: it's a pawn push designed to control d4 and to challenge the d4-pawn. It is played 20% of the time, more than 45,000 games. We should just capture the d-pawn. We'll be able to continue our plans afterwards.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxd5 {This move is an option for Black. It is rare, but you may still see it. If we trade here, Black will be forced to recapture with either a pawn or queen. I argue that both options give us a small advantage.} 6.Nxd5 6...exd5 {This is the correct recapture. There are no tempi on Black's queen, and the pawns control the center. But we've got the unconventional strike b4! We're undermining the c-pawn.} 7.b4 7...cxb4 {If Black takes, they're playing into our hand.} 8.Bb2 {Without the c5-pawn, we now control d4 completely. Our pressure on the diagonal is significant. We'll play around the d4-square and threaten to win the b-pawn back while developing our pieces. It's a very pleasant position for us and Black may be uncomfortable when you blitz out Rc1, Bb5, 0-0 and Nd4 with ease.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 2: Black Plays 4...c5 / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/544d90ad-8eea-48ad-a8e5-2315a1cb141c/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {The possibility of transposing into a Queen's Gambit Opening arises out of 1...e6. 1...e6 is the precursor to a ...d5 pawn push, which happens in the Queen's Gambit Declined. Against 1...e6 we'll develop our knight and control d5.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {This leads us to the next chapter, which is an opening called the Mikenas-Carls.} 3.e4 {Our idea in that opening is further d5 control, but more of that will come later.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bb4 or 4...dxc4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/4b1a4c70-8faa-40de-8b77-b391fd949953/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {And Black will play their standard 1...e6 approach. We'll develop our knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black pushes their d-pawn. And we'll go for our Anti-QGD controlling d4 and protecting our c-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...dxc4 {Finally, 4...dxc4 is played in around 11,000 games, or ony about 5% of games in the database. But the reason it's important is because we're being taken to Queen's Gambit Accepted territory, and we need to know what to do. Recapturing the pawn and developing is a pretty easy option.} 5.Bxc4 5...a6 {Or you may also see 5...a6. This move aims to achieve the ...b5 pawn push and it's a common idea in these Queen's Gambit positions. 6. a4 is a very logical reaction. We're preventing an easy b5 and creating a retreat square for our bishop.} 6.a4 6...c5 {But since we went a4, we didn't do anything about the c-pawn push. This is nothing to concern ourselves with too much, though. We'll castle first. d4 and d5 is our plan, but it's always good to castle first.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. Qe2 continues our preparations to pushing d4. Rd1 is coming next. The rook will help support the pawn up the board.} 8.Qe2 8...Be7 {The bishop comes out to e7. And we'll get the rook to the d-file.} 9.Rd1 9...O-O {Black castles. Now it's important to show what kind of reactions Black may have to us finally playing d4. They could of course capture the pawn, or do something else.} 10.d4 10...Qc7 {You may see 10...Qc7, ignoring the pawn for now. We should therefore aim to place our c1-bishop on the best possible square if there are no central trades.} 11.b3 11...b6 {Black may attempt to do the same. And just remember that d5 is a good move if the conditions are favorable.} 12.d5 12...exd5 {Black takes. We'll recapture with the knight.} 13.Nxd5 13...Nxd5 {Black trades knights. We'll recapture, pinning the knight.} 14.Bxd5 14...Bf6 {The bishop may attack our rook. But a dark-squared bishop trade is a useful idea for us here, leaving us with the more active minor pieces.} 15.Bb2 15...Bxb2 {A trade is the most logical outcome. We'll recapture.} 16.Qxb2 16...Bb7 {The bishop goes to b7.} 17.Rd2 {We'll double on the d-file and we'll be ready for a bishop trade, since we can't allow the bishop on b7 to aim at f3 and g2. If the knight moves, we'll also play Qe5 to trade queens since we have control of the only open file.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bb4 or 4...dxc4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/4b1a4c70-8faa-40de-8b77-b391fd949953/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {And Black will play their standard 1...e6 approach. We'll develop our knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black pushes their d-pawn. And we'll go for our Anti-QGD controlling d4 and protecting our c-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...dxc4 {Finally, 4...dxc4 is played in around 11,000 games, or ony about 5% of games in the database. But the reason it's important is because we're being taken to Queen's Gambit Accepted territory, and we need to know what to do. Recapturing the pawn and developing is a pretty easy option.} 5.Bxc4 5...a6 {Or you may also see 5...a6. This move aims to achieve the ...b5 pawn push and it's a common idea in these Queen's Gambit positions. 6. a4 is a very logical reaction. We're preventing an easy b5 and creating a retreat square for our bishop.} 6.a4 6...c5 {But since we went a4, we didn't do anything about the c-pawn push. This is nothing to concern ourselves with too much, though. We'll castle first. d4 and d5 is our plan, but it's always good to castle first.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. Qe2 continues our preparations to pushing d4. Rd1 is coming next. The rook will help support the pawn up the board.} 8.Qe2 8...Be7 {The bishop comes out to e7. And we'll get the rook to the d-file.} 9.Rd1 9...O-O {Black castles. Now it's important to show what kind of reactions Black may have to us finally playing d4. They could of course capture the pawn, or do something else.} 10.d4 10...cxd4 {You could also see this capture because maybe Black isn't interested in the tension in the center. The reason it's fine to take with the pawn and accept an IQP is because d5 is coming.} 11.exd4 11...Nb4 {Black may go for blockading the d5-square. We'll make use of the e5-square.} 12.Ne5 12...Nbd5 {Black successfully blockades the d5-square.} 13.Rd3 {But we'll start making use of our IQP by having as an anchor for our attack. Our rook will pivot to g3 where it will aim at the king. The good thing about this is that g3 and h3 are both good options for the rook, depending on how Black reacts during the attack. We should have a comfortable edge here.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bb4 or 4...dxc4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/4b1a4c70-8faa-40de-8b77-b391fd949953/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {And Black will play their standard 1...e6 approach. We'll develop our knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black pushes their d-pawn. And we'll go for our Anti-QGD controlling d4 and protecting our c-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...dxc4 {Finally, 4...dxc4 is played in around 11,000 games, or ony about 5% of games in the database. But the reason it's important is because we're being taken to Queen's Gambit Accepted territory, and we need to know what to do. Recapturing the pawn and developing is a pretty easy option.} 5.Bxc4 5...a6 {Or you may also see 5...a6. This move aims to achieve the ...b5 pawn push and it's a common idea in these Queen's Gambit positions. 6. a4 is a very logical reaction. We're preventing an easy b5 and creating a retreat square for our bishop.} 6.a4 6...c5 {But since we went a4, we didn't do anything about the c-pawn push. This is nothing to concern ourselves with too much, though. We'll castle first. d4 and d5 is our plan, but it's always good to castle first.} 7.O-O 7...Nc6 {Black develops their knight. Qe2 continues our preparations to pushing d4. Rd1 is coming next. The rook will help support the pawn up the board.} 8.Qe2 8...Be7 {The bishop comes out to e7. And we'll get the rook to the d-file.} 9.Rd1 9...O-O {Black castles. Now it's important to show what kind of reactions Black may have to us finally playing d4. They could of course capture the pawn, or do something else.} 10.d4 10...cxd4 {You could also see this capture because maybe Black isn't interested in the tension in the center. The reason it's fine to take with the pawn and accept an IQP is because d5 is coming.} 11.exd4 11...Nb4 {Black may go for blockading the d5-square. We'll make use of the e5-square.} 12.Ne5 12...Bd7 {Black could attempt to get their bishop into the game, too. We should be ready to undermine the knight which defends the kingside with Bg5.} 13.Bg5 13...Bc6 {After 13...Bc6, Black is saying they're fine with the trade on c6 because their pawn will help control d5. We'll take them up on this offer since the c-pawn can be weak, and we are acquiring the bishop pair.} 14.Nxc6 14...bxc6 {Black recaptures.} 15.a5 {And I like playing a5 here, to prevent Black from playing a5 themselves and anchoring their knight. We'll also have a nice rook lift to start applying pressure on the knight. Moves like g3 are also sound because Black lacks a light-squared bishop and we need to create luft for the king.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bb4 or 4...dxc4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/4b1a4c70-8faa-40de-8b77-b391fd949953/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {And Black will play their standard 1...e6 approach. We'll develop our knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black pushes their d-pawn. And we'll go for our Anti-QGD controlling d4 and protecting our c-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Bb4 {Black may play this move. It occurs in 14% of games in the database, which accounts for more than 31,000 games. Black's aim is to undermine the knight and make the d4-pawn push harder to play. 5.b3 helps us stay consistent.} 5.b3 5...O-O {The most common move is for Black to castle. We'll just fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is a popular move now. We'll take in the center.} 7.cxd5 7...exd5 {Black recaptures. And now d4 is not possible because of the pin on the knight. So we'll go 8.Na4 to threaten a3 and to take on c5.} 8.Na4 8...b6 {b6 is the best way to defend the pawn.} 9.Be2 {And now the calm Be2. We're going to castle, place a rook on the c-file and make use of the e5-square for our knight. The bishop on b4 only delayed d4, but it's not going to be the most useful piece after we castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bb4 or 4...dxc4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/4b1a4c70-8faa-40de-8b77-b391fd949953/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {And Black will play their standard 1...e6 approach. We'll develop our knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black pushes their d-pawn. And we'll go for our Anti-QGD controlling d4 and protecting our c-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Bb4 {Black may play this move. It occurs in 14% of games in the database, which accounts for more than 31,000 games. Black's aim is to undermine the knight and make the d4-pawn push harder to play. 5.b3 helps us stay consistent.} 5.b3 5...O-O {The most common move is for Black to castle. We'll just fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...c5 {6...c5 is a popular move now. We'll take in the center.} 7.cxd5 7...Nxd5 {This is a bad move: Black isn't recapturing with a pawn, so they're losing control of the center. The bishop on c8 is also trapped behind the pawns.} 8.Qc2 {You will even get pressure on h7, 0-0-0, and really dynamic chances against Black's kingside. Black is down in development and with less central control, despite the fact that we are delaying moving our d-pawn. If ...Nxc3 or ...Bxc3, we'll recapture with the d-pawn to open up our rook on d1.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bb4 or 4...dxc4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/4b1a4c70-8faa-40de-8b77-b391fd949953/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {And Black will play their standard 1...e6 approach. We'll develop our knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black pushes their d-pawn. And we'll go for our Anti-QGD controlling d4 and protecting our c-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...dxc4 {Finally, 4...dxc4 is played in around 11,000 games, or ony about 5% of games in the database. But the reason it's important is because we're being taken to Queen's Gambit Accepted territory, and we need to know what to do. Recapturing the pawn and developing is a pretty easy option.} 5.Bxc4 5...Be7 {Now you'll most likely see 5...Be7. This is the most natural developing move. We'll simply push in the center with our d-pawn.} 6.d4 6...O-O {Black castles.} 7.Qe2 {And we'll continue with standard development. If ...c5, we'll take and bring our rook to the d-file. We'll also push our e-pawn not only to control central squares, but to develop our dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 10: The Queen's Gambit Declined / Study 3: Black Plays 4...Bb4 or 4...dxc4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/b26e4beb-33ad-4f6f-9be7-f074c88c33e7/studies/4b1a4c70-8faa-40de-8b77-b391fd949953/lines"]

1.c4 1...e6 {And Black will play their standard 1...e6 approach. We'll develop our knight.} 2.Nc3 2...d5 {Black pushes their d-pawn. And we'll go for our Anti-QGD controlling d4 and protecting our c-pawn.} 3.e3 3...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. And we'll develop ours.} 4.Nf3 4...Bb4 {Black may play this move. It occurs in 14% of games in the database, which accounts for more than 31,000 games. Black's aim is to undermine the knight and make the d4-pawn push harder to play. 5.b3 helps us stay consistent.} 5.b3 5...Bxc3 {Some people think it's a good idea to trade on c3. I don't like it since I think they're giving up the bishop pair for nothing. We'll recapture.} 6.dxc3 6...O-O {And after Black castles, we can already make use of the bishop.} 7.Ba3 {We target the rook and ask Black how they'll defend their dark squares. Qc2 and Rd1 are normal developing moves we'll strive for in this middlegame in which we're up the bishop pair and have pressure against the central pawns. With two c-pawns, we'll be able to do much against Black's center.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 1:  Black's Main Line (3...d5) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/23ddcdbc-a941-4cb3-a66a-1587d2bffd8c/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. By developing the b1-knight we add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And with e4, we go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...d5 {The best move to counterattack, immediately pressuring our central pawns. This is the most common response, with 38% occurrence. We'll push in the center and attack the knight.} 4.e5 4...d4 {Black responds actively and attacks our knight in return. Let's capture on f6, since moving the knight is too passive.} 5.exf6 5...dxc3 {Black captures our piece in return. We'll recapture on c3, so we'll have d4 later.} 6.bxc3 6...Qxf6 {Black captures the pawn. We'll continue by developing the knight.} 7.Nf3 7...b6 {Black prepares to fianchetto their light-squared bishop. We'll take over the center with d4. 8.c5 is an interesting gambit that you could play, but let's keep things solid.} 8.d4 8...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes. d3 is a typical place for the light-squared bishop. It occupies the b1-h7 diagonal and fights for the e4-square.} 9.Bd3 9...Bxf3 {Black trades on f3 immediately, which happens in nearly 15% of games. We don't mind a queen trade. White will be doing well in the endgame.} 10.Qxf3 10...Qxf3 {Black trades the queens. We recapture.} 11.gxf3 11...Nd7 {Black develops conventionally. Let's gain some space on the queenside with a4.} 12.a4 12...a5 {Black stops our expansion. The bishop gets centralized to e4, which also comes with an attack on the rook.} 13.Be4 13...Rd8 {Black moves the rook.} 14.Bc6 {And the bishop occupies the weak queenside light-squares. White's position is very comfortable with the bishop pair and the open b and g-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 1:  Black's Main Line (3...d5) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/23ddcdbc-a941-4cb3-a66a-1587d2bffd8c/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. By developing the b1-knight we add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And with e4, we go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...d5 {The best move to counterattack, immediately pressuring our central pawns. This is the most common response, with 38% occurrence. We'll push in the center and attack the knight.} 4.e5 4...d4 {Black responds actively and attacks our knight in return. Let's capture on f6, since moving the knight is too passive.} 5.exf6 5...dxc3 {Black captures our piece in return. We'll recapture on c3, so we'll have d4 later.} 6.bxc3 6...Qxf6 {Black captures the pawn. We'll continue by developing the knight.} 7.Nf3 7...c5 {The most popular move, which happens in 32% of the games, is 7...c5. Black fights for the d4-square. But we'll push anyway! Let's play actively here.} 8.d4 8...cxd4 {Black doesn't stop Bg5 and captures on d4 immediately. So we'll attack the queen.} 9.Bg5 9...Qf5 {Black saves the queen. And now we recapture on d4.} 10.cxd4 10...Bb4+ {Black tries to play actively and take advantage of our uncastled king. We can just simply retreat the bishop.} 11.Bd2 11...Qa5 {Black tries to improve their queen. We'll develop the bishop to d3 and prepare to castle.} 12.Bd3 12...Nc6 {Black develops. And we'll put pressure on the b4-bishop.} 13.Rb1 13...Bxd2+ {Black trades off the bishops.} 14.Qxd2 {We'll recapture. White is once again doing very well, with the better center and the better development.} *

