1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. By developing the b1-knight we add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And with e4, we go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...d5 {The best move to counterattack, immediately pressuring our central pawns. This is the most common response, with 38% occurrence. We'll push in the center and attack the knight.} 4.e5 4...d4 {Black responds actively and attacks our knight in return. Let's capture on f6, since moving the knight is too passive.} 5.exf6 5...dxc3 {Black captures our piece in return. We'll recapture on c3, so we'll have d4 later.} 6.bxc3 6...Qxf6 {Black captures the pawn. We'll continue by developing the knight.} 7.Nf3 7...c5 {The most popular move, which happens in 32% of the games, is 7...c5. Black fights for the d4-square. But we'll push anyway! Let's play actively here.} 8.d4 8...h6 {Black stops Bg5 with 8...h6. We'll bring the other bishop to the d3-square.} 9.Bd3 9...Nc6 {Black develops. We'll get castled.} 10.O-O 10...cxd4 {Black tries to win a pawn on d4 and weaken our center. We'll recapture with the pawn.} 11.cxd4 11...Nxd4 {Black wins a pawn! But we'll trade...} 12.Nxd4 12...Qxd4 {...and after Black recaptures.} 13.Rb1 {We save the rook. Black's king is still in the center, our bishops have more scope and there are always Bh7+ threats lurking if Black castles and doesn't protect their queen.} *

