[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 1:  Black's Main Line (3...d5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/23ddcdbc-a941-4cb3-a66a-1587d2bffd8c/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. By developing the b1-knight we add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And with e4, we go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...d5 {The best move to counterattack, immediately pressuring our central pawns. This is the most common response, with 38% occurrence. We'll push in the center and attack the knight.} 4.e5 4...Nfd7 {Black retreats their knight instead, which is the most popular move here with 54% of the games. We'll snatch the central pawn.} 5.cxd5 5...Nxe5 {Black regains the material by capturing on e5. Let's expand with a tempo by playing d4!} 6.d4 6...Ng6 {The knight retreats to g6. We'll grab the pawn on e6!} 7.dxe6 7...Bxe6 {Black recaptures with the bishop to not mess up their pawn structure. We'll just keep pushing in the center! It's all about space here, and our king is fine on e1 since Black is lacking development.} 8.d5 8...Bc8 {The bishop retreats.} 9.h4 {And we'll grab even more space by pushing the h-pawn! White's position is very solid with a slight advantage. Black is once again lacking development and has less space.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 1:  Black's Main Line (3...d5) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/23ddcdbc-a941-4cb3-a66a-1587d2bffd8c/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. By developing the b1-knight we add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And with e4, we go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...d5 {The best move to counterattack, immediately pressuring our central pawns. This is the most common response, with 38% occurrence. We'll push in the center and attack the knight.} 4.e5 4...d4 {Black responds actively and attacks our knight in return. Let's capture on f6, since moving the knight is too passive.} 5.exf6 5...dxc3 {Black captures our piece in return. We'll recapture on c3, so we'll have d4 later.} 6.bxc3 6...Qxf6 {Black captures the pawn. We'll continue by developing the knight.} 7.Nf3 7...b6 {Black prepares to fianchetto their light-squared bishop. We'll take over the center with d4. 8.c5 is an interesting gambit that you could play, but let's keep things solid.} 8.d4 8...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes. d3 is a typical place for the light-squared bishop. It occupies the b1-h7 diagonal and fights for the e4-square.} 9.Bd3 9...Bd6 {This is a bad move that illustrates why you need to prevent Bg5. Let's go ahead and attack the queen.} 10.Bg5 10...Bxf3 {The only move for Black to not lose immediately. Now Qd2 is a clever move. We support our bishop on g5, and the black queen is still attacked.} 11.Qd2 11...Bf4 {This is the only move for Black, otherwise they would lose the queen. We'll capture the bishop.} 12.Bxf4 12...Bxg2 {Black captures on g2. We'll save our rook.} 13.Rg1 13...Bb7 {The bishop retreats.} 14.O-O-O {And White castles queenside. We've got the bishop pair, and furthermore, our bishop on f4 is very powerful on the kingside dark squares.} *

