1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. By developing the b1-knight we add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And with e4, we go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...d5 {The best move to counterattack, immediately pressuring our central pawns. This is the most common response, with 38% occurrence. We'll push in the center and attack the knight.} 4.e5 4...Nfd7 {Black retreats their knight instead, which is the most popular move here with 54% of the games. We'll snatch the central pawn.} 5.cxd5 5...exd5 {If Black recaptures on d5 with the pawn... ...we can create a strong center by playing d4.} 6.d4 6...Nb6 {Black re-routes their knight, which was blocking the development of other pieces. The bishop on e3 will support our central pawns.} 7.Be3 7...Be7 {Black prepares to castle.} 8.Bd3 {We are much more active here, and with Nge2 and O-O coming, our position is very comfortable.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines (3...Bb4, 3...d6, and 3...c5) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/08d8574f-7877-40d0-b91d-ef340720ad95/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. We'll develop our queen's knight and add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And we'll go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...c5 {Black might try to push ...c5, which doesn't happen very often (9%), but it is tempting for Black to try to take advantage of the weak d4-square. We'll force Black's knight to retreat all the way back to g8.} 4.e5 4...Ng8 {The only available square! We'll develop our other knight and support the pawn on e5.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queen's knight. And here comes d4! We offer a pawn sacrifice to speed up our development.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black accepts. We'll simply recapture.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nxe5 {As anticipated, Black grabs the e-pawn, otherwise they'll just lose space for nothing. But our knight moves to b5, eyeing the weakness on d6.} 8.Ndb5 8...a6 {Black could ignore it, and attack our knight instead. Let's jump into Black's camp with a check!} 9.Nd6+ 9...Bxd6 {Black captures, which is practically forced. Now, our queen gets to a dominant position and takes aim at some weak dark squares while attacking the knight.} 10.Qxd6 10...f6 {Black keeps the knight in the center and protects it. We'll develop the c1-bishop and continue our dark-square domination!} 11.Be3 11...Ne7 {Black is going for ...Nf5, to kick out our queen. But we have Bb6, trapping the black queen!} 12.Bb6 12...Nf7 {...Nf7 is a move we would like to see. We have an opportunity to improve our knight by centralizing it!} 13.Ne4 13...Nf5 {Black threatens our queen once more. We'll retreat to b4, keeping the queen on the dark-squared diagonal, where it protects the b6-bishop.} 14.Qb4 14...Qe7 {Black tries to release the pressure with a queen trade.} 15.c5 {But a queen trade would only benefit Black, so we push our c-pawn, increasing our control of the dark squares. We have more space and our pieces are just beautifully placed.} *

