passant.} 17.cxd6+ 17...Nxd6 {Black recaptures. And let's pin the knight on d6 and just keep adding pressure to it!} 18.Bc5 18...Nf7 {Black reinforces d6. Now our rook joins the attack!} 19.Rd1 19...Rd8 {Black protects d6.} 20.Be2 {And with Be2, White prepares to castle. We'll connect the rooks, and our position is very comfortable with the bishop pair and more active pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines (3...Bb4, 3...d6, and 3...c5) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/08d8574f-7877-40d0-b91d-ef340720ad95/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. We'll develop our queen's knight and add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And we'll go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...c5 {Black might try to push ...c5, which doesn't happen very often (9%), but it is tempting for Black to try to take advantage of the weak d4-square. We'll force Black's knight to retreat all the way back to g8.} 4.e5 4...Ng8 {The only available square! We'll develop our other knight and support the pawn on e5.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queen's knight. And here comes d4! We offer a pawn sacrifice to speed up our development.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black accepts. We'll simply recapture.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nxe5 {As anticipated, Black grabs the e-pawn, otherwise they'll just lose space for nothing. But our knight moves to b5, eyeing the weakness on d6.} 8.Ndb5 8...a6 {Black could ignore it, and attack our knight instead. Let's jump into Black's camp with a check!} 9.Nd6+ 9...Bxd6 {Black captures, which is practically forced. Now, our queen gets to a dominant position and takes aim at some weak dark squares while attacking the knight.} 10.Qxd6 10...f6 {Black keeps the knight in the center and protects it. We'll develop the c1-bishop and continue our dark-square domination!} 11.Be3 11...Ne7 {Black is going for ...Nf5, to kick out our queen. But we have Bb6, trapping the black queen!} 12.Bb6 12...Nf5 {But Black can counter-attack ours. Let's go ahead and capture the queen.} 13.Bxd8 13...Nxd6 {Black takes our queen. Our bishop retreats and attacks the knight.} 14.Bc7 14...Ndxc4 {Black captures on c4, since the knights protect each other. But with f4, we'll attack one of them!} 15.f4 15...Ne3 {Black intends to fork us on c2, but our pieces will be superior. Let's grab the knight.} 16.fxe5 16...Nc2+ {Black goes for the fork. We'll move the king and ensure that the knight can't get back out.} 17.Kd2 17...Nxa1 {Black captures the rook.} 18.Be2 {The knight on a1 is lost, and our pieces are much more active. With the better pieces and the dark-square control on the queenside, we have the advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines (3...Bb4, 3...d6, and 3...c5) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/08d8574f-7877-40d0-b91d-ef340720ad95/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. We'll develop our queen's knight and add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And we'll go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...c5 {Black might try to push ...c5, which doesn't happen very often (9%), but it is tempting for Black to try to take advantage of the weak d4-square. We'll force Black's knight to retreat all the way back to g8.} 4.e5 4...Ng8 {The only available square! We'll develop our other knight and support the pawn on e5.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queen's knight. And here comes d4! We offer a pawn sacrifice to speed up our development.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black accepts. We'll simply recapture.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nxe5 {As anticipated, Black grabs the e-pawn, otherwise they'll just lose space for nothing. But our knight moves to b5, eyeing the weakness on d6.} 8.Ndb5 8...d6 {Black's second option is to occupy the d6-square with their pawn. Let's chip away at the weak d6-pawn and play c5!} 9.c5 9...a6 {Black attacks our knight in return. We'll simply take on d6 with check.} 10.Nxd6+ 10...Bxd6 {Black captures the knight. We'll take back with the pawn, which will be a great asset that gives us space and limits Black.} 11.cxd6 11...Nf6 {Black continues their development.} 12.Be2 {And White prepares to castle. The d6-pawn is a thorn in Black's side, and the combination of the bishop pair plus more space gives us an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines (3...Bb4, 3...d6, and 3...c5) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/08d8574f-7877-40d0-b91d-ef340720ad95/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. We'll develop our queen's knight and add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And we'll go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...c5 {Black might try to push ...c5, which doesn't happen very often (9%), but it is tempting for Black to try to take advantage of the weak d4-square. We'll force Black's knight to retreat all the way back to g8.} 4.e5 4...Ng8 {The only available square! We'll develop our other knight and support the pawn on e5.} 5.Nf3 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queen's knight. And here comes d4! We offer a pawn sacrifice to speed up our development.} 6.d4 6...cxd4 {Black accepts. We'll simply recapture.} 7.Nxd4 7...Nxe5 {As anticipated, Black grabs the e-pawn, otherwise they'll just lose space for nothing. But our knight moves to b5, eyeing the weakness on d6.} 8.Ndb5 8...d6 {Black's second option is to occupy the d6-square with their pawn. Let's chip away at the weak d6-pawn and play c5!} 9.c5 9...dxc5 {Capturing the pawn would be a big mistake. Now Bf4 is a very strong move! We attack the knight on e5 and eye the weak c7-square.} 10.Bf4 10...f6 {Black supports the knight so we cannot invade on c7. We'll get rid of Black's most active piece.} 11.Bxe5 11...fxe5 {Black recaptures.} 12.Qh5+ {We'll avoid a queen trade and capture on e5 next. White is completely winning. Black's king is weak, and there are a lot of threats.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines (3...Bb4, 3...d6, and 3...c5) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/08d8574f-7877-40d0-b91d-ef340720ad95/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. We'll develop our queen's knight and add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And we'll go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...d6 {Black might go for a non-commital approach with 3...d6. We can see this move in 12% of the games. Let's grab the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Be7 {Black prepares to castle. And we'll grab even more space with f4! Aesthetic but 5. Nf3 was also perfectly normal.} 5.f4 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll get our kingside development going with Nf3.} 6.Nf3 6...c5 {Black creates some central counterplay. Let's close down the center with d5! We have more space.} 7.d5 7...exd5 {Black trades. We'll recapture towards the center.} 8.cxd5 8...Re8 {Black centralizes their rook. We'll continue to develop, and prepare to castle.} 9.Be2 9...Nbd7 {Black develops. Let's get the king into safety.} 10.O-O 10...Bf8 {Black attacks our e4-pawn. We'll simply protect it with the queen.} 11.Qc2 11...a6 {Black would like to push ...b5 and gain space on the queenside...} 12.a4 {...so we will stop it! We've got a very nice center and more space for our pieces to maneuver!} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines (3...Bb4, 3...d6, and 3...c5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/08d8574f-7877-40d0-b91d-ef340720ad95/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. We'll develop our queen's knight and add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And we'll go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...Bb4 {Black goes for 3...Bb4. This is actually a mistake! Yet, it happens 16% of the time! We can gain space with e5.} 4.e5 4...Ng8 {Retreating immediately would be very passive. We'll play Qg4 to attack the g7-pawn.} 5.Qg4 5...g6 {Black defends it.} 6.Nf3 {And after this simple developing move, White is much better with greater activity and more space.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines (3...Bb4, 3...d6, and 3...c5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/08d8574f-7877-40d0-b91d-ef340720ad95/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. We'll develop our queen's knight and add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And we'll go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...Bb4 {Black goes for 3...Bb4. This is actually a mistake! Yet, it happens 16% of the time! We can gain space with e5.} 4.e5 4...Bxc3 {Black trades on c3 first. We'll recapture and open up our dark-squared bishop.} 5.dxc3 5...Ng8 {The only available square for the knight! We'll move our queen to g4 and attack the g7-pawn. This is a very common move in these variations.} 6.Qg4 6...g6 {Black defends it.} 7.Bg5 {And we take control over the dark squares! Black's passivity and weak dark squares will be fatal.} *

[Event "Chapter 11: The Mikenas-Carls Variation / Study 2: Move 3 Sidelines (3...Bb4, 3...d6, and 3...c5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/4886102e-4713-4c78-9347-0e30756b6ace/studies/08d8574f-7877-40d0-b91d-ef340720ad95/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nf6 {Black responds with the development of their kingside knight. We'll develop our queen's knight and add even more control to the center.} 2.Nc3 2...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop. And we'll go for the Mikenas-Carls variation! It is a very challenging system for Black to face since we grab a large chunk of the center.} 3.e4 3...Bb4 {Black goes for 3...Bb4. This is actually a mistake! Yet, it happens 16% of the time! We can gain space with e5.} 4.e5 4...Bxc3 {Black trades on c3 first. We'll recapture and open up our dark-squared bishop.} 5.dxc3 5...Ne4 {Jumping to e4 is a big mistake.} 6.Qg4 {We fork the knight and the g-pawn, winning material.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 1: The Leningrad Dutch / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/96343750-e454-4ea9-bcbf-b06c4bea5e4c/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can play the Dutch Setup and choose what type of Dutch they'll go for in the coming moves. We'll continue with normal development. It's important to get the knight out to control the e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. We'll play d4. Having this central pawn duo is something we'll be using against the Dutch.} 3.d4 3...g6 {This is the Leningrad Dutch. From this move order, it has been played 20% of the time in around 37,000 games. Black's aim is to fianchetto and develop quickly. We'll play the solid e3. Black may think we're going for a b3-setup. But we're actually planning something different.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes, as expected in this line. And now b4! We accelerate our queenside play and create a square for our bishop to come out to. In the future a4 will be useful to completely take the queenside over, but for now we content ourselves.} 5.b4 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...d6 {Black plays 6...d6. The plan of Black's opening is to go ...e5 at some point if possible. We should now clear the way for castling.} 7.Be2 7...Qe8 {Placing a queen on e8 isn't that odd in Dutch positions. The problem for Black here is that they lack space and the queen cannot even go to the kingside yet because the g6-pawn is in the way. We should therefore just castle.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {Black continues preparing their pawn push. But we can continue developing with Nc3, since we have more space and ...e5 doesn't solve their problems.} 9.Nc3 9...c6 {A move like ...c6 now is sober. It controls the center and admits that Black is simply going to be playing with less space.} 10.a4 {We're going to have a much better position moving forward. We will employ our ideas of queenside play due to having more space on that side of the board, striving for moves like Qb3, a5 and b5 to open up lines.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 1: The Leningrad Dutch / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/96343750-e454-4ea9-bcbf-b06c4bea5e4c/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can play the Dutch Setup and choose what type of Dutch they'll go for in the coming moves. We'll continue with normal development. It's important to get the knight out to control the e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. We'll play d4. Having this central pawn duo is something we'll be using against the Dutch.} 3.d4 3...g6 {This is the Leningrad Dutch. From this move order, it has been played 20% of the time in around 37,000 games. Black's aim is to fianchetto and develop quickly. We'll play the solid e3. Black may think we're going for a b3-setup. But we're actually planning something different.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes, as expected in this line. And now b4! We accelerate our queenside play and create a square for our bishop to come out to. In the future a4 will be useful to completely take the queenside over, but for now we content ourselves.} 5.b4 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...d6 {Black plays 6...d6. The plan of Black's opening is to go ...e5 at some point if possible. We should now clear the way for castling.} 7.Be2 7...Qe8 {Placing a queen on e8 isn't that odd in Dutch positions. The problem for Black here is that they lack space and the queen cannot even go to the kingside yet because the g6-pawn is in the way. We should therefore just castle.} 8.O-O 8...Nbd7 {Black continues preparing their pawn push. But we can continue developing with Nc3, since we have more space and ...e5 doesn't solve their problems.} 9.Nc3 9...e5 {The timing on this isn't good at all, especially once the queen has gone to e8.} 10.Nb5 {Thanks to our bishop on b2, we have this tactic. Black cannot defend c7 without losing a central pawn. 10...e4 may seem like something to consider, but 11.Ng5 or 11.Nxc7 work in our favor, being +3 according to Stockfish. 11.Ng5 is simpler, because in the Nxc7 line we'll have to find ways to free our knight from a8.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 1: The Leningrad Dutch / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/96343750-e454-4ea9-bcbf-b06c4bea5e4c/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can play the Dutch Setup and choose what type of Dutch they'll go for in the coming moves. We'll continue with normal development. It's important to get the knight out to control the e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. We'll play d4. Having this central pawn duo is something we'll be using against the Dutch.} 3.d4 3...g6 {This is the Leningrad Dutch. From this move order, it has been played 20% of the time in around 37,000 games. Black's aim is to fianchetto and develop quickly. We'll play the solid e3. Black may think we're going for a b3-setup. But we're actually planning something different.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes, as expected in this line. And now b4! We accelerate our queenside play and create a square for our bishop to come out to. In the future a4 will be useful to completely take the queenside over, but for now we content ourselves.} 5.b4 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...d6 {Black plays 6...d6. The plan of Black's opening is to go ...e5 at some point if possible. We should now clear the way for castling.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Black may realize they'll just need to play with less space. This is probably a sober realization. We should castle.} 8.O-O 8...Qe7 {The e7-square is great for the queen because it will help support pawn breaks and keep the queen relevant. Our knight has the great c3-square.} 9.Nc3 9...Kh8 {...Kh8 is a move you'll see in many Dutch lines because the king wants to get off the light square diagonal. b3 is one of the best squares for our queen. It supports the queenside pawns.} 10.Qb3 10...b6 {Going for another fianchetto is a sound choice for Black. It's trying to find solutions for their lack of space. But since we are the ones with space, we can start getting ready to open up lines after Rfc1.} 11.Rfc1 11...Nbd7 {The knight goes to d7 where it helps prepare a pawn break. Unfortunately for Black, we have a significant space advantage. We have some time to start pushing things on the queenside.} 12.a4 12...a5 {If Black tries stopping us, we've got more pawn breaks to open up the position.} 13.c5 {Once we open the position up, we'll have great piece play because of our pieces. We've got easy moves to find to apply pressure at the right moments. Ba3 to attack the queen on the dark diagonal, Nb5 to attack the c7-pawn. Black won't be able to hold on for long.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 1: The Leningrad Dutch / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/96343750-e454-4ea9-bcbf-b06c4bea5e4c/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can play the Dutch Setup and choose what type of Dutch they'll go for in the coming moves. We'll continue with normal development. It's important to get the knight out to control the e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. We'll play d4. Having this central pawn duo is something we'll be using against the Dutch.} 3.d4 3...g6 {This is the Leningrad Dutch. From this move order, it has been played 20% of the time in around 37,000 games. Black's aim is to fianchetto and develop quickly. We'll play the solid e3. Black may think we're going for a b3-setup. But we're actually planning something different.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes, as expected in this line. And now b4! We accelerate our queenside play and create a square for our bishop to come out to. In the future a4 will be useful to completely take the queenside over, but for now we content ourselves.} 5.b4 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...d6 {Black plays 6...d6. The plan of Black's opening is to go ...e5 at some point if possible. We should now clear the way for castling.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Black may realize they'll just need to play with less space. This is probably a sober realization. We should castle.} 8.O-O 8...Qe7 {The e7-square is great for the queen because it will help support pawn breaks and keep the queen relevant. Our knight has the great c3-square.} 9.Nc3 9...Kh8 {...Kh8 is a move you'll see in many Dutch lines because the king wants to get off the light square diagonal. b3 is one of the best squares for our queen. It supports the queenside pawns.} 10.Qb3 10...b6 {Going for another fianchetto is a sound choice for Black. It's trying to find solutions for their lack of space. But since we are the ones with space, we can start getting ready to open up lines after Rfc1.} 11.Rfc1 11...Bb7 {Black may fianchetto. We'll push d5, blocking the bishop. We would love to open up the c-file for our rook.} 12.d5 12...e5 {In case Black chooses to keep the position closed, we'll insist.} 13.c5 {Our queen on b3 and knight on c3 guard the powerful d5-pawn, so 13.c5 is possible. We're threatening 14.c6. It's a move we'll likely play in conjunction with a4 and a5 to open up the queenside. White is doing well.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 1: The Leningrad Dutch / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/96343750-e454-4ea9-bcbf-b06c4bea5e4c/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can play the Dutch Setup and choose what type of Dutch they'll go for in the coming moves. We'll continue with normal development. It's important to get the knight out to control the e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. We'll play d4. Having this central pawn duo is something we'll be using against the Dutch.} 3.d4 3...g6 {This is the Leningrad Dutch. From this move order, it has been played 20% of the time in around 37,000 games. Black's aim is to fianchetto and develop quickly. We'll play the solid e3. Black may think we're going for a b3-setup. But we're actually planning something different.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes, as expected in this line. And now b4! We accelerate our queenside play and create a square for our bishop to come out to. In the future a4 will be useful to completely take the queenside over, but for now we content ourselves.} 5.b4 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...d6 {Black plays 6...d6. The plan of Black's opening is to go ...e5 at some point if possible. We should now clear the way for castling.} 7.Be2 7...c6 {Black can attempt 7...c6 to try and prevent us from controlling the d5-square. Since this may end up similar to the 7...Qe8 line, our plans stay the same, and we'll play Nc3.} 8.Nc3 8...Qe8 {The queen goes to e8 to support e5. If 8...a5, 9.b5 is good because our b5-pawn has enough support. We'll continue with our plan of expanding on the queenside.} 9.a4 9...e5 {Let's say that Black attempts this pawn break without ...Nbd7. If 9...Nbd7, we'll castle and we've got a similar position to 7...Qe8 in our hands. We can capture and clarify the situation in the center because the situation on the queenside favors us.} 10.dxe5 10...dxe5 {Black recaptures.} 11.O-O {And we castle. If ...e4, we've got Nd4. We're going to play Qb3 and Rd1, pushing our queenside pawns up the board to create strong counterplay in reply to Black's expansion. We have a small advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 1: The Leningrad Dutch / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/96343750-e454-4ea9-bcbf-b06c4bea5e4c/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can play the Dutch Setup and choose what type of Dutch they'll go for in the coming moves. We'll continue with normal development. It's important to get the knight out to control the e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. We'll play d4. Having this central pawn duo is something we'll be using against the Dutch.} 3.d4 3...g6 {This is the Leningrad Dutch. From this move order, it has been played 20% of the time in around 37,000 games. Black's aim is to fianchetto and develop quickly. We'll play the solid e3. Black may think we're going for a b3-setup. But we're actually planning something different.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes, as expected in this line. And now b4! We accelerate our queenside play and create a square for our bishop to come out to. In the future a4 will be useful to completely take the queenside over, but for now we content ourselves.} 5.b4 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...d6 {Black plays 6...d6. The plan of Black's opening is to go ...e5 at some point if possible. We should now clear the way for castling.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Black may realize they'll just need to play with less space. This is probably a sober realization. We should castle.} 8.O-O 8...Qe7 {The e7-square is great for the queen because it will help support pawn breaks and keep the queen relevant. Our knight has the great c3-square.} 9.Nc3 9...Kh8 {...Kh8 is a move you'll see in many Dutch lines because the king wants to get off the light square diagonal. b3 is one of the best squares for our queen. It supports the queenside pawns.} 10.Qb3 10...e5 {Black pushes the e-pawn forward. This is a standard pawn break that you should be aware of. Black is trying to gain space and free their position. The problem with the pawn break is that we have developed our pieces harmoniously and Black still has many underdeveloped forces. We take on e5.} 11.dxe5 11...dxe5 {Black recaptures. And this knight centralization is good. We'll likely get a pawn wedge on d5.} 12.Nd5 12...Nxd5 {Black takes.} 13.cxd5 {And we recapture. Our queen is already in an optimal position. We'll centralize rooks with Rfd1. Rac1 is an option, but so is a4 for b5. In particular, if Black gives us a hook on the queenside to attack, a move like a6 or c6, we'll be wanting to play b5.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 1: The Leningrad Dutch / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/96343750-e454-4ea9-bcbf-b06c4bea5e4c/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can play the Dutch Setup and choose what type of Dutch they'll go for in the coming moves. We'll continue with normal development. It's important to get the knight out to control the e5-square.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. We'll play d4. Having this central pawn duo is something we'll be using against the Dutch.} 3.d4 3...g6 {This is the Leningrad Dutch. From this move order, it has been played 20% of the time in around 37,000 games. Black's aim is to fianchetto and develop quickly. We'll play the solid e3. Black may think we're going for a b3-setup. But we're actually planning something different.} 4.e3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes, as expected in this line. And now b4! We accelerate our queenside play and create a square for our bishop to come out to. In the future a4 will be useful to completely take the queenside over, but for now we content ourselves.} 5.b4 5...O-O {Black castles. We'll fianchetto.} 6.Bb2 6...d6 {Black plays 6...d6. The plan of Black's opening is to go ...e5 at some point if possible. We should now clear the way for castling.} 7.Be2 7...e6 {Black may realize they'll just need to play with less space. This is probably a sober realization. We should castle.} 8.O-O 8...Qe7 {The e7-square is great for the queen because it will help support pawn breaks and keep the queen relevant. Our knight has the great c3-square.} 9.Nc3 9...e5 {This would be a premature pawn push. Yes, our b4-pawn is loose, but the central dynamics are more important. Therefore we will just take.} 10.dxe5 10...dxe5 {Black recaptures. But now with Nd5 we can land a knight in the center with great effect.} 11.Nd5 11...Nxd5 {Black takes.} 12.cxd5 {And we can just take with the pawn because although b4 hangs, so does e5. If a logical move like Rd8, Qb3 is good. We'll be defending everything and centralizing our rooks soon with Rfd1.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 2: The Stonewall Dutch and Others / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/912b546f-7c57-4246-b0d8-f512d8bb20f9/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can go for the Dutch, hoping we transpose back into normal Dutch territory. We'll play our knight out to f3 first.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. And we can get two pawns in the center.} 3.d4 3...e6 {This move 3...e6 is the prelude to the Stonewall or Gotham Dutch. Black can even go to e7 with their bishop or just fianchetto on the queenside. Let's see how to face these options. c2 is a great square for our queen. This helps us remove the sting from the Gotham Dutch which would have occurred after 4.Nc3 Bb4.} 4.Qc2 4...d5 {This is Stonewall territory. Black wants to have a strong light-squared chain of pawns controlling the e4-square. But the Stonewall allows us to maneuver around Black's weak dark squares. 5.Bf4 is precisely the move to begin doing that.} 5.Bf4 5...c6 {...c6 is a good follow-up to ensure they'll hold the center down. We should play 6.e3 to connect our pawns, especially since our bishop has been developed.} 6.e3 6...Bd6 {Black may attempt to trade bishops, since the f4-bishop is quite strong. We'll accept the trade because their dark squares will be weak.} 7.Bxd6 7...Qxd6 {Black will recapture. And after developing, we still have a strong position because we control the e5-square and having fewer weak squares in the long term.} 8.Nc3 8...O-O {Black castles. And we can bring our bishop to d3.} 9.Bd3 9...Nbd7 {Black cannot play 10...e5 because their f-pawn would hang. Therefore, we should just castle and wait to see Black's hand.} 10.O-O 10...Ne4 {Ne4 is to be expected.} 11.Rab1 {11.Rab1 prepares queenside expansion. With little happening on the kingside for Black, it seems we're the only ones that will be having fun on the queenside. The goal of Rab1 is to push the b-pawn and attack the hook on c6. Eventually, we may end up having an open file for the rook and Black will struggle with developing the light-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 2: The Stonewall Dutch and Others / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/912b546f-7c57-4246-b0d8-f512d8bb20f9/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can go for the Dutch, hoping we transpose back into normal Dutch territory. We'll play our knight out to f3 first.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. And we can get two pawns in the center.} 3.d4 3...e6 {This move 3...e6 is the prelude to the Stonewall or Gotham Dutch. Black can even go to e7 with their bishop or just fianchetto on the queenside. Let's see how to face these options. c2 is a great square for our queen. This helps us remove the sting from the Gotham Dutch which would have occurred after 4.Nc3 Bb4.} 4.Qc2 4...Be7 {The most popular and natural move is for Black to enable castling. It occurs around 35% of the time, but since our move 4.Qc2 is rare, there's only 1300 games from this position. 4...Be7 is especially common when Black is playing the Dutch as a setup-based opening. We'll bring our knight out to c3. We're helping fight for the control of the light squares in the center.} 5.Nc3 5...O-O {Black castles. Before playing e4, we want to develop. We'll prepare a fianchetto with g3.} 6.g3 6...d6 {Black starts controlling the e5-square because their long-term plan is to push e5 or even to get the queen to h5. We should fianchetto, developing before we open the position up.} 7.Bg2 7...Qe8 {And this is the common Dutch idea that you're bound to see. Black knows that they can attack in the Dutch, and Qe8 to h5 is a classic maneuver for that purpose. We have a strong bishop on g2 that will help defend our king. We're also very near to breaking the position open with e4.} 8.O-O 8...Qh5 {Here comes the queen. Black usually wants stuff like ...Ng4 to target h2. But, trusting that our king has sufficient defense thanks to our minor pieces on the kingside, we can begin a queenside initiative.} 9.b4 9...Nc6 {This is a bad square for the knight because it encourages us to gain more space, which is what we want. We'll simply advance on the queenside.} 10.b5 10...Nd8 {The knight falls back to d8. We can gain more space by playing a4.} 11.a4 11...Nf7 {This would be the only logical maneuver for that knight, but I hope it shows how you can take over the center of the board to limit Black's choices. Now Ba3 makes the e5-pawn break impossible to play due to the pin on the d6-pawn.} 12.Ba3 12...g5 {You should expect a kingside attack of some sort. But just keep the rule of +2 in mind. If Black doesn't have +2 attackers, you should be perfectly able to fend off their attack.} 13.c5 {Therefore, we can just keep on pushing on the queenside. After 13...dxc5 and 14.dxc5, we can even consider pushing c6 to get even more space. Depending on what Black does, there are some knight maneuvers we'd like to make - ideas like Ne1 for Nd3, for example.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 2: The Stonewall Dutch and Others / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/912b546f-7c57-4246-b0d8-f512d8bb20f9/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can go for the Dutch, hoping we transpose back into normal Dutch territory. We'll play our knight out to f3 first.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. And we can get two pawns in the center.} 3.d4 3...e6 {This move 3...e6 is the prelude to the Stonewall or Gotham Dutch. Black can even go to e7 with their bishop or just fianchetto on the queenside. Let's see how to face these options. c2 is a great square for our queen. This helps us remove the sting from the Gotham Dutch which would have occurred after 4.Nc3 Bb4.} 4.Qc2 4...Be7 {The most popular and natural move is for Black to enable castling. It occurs around 35% of the time, but since our move 4.Qc2 is rare, there's only 1300 games from this position. 4...Be7 is especially common when Black is playing the Dutch as a setup-based opening. We'll bring our knight out to c3. We're helping fight for the control of the light squares in the center.} 5.Nc3 5...O-O {Black castles. Before playing e4, we want to develop. We'll prepare a fianchetto with g3.} 6.g3 6...d6 {Black starts controlling the e5-square because their long-term plan is to push e5 or even to get the queen to h5. We should fianchetto, developing before we open the position up.} 7.Bg2 7...Qe8 {And this is the common Dutch idea that you're bound to see. Black knows that they can attack in the Dutch, and Qe8 to h5 is a classic maneuver for that purpose. We have a strong bishop on g2 that will help defend our king. We're also very near to breaking the position open with e4.} 8.O-O 8...Qh5 {Here comes the queen. Black usually wants stuff like ...Ng4 to target h2. But, trusting that our king has sufficient defense thanks to our minor pieces on the kingside, we can begin a queenside initiative.} 9.b4 9...Nbd7 {You should expect the knight to come out at this point. Black really wants to try and attack. However, we'll play c5. Our space advantage is significant, and our attack seems to be getting places a lot quicker.} 10.c5 10...e5 {Black isn't as quick as us, even though they'd like to be. The move 10...e5 illustrates this point. This is their desired pawn break, but they simply aren't ready for it.} 11.Nb5 {We're winning on the queenside. Black will at least lose a pawn in this transaction. The engine gives us a +2 advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 2: The Stonewall Dutch and Others / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/912b546f-7c57-4246-b0d8-f512d8bb20f9/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can go for the Dutch, hoping we transpose back into normal Dutch territory. We'll play our knight out to f3 first.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. And we can get two pawns in the center.} 3.d4 3...e6 {This move 3...e6 is the prelude to the Stonewall or Gotham Dutch. Black can even go to e7 with their bishop or just fianchetto on the queenside. Let's see how to face these options. c2 is a great square for our queen. This helps us remove the sting from the Gotham Dutch which would have occurred after 4.Nc3 Bb4.} 4.Qc2 4...b6 {Developing with ...b6 is also something you may encounter. It occurs in around 15% of games after 4.Qc2. We'll play g3, and prepare to place our bishop on the long diagonal.} 5.g3 5...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll do likewise.} 6.Bg2 6...Bb4+ {This check is also feasible to get quicker development. We'll block with the bishop as we don't mind a trade of dark-squared bishops. After all, it is Black who has chosen to weaken their dark squares with ...f5 and ...e6.} 7.Bd2 7...Bxd2+ {After this trade, we'll recapture with the queen. The c3-square remains a good square for our knight. Ne4 isn't a problem because we can just move away to c2.} 8.Qxd2 8...O-O {Black could castle now. We'll castle as well.} 9.O-O 9...Ne4 {Black attacks our queen.} 10.Qc2 {But we should be perfectly fine. Nc3 and e3 are coming right up. We'll have a solid center and connected rooks. The position is about equal.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 2: The Stonewall Dutch and Others / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/912b546f-7c57-4246-b0d8-f512d8bb20f9/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can go for the Dutch, hoping we transpose back into normal Dutch territory. We'll play our knight out to f3 first.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. And we can get two pawns in the center.} 3.d4 3...e6 {This move 3...e6 is the prelude to the Stonewall or Gotham Dutch. Black can even go to e7 with their bishop or just fianchetto on the queenside. Let's see how to face these options. c2 is a great square for our queen. This helps us remove the sting from the Gotham Dutch which would have occurred after 4.Nc3 Bb4.} 4.Qc2 4...d5 {This is Stonewall territory. Black wants to have a strong light-squared chain of pawns controlling the e4-square. But the Stonewall allows us to maneuver around Black's weak dark squares. 5.Bf4 is precisely the move to begin doing that.} 5.Bf4 5...c6 {...c6 is a good follow-up to ensure they'll hold the center down. We should play 6.e3 to connect our pawns, especially since our bishop has been developed.} 6.e3 6...Be7 {Black could also develop with Be7 to simply castle and avoid trading their important dark-squared bishop. This is a bad approach because the bishop on f4 controls important real estate on e5. We'll develop our knight to c3. From here it'll help fight for e4.} 7.Nc3 7...O-O {Black castles. We increase our pressure on e4, even though we wouldn't capture the knight if it lands on e4.} 8.Bd3 8...Ne4 {Black jumps their knight onto e4. I can't stress this enough, we're not looking to capture the knight on e4. Bxe4 allows Black to recapture with a pawn and make their light square pawn chain longer. This loses the advantage for White.} 9.g4 {Instead, we have this interesting move. We are trying to weaken Black's Stonewall Setup, or alternatively open up the g-file for our rook. White has a nice space advantage. It's hard to suggest what Black should do with their light-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 2: The Stonewall Dutch and Others / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/912b546f-7c57-4246-b0d8-f512d8bb20f9/lines"]

1.c4 1...f5 {Black can go for the Dutch, hoping we transpose back into normal Dutch territory. We'll play our knight out to f3 first.} 2.Nf3 2...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to control e4. And we can get two pawns in the center.} 3.d4 3...e6 {This move 3...e6 is the prelude to the Stonewall or Gotham Dutch. Black can even go to e7 with their bishop or just fianchetto on the queenside. Let's see how to face these options. c2 is a great square for our queen. This helps us remove the sting from the Gotham Dutch which would have occurred after 4.Nc3 Bb4.} 4.Qc2 4...Bb4+ {This check is an attempt to develop quickly. It occurs 12% of the time in the database. Like I said previously, we don't mind a trade of dark-squared bishops.} 5.Bd2 5...Bxd2+ {Black trades. I know that I recommended taking with the queen in the 4...b6 line, but here we can go for a quick e4 by recapturing with the knight. In the 4...b6 line, e4 simply wasn't possible because Black controlled that square with the b7-bishop. This is not the case here.} 6.Nbxd2 6...O-O {If Black castles, as was their plan when they played 4...Bb4+ ...} 7.e4 {We can be slightly better after 7.e4 because we're challenging Black's control of the center and getting ready to open the position up favorably. We have more space and more pieces developed.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 3: Unconventional 1st Moves: 1...g6, 1...b6, 1...Nc6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/d2d646e5-960f-4ee2-80fe-6b67b9e3b48e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nc6 {Black can also play 1...Nc6, simply developing the queenside knight. This move might look offbeat at first glance, but has no independent value as Black can hardly continue without ...e5. Against ...Nc6, we'll also develop our queenside knight behind the c-pawn.} 2.Nc3 2...Nf6 {Black could also start with ...Nf6. We'll develop with Nf3.} 3.Nf3 3...e5 {Black then advances their e-pawn.} 4.e3 {And we go e3, preparing a d4 push. This position has been analyzed in the first two chapters of the course.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 3: Unconventional 1st Moves: 1...g6, 1...b6, 1...Nc6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/d2d646e5-960f-4ee2-80fe-6b67b9e3b48e/lines"]

1.c4 1...Nc6 {Black can also play 1...Nc6, simply developing the queenside knight. This move might look offbeat at first glance, but has no independent value as Black can hardly continue without ...e5. Against ...Nc6, we'll also develop our queenside knight behind the c-pawn.} 2.Nc3 2...e5 {Black typically advances ...e5, taking some central space & preventing d4.} 3.Nf3 {And we'll simply meet ...e5 with Nf3. This position has been analyzed in the second study of the third chapter.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 3: Unconventional 1st Moves: 1...g6, 1...b6, 1...Nc6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/d2d646e5-960f-4ee2-80fe-6b67b9e3b48e/lines"]

1.c4 1...b6 {Black can also play 1...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. Against ...b6, we'll also develop our queenside knight behind the c-pawn.} 2.Nc3 2...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 3.d4 3...e6 {Black goes ...e6, controlling d5 and opening up the dark-squared bishop. And I suggest we proceed with 4.a3! This is a very important move - we're preventing the annoying ...Bb4 pin and preparing to take the center with e4.} 4.a3 4...f5 {Most often, Black advances ...f5. Against this, I suggest we push d5! I believe this approach is practically very challenging for Black since the d5-pawn kills the fianchettoed bishop.} 5.d5 5...Nf6 {Black typically continues with ...Nf6. We will then proceed with g3. Our plan is simple - fianchetto the bishop, go Nf3, and castle short!} 6.g3 6...Bd6 {Black develops the f8-bishop to d6. ...Be7 is also possible, but ...Bd6 is more active. The convenient thing is that our plan is the same either way. We develop our kingside knight. It's very slightly better to start with Nf3 in order to prevent any ...Be5 ideas.} 7.Nf3 7...O-O {Black continues by castling We'll fianchetto our bishop, as planned.} 8.Bg2 8...a5 {Black advances their a-pawn, taking queenside space and preventing b4. We'll simply get our king to safety.} 9.O-O 9...Na6 {Black goes ...Na6, developing their other knight and looking for ...Nc5. We can then go Nb5, making use of the weakened b5-square and targeting Black's dark-squared bishop on d6.} 10.Nb5 10...Nc5 {Black goes ...Nc5, as planned.} 11.b3 {And we can go b3, preventing Black from pushing ...a4, supporting c4 & preparing to develop the c1-bishop. White definitely enjoys an edge here thanks to the space advantage. We'll continue with Bb2 & should look into capturing on d6 at the right moment.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 3: Unconventional 1st Moves: 1...g6, 1...b6, 1...Nc6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/d2d646e5-960f-4ee2-80fe-6b67b9e3b48e/lines"]

1.c4 1...b6 {Black can also play 1...b6, preparing to fianchetto the light-squared bishop. Against ...b6, we'll also develop our queenside knight behind the c-pawn.} 2.Nc3 2...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll expand in the center with d4.} 3.d4 3...e6 {Black goes ...e6, controlling d5 and opening up the dark-squared bishop. And I suggest we proceed with 4.a3! This is a very important move - we're preventing the annoying ...Bb4 pin and preparing to take the center with e4.} 4.a3 4...Nf6 {Black's other option is to control the e4-square with ...Nf6 instead of ...f5. Against this, I also suggest d5, with the idea to shut down Black's b7-bishop.} 5.d5 5...Bd6 {Black develops the f8-bishop to d6. We should then develop our kingside knight, preventing any ...Be5 ideas.} 6.Nf3 6...O-O {Black castles their king.} 7.g3 {And we can simply proceed with g3. Once again, White is slightly better thanks to the space advantage. Our plan is to continue with Bg2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 12: The Dutch & Other 1st Moves / Study 3: Unconventional 1st Moves: 1...g6, 1...b6, 1...Nc6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/62a39420-2f65-4e78-ab12-988e98629973/chapters/f6c7823f-b072-4041-8495-458298030172/studies/d2d646e5-960f-4ee2-80fe-6b67b9e3b48e/lines"]

1.c4 1...g6 {Black can play 1...g6, preparing to fianchetto. Often this will transpose into a mainstream variation like the King's Indian or Grunfeld, but this move order holds some independent value. Against ...g6, we'll simply develop our queenside knight. It might be tempting to quickly take the center, but our plan here is to develop with g3 and Bg2.} 2.Nc3 2...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll proceed with 3.g3, looking to fianchetto our light-squared bishop.} 3.g3 3...e5 {Black advances their e-pawn, taking control of the center and preventing d4. We'll fianchetto the bishop, as planned.} 4.Bg2 4...d6 {Black goes ...d6, supporting e5 and opening up the light-squared bishop.} 5.b4 {And we advance our b-pawn, expanding on the queenside. Black can continue developing in a handful of ways here, but our plan stays consistent: we'll go Rb1 to support the b-pawn and get off of the diagonal, play d3 to support the c4-pawn and e4-square, and complete our development with Nf3 and O-O.} *